\subsection{Ablation Study}\label{sec:ablation}
\paragraph{Training in Sequence}
For training Step 3, we can either employ simulated $\Vec v_\text{sim}$ or predicted velocities $\Vec v_\text{pred}$ as inputs.
As illustrated in~\Cref{fig:dummyK_pki_T}, training on $\Vec v_\text{pred}$ leads to physically implausible temperature fields with noisy artifacts and fragmented plumes. This likely stems from local misalignments between the inputs $\Vec s$ and $\Vec v_\text{pred}$, and the corresponding label $T$, which strongly complicates localized training.
Although loss values in~\Cref{tab:modelT_metrics} appear similar, the resulting temperature fields are less physically consistent and resemble those produced by end-to-end inference of UNet$_\text{\textit{3dp}}$ in \Cref{sec:general_approaches}.

\paragraph{Zero-Padding}
Removing zero-padding from all convolutional layers yields smaller but cleaner output fields, as shown in~\Cref{fig:results_T}. As a result, we obtain improved performance, reflected in lower PAT and higher SSIM scores in~\Cref{tab:modelT_metrics}.

\paragraph{Partitioning Training Data}
Hyperparameter search shows the best performance for partitioning the domain during training into 20\,736 overlapping patches of 256$\times$256 cells for Step 1 and 82\,944 patches for Step 3. Although this increases per-epoch compute time compared to full-domain training, it significantly reduces the number of epochs needed to reach comparable test performance (cf.~\Cref{tab:modelV_metrics,tab:modelT_metrics}),
resulting in an overall training time reduction of 67--90\% (cf.~\Cref{tab:times}).

\paragraph{Replacing Isolated Steps with UNet$_\textit{101dp}$ and $2\times 2$-DDUNet$_\textit{101dp}$}
We individually replace each of the three steps with an optimized (cf.~\Cref{app:hyperparam_optimization}) vanilla approach, i.e., UNet or DDUNet trained on \textit{101dp}.
For Step~2, both fail completely; results for Steps~1 and~3 are summarized in~\Cref{tab:modelV_metrics,tab:modelT_metrics}. In Step~1, UNet slightly outperforms DDUNet, while the reverse holds for Step~3. A visual comparison of the scaling datapoint in~\Cref{fig:scalability} reveals that increasing training data from \textit{3dp} to \textit{101dp} has minimal impact on the performance in Step~3.

\paragraph{Modifying Streamline Inputs of Step~3}
We evaluate the influence of Step 3's inputs, focusing on the effect of modifying or omitting individual components of the streamlines $\Vec s = \left (s, s_o \right )$. When both the central streamlines $s$ and offset streamlines $s_o$ are excluded, predicted plumes fail to follow flow paths beyond the CNN's receptive field. Omitting only $s_o$ results in overly narrow plumes, whereas excluding only $s$ produces overly smeared temperature fields.
Including both but removing the fading of values along the streamlines leads to diffuse background temperatures and less localized plumes. Overall, the Huber validation loss increases by 32–132\%, depending on the experiment.
For visual, quantitative, and extended results, we refer to~\Cref{app:exp_inputs}.

\section{Domain Transfer to Real Permeability Fields}\label{sec:transfer}
