\section{Empirical Application}\label{sec:application}

This section apply the projection test to the data from \cite{bb}. This data consists of 21,740 two-sided t-statistics drawn from 684 articles. These articles were the universe of articles published in 25 top economics journals between 2015 and 2018 that used one of four causal methods. Each $t$-score corresponds to a two-sided $t$-test of a main hypothesis of interest. The coefficients in the numerators of the $t$-scores were estimated using Randomized Controlled Trials (RCT), Difference in Differences (DID), Regression Discontinuity Designs (RDD), or Instrumental Variables (IV). All  ``regression controls, constant terms, balance and robustness checks, heterogeneity of effects, and
placebo tests" were excluded. Some tests were reported as $p$-values and \cite{bb} converted these to  $t$-scores using the standard normal distribution. 

We run our tests on each of the four causal inference methods separately. The results are in Table \ref{tab:brodeur_results}. We report results for $J=30$ and $J=20$ to demonstrate that our conclusions are not very sensitive to the choice of smoothing parameter $J$. The $p$-values in columns 4 and 7 mostly confirm the findings from \cite{kudrinjmp24}. We detect selective reporting for RCTs, IVs, and DIDs under the assumption that $T|h\sim N(h,1)$. 

These results also apply a novel caveat to existing knowledge. In Columns 6 and 9, we report the maximum size distortion (“breakdown size”), which never exceeds 0.53\%. In practical terms, even if we attribute the rejection of $\mathbf{H}_0$ solely to the fact that $T\mid h$ is only approximately normal, this approximation error would inflate the researcher’s $t$-test size by no more than 0.53\%. It is  plausible that many researchers believe they are conducting a 5\% test when their actual Type I error rate is closer to 5.5\%. Hence, the degree of non-normality needed to challenge these $p$-hacking detections is minimal.  We conclude that these $p$-hacking detections could be challenged on the grounds that the $t$-curve is only approximately a Gaussian convolution.

\begin{remark}\normalfont
    If we were to impose smoothness assumptions on the distribution of true effects, we could sharpen the bound in (\ref{eq:loose_upper}). This would increase the BSD's and possibly reverse the conclusion that the detections of $p$-hacking are fragile.
\end{remark}

