\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:1}
\begin{quote}
\textit{“The single biggest mistake writers make when creating characters is that they think of the hero and all other characters as separate individuals. [...] To create great characters, think of all your characters as part of a web in which each helps define the others”} \cite{truby2008anatomy}.
\end{quote}

Characters are essential building blocks of fictional stories. While the heart of the story may rest in one central character, its pulse comes to life through many. A well-constructed ensemble not only propels the plot \cite{myers2022protagonist} but also creates emotional depth and thematic resonance by allowing characters to challenge, support, and transform one another \cite{mckee2021character, truby2008anatomy, egri1972art, myers2022protagonist}. Thus, crafting an interwoven cast of characters is an important activity for all forms of storytelling—novels, plays, comics, films, and drama—but is especially crucial for full-length stories where the extended scope provides greater room to develop the lives of its characters \cite{mckee2021character}. While this process is expressed through terms such as character orchestration \cite{egri1972art}, interconnected web \cite{truby2008anatomy}, and cast formation \cite{mckee2021character}, we refer to it as \textit{interconnected character creation} in this research.

Acknowledging the importance of character creation, Human-Computer Interaction (HCI) researchers have developed computational tools to aid creative writers. One line of work has implemented chatbots embodying writer-defined personas, enabling users to iteratively shape and interact with their characters through free-form conversation \cite{10.1145/3450741.3465253, 10.1145/3613904.3642105, 10.1145/3706598.3713146}. While these works support some relational context (e.g., roleplaying), they primarily focus on single-character development. In contrast, we aim to support character cast creation, and to do so, we see an opportunity for leveraging Large Language Model-based Multi-Agents (LLM-MA). 

LLM-MA are distinguished by their focus on a variety of agent profiles, interactions between agents, and their collective decision-making processes \cite{10.24963/ijcai.2024/890}. Prior HCI research has leveraged LLM-MA to provide users with opinions from diverse viewpoints, finding applications in domains that require a multitude of expert suggestions \cite{park2023choicemates}, audiences \cite{10.1145/3586182.3625114, 10.1145/3706598.3714034}, or simulated users within social computing systems \cite{10.1145/3526113.3545616, 10.1145/3613904.3642545}. Although these works are not directly related to the storytelling context, they collectively underscore the potential of LLM-MA when used for interconnected character creation. As noted by storywriting literature, \textit{“the most important step in creating your hero, as well as all other characters, is to connect and compare each to the others”} \cite{truby2008anatomy}. Thus, by enabling the simultaneous exploration of contrasting character perspectives, LLM-MA can empower writers to construct a complex, multilayered cast of characters. 

To design an LLM-MA tool that supports storywriters’ interconnected character creation, we first sought to understand the challenges they face during this process and ground our system in the strategies they find effective for overcoming these difficulties. To this end, we conducted a formative study with 14 writers experienced in creating full-length fictional stories. We found that writers address key challenges through the following strategies: 1) To conceptualize new characters capable of influencing existing ones, they brainstorm a wide range of related characters. 2) To balance similarities and differences between characters, they imagine each character’s inner world and how each would respond uniquely to the same situation. 3) To flesh out character relationships, they often create moments of interaction regardless of the main plot to gain insight into their dynamics.

With these observations, we derived three design goals for an LLM-MA tool to support interconnected character creation: 1) Suggest diverse characters related to existing ones, 2) support the comparison and investigation of in-depth character identities, and 3) manifest character relationships through exploratory interactions. To translate these goals into concrete system features, we drew inspiration from interaction concepts found in social media (e.g., 
\cite{facebook2025, threads2025, reddit2025, cyworld2025}), as they provide effective means for designing social interactions among story characters that align with each design goal. Specifically, these platforms enable users to connect with individuals beyond their usual acquaintances through potential connection points, freely express differing or similar thoughts on topics of mutual interest, and display or reinforce relationships by reacting to others’ content. We anticipated that drawing on these affordances to simulate character interactions would intuitively facilitate our design goals by echoing everyday social behavior.

Thus, we propose Constella\footnote{Constella is short for constellation, underscoring that characters, like stars, gain new meaning as they connect, relate, and are viewed together.}, an LLM-MA tool that supports storywriters’ interconnected character creation through social media-inspired interactions (\autoref{fig:teaser}). Constella enables writers to iteratively build characters and their relationships via dedicated character profiles. Upon creating a profile, the FRIENDS DISCOVERY feature helps writers uncover new characters linked to existing ones by generating their mini profiles. Writers can also use the JOURNALS feature to contrast the inner mindscapes of characters by generating their diary entries. Lastly, writers can elaborate upon character relationships as they respond to each other’s journal entries through the COMMENTS feature. Together, these features offer resources for writers to flexibly expand, deeply inspect, and tangibly concretize their character casts throughout their development process.

To explore how our tool integrates into storywriters’ practices, we conducted a 7–8 day deployment study with 11 writers. During the study, participants used the tool through tasks that included creating multiple characters, a backstory, scenes, and a story outline, all within the context of writing a full-length fictional story. The results show that Constella supported diverse approaches to interconnected character creation. It facilitated the construction of multiple worlds populated by distinct yet interlinked characters, supported the analysis of character differences and similarities that inspired new scenes, and enabled deeper exploration of character relationships by surfacing emotional tensions. Notably, our tool encouraged writers to focus on a broader range of characters, including those that might otherwise have remained underdeveloped. Constella was also perceived to play a complementary role in the co-creative process without compromising their sense of authorial control. We conclude by discussing the potential of LLM-MA to help writers distribute creative attention across the entire character cast and how AI-infused writing tools can be designed to support authorial agency. 

This paper presents the following contributions: 



