\section{Background and Related Work}
\label{sec:2}
This work aims to support storywriters’ interconnected character creation process through LLM-MA. In this section, we first outline the theoretical foundations of interconnected character creation in storywriting (§\ref{sec:2.1}). We then review prior AI tools designed to assist character creation (§\ref{sec:2.2}). Finally, we position our work within the broader landscape of AI-assisted writing tools that move beyond ready-made text generation to support creative processes (§\ref{sec:2.3}).

\subsection{Interconnected Character Creation}
\label{sec:2.1}

Narratives consist of actions, happenings, characters, settings, and discourse \cite{chatman1980story}. Our research focuses on the element of characters, which can be defined through archetypes\footnote{An archetype means \textit{“(from Greek archetypos, “original pattern”), in literary criticism, a primordial image, character, or pattern of circumstances that recurs throughout literature and thought consistently enough to be considered a universal concept or situation”} \cite{britannicaArchetype}.} \cite{lee2013psychology, mckee2021character, truby2008anatomy, myers2022protagonist, schmidt201245}, backstories \cite{lee2013psychology, mckee2010story, mckee2021character, truby2008anatomy, card2010elements, seger1990creating, corbett2013art, myers2022protagonist}, and various physical, psychological, and sociological attributes \cite{chatman1980story, mckee2010story, card2010elements, seger1990creating, egri1972art, field2005screenplay, barker2019build, edelstein2006writer}.

As full-length fictional stories typically feature an ensemble of characters, writing guides offer suggestions for developing characters in relation to one another \cite{mckee2021character, truby2008anatomy, egri1972art, myers2022protagonist, eastman2021shakespeare}. For instance, McKee \cite{mckee2010story} uses the metaphor of a solar system, suggesting that writers imagine the main character as a star and other characters as orbiting planets, each positioned based on their influence on the protagonist and one another. He advises writers to begin by creating the central role, followed by the incremental development of supporting roles. In contrast, Truby \cite{truby2008anatomy} stresses the importance of developing characters simultaneously, encouraging writers to draw continuous comparisons and connections among them. He argues that this process highlights each character’s individuality, allowing even supporting characters to become more distinct and meaningful alongside the protagonist.

While literature on storywriting underscores the value of creating characters in an interconnected manner, it tends to focus on general principles rather than providing comprehensive explanations of the real-world challenges writers face during interconnected character creation and the underlying causes of these difficulties. Furthermore, the degree to which these theoretical approaches reflect actual writing practices remains uncertain. As prior HCI research has highlighted the diversity of writers’ practices and the variability of their needs \cite{ippolito2022creative, guo2024pen, 10.1145/3544548.3580782}, we anticipated discrepancies between the formalized processes outlined in theory and the actual practices of writers in the wild. Thus, to design a tool that effectively supports storywriters, we determined it was essential to draw not only on theoretical insights but also to engage deeply with empirical evidence. Based on this background, we grounded the development of our tool through in-depth interviews with storywriters while using the previously cited writing guides as references for developing the interview questions. 

\subsection{AI Support for Character Construction}
\label{sec:2.2}
In this section, we review LLM-based tools designed for storywriters, focusing on systems that support the creation of new characters or facilitate character interactions through agents that embody writer-defined personas. 

Several works utilized Large Language Models (LLMs) to generate new characters \cite{10.1145/3544548.3581225, 10.1145/3654777.3676352, kreminski2024intent, swanson-etal-2021-story, sudowrite2025, chen-etal-2024-hollmwood, huot2024agents}, often drawing from other elements of the story to align with the writers’ overall vision and storytelling goals. For example, \textit{Dramatron} \cite{10.1145/3544548.3581225} allows users to first define a logline\footnote{A logline is \textit{“a single sentence describing your entire story”} \cite{russell2000screen}, such as what the protagonist wants or desires and what is at stake \cite{bishop2016sell}.}, which is then used to generate not only the characters but also the title and plot. \textit{Patchview} \cite{10.1145/3654777.3676352} enables writers to create concepts, such as good and evil, and choose positions along these spectra, guiding the system to create appropriate worldbuilding elements, including characters. However, existing works do not explicitly focus on user-specified relational ties between characters as a primary axis for character generation, despite the central role they play in character development and plot progression \cite{varotsi2019conceptualisation, truby2008anatomy, myers2022protagonist, eastman2021shakespeare, harvey1970character}.

Another line of work has aimed to enable storywriter-character interactions through chatbots designed to reflect writer-defined attributes \cite{10.1145/3450741.3465253, 10.1145/3613904.3642105, banterbot2019, 10.1145/3532106.3533506, stark2023can}. Most relatedly, \textit{CharacterChat} \cite{10.1145/3450741.3465253} first introduced the concept of \textit{“progressive manifestation,”} where the writers gradually shape a chatbot into their character by adding new attributes through back-and-forth conversation. \textit{CharacterMeet} \cite{10.1145/3613904.3642105} extends this research by utilizing an LLM and implementing a conversation context, enabling writers to roleplay as other characters. However, with the exception of those created for children \cite{10.1145/3706599.3719932}, implementations of agent-based character embodiments for storywriters \cite{10.1145/3450741.3465253, 10.1145/3613904.3642105, banterbot2019} have mostly concentrated on single-character development. 

While we focus on providing support for fictional storywriters, prior research in the domain of interactive storytelling has proposed tools \cite{10.1145/3649921.3656987, sun2025drama, 10.1145/3706598.3713363, han-etal-2024-ibsen} that utilize multiple character embodiments via LLM-MA. As the emergent nature of LLM-MA poses challenges for authors to steer the direction for interactive stories (e.g., games), these works focus on providing control mechanisms that mediate between the author’s intent, the unpredictability of LLM-based agents, and player-introduced actions. For example, \textit{Storyverse} \cite{10.1145/3649921.3656987} translates high-level plot outlines into characters’ action sequences during playtime, while the game player can also steer the unfolding events, such as by killing a character. 

Beyond interactive storytelling, several works \cite{yu-etal-2025-multi, chen-etal-2024-hollmwood, huot2024agents} adapt LLM-MA for fictional storywriting, but their emphasis is on proposing automated story generation pipelines, thereby providing limited support for writers’ iterative and incremental character construction processes \cite{kreminski-martens-2022-unmet}. For instance, Yu et al.’s work \cite{yu-etal-2025-multi} assumes that detailed character personas and the plot outline are readily available and proceeds to generate inter-character simulations that produce the detailed text for the story. Other systems \cite{chen-etal-2024-hollmwood, huot2024agents} limit the writer’s contribution to supplying a seed storyline; from that point onward, agents automatically detail the plot and characters, multi-agent exchanges generate the dialogue, and a final agent combines them together into the complete narrative. 

While these approaches demonstrate the potential of LLM-MA in storywriting, they primarily utilize LLM-MA simulations as either real-time, emergent narratives or as components within largely automated pipelines for complete story generation. In contrast, we focus on leveraging LLM-MA to support fictional storywriters in constructing their character casts, specifically through the design of scaffolding materials (e.g., journal entries and comments) rather than final story text. This design choice reflects our deliberate intent to uphold authorial agency by preserving the writers’ role in the expressive act of the writing process. In line with this focus, we investigate how our tool supports writers through a deployment study, which, to our knowledge, is the first research to offer empirical insights into how a character-based LLM-MA tool integrates into the real-world practices of fictional storywriters.

\subsection{Assisting Writers Beyond Generating Ready-Made Text}
\label{sec:2.3}
Recent advancements in language models have spurred the development of various tools aimed at assisting writers. One predominant trend in this space involves co-writing systems that offer suggestions in the form of output-ready text, such as next sentence generation \cite{swanson-etal-2021-story, 10.1145/3491102.3502030, 10.1145/3490099.3511105, 10.1145/3172944.3172983, yang2022ai, 10.1145/3180308.3180329, calderwood2020novelists, 10.1145/3462204.3481771}, text variation \cite{swanson-etal-2021-story, 10.1145/3490099.3511105, 10.1145/2757226.2757229, 10.1145/3613904.3641899}, and narrative generation \cite{10.1145/3544548.3581225, 10.1145/3649921.3656987, 10.1007/978-3-031-76806-4_7, sun2025drama, 10.1145/3706598.3713363, 10.1145/3411763.3450391, yu-etal-2025-multi, chen-etal-2024-hollmwood, huot2024agents, han-etal-2024-ibsen}. However, recent studies investigating writers’ perceptions of AI systems have highlighted the importance of the \textit{“emotional values of writing”} \cite{10.1145/3532106.3533506}, values that may be diminished when AI systems reduce opportunities for writers to \textit{“express their ideas on their own”} \cite{10.1145/3532106.3533506}.

In response, a growing body of research \cite{stark2023can, 10.1145/3635636.3656187, arnold2021generative, 10.1145/3526113.3545672, kim2024towards} has been actively proposing alternative ways for generative models to support writers without direct text generation; that is, text that can be immediately inserted in their work without further modification. For example, AI tools that provide experience advising \cite{stark2023can}, questions \cite{arnold2021generative}, or continuous summaries to support process-oriented reflection \cite{10.1145/3526113.3545672} have been introduced. Collectively, these approaches are promising ways to ensure that writers retain a sense of ownership over their work. 

We situate our tool in this body of work by proposing a Creativity Support Tool (CST) \cite{10.1145/1323688.1323689, 10.1145/3290605.3300619, 10.1145/3461778.3462050} that offers open-ended resources for storywriters. Specifically, we provide resources in social media-inspired formats such as mini profiles, diaries, and comments, which likely encourage writers to iterate over, adapt, and interpret the generated content rather than directly copying and inserting it as-is. In doing so, we aim to create room for the author’s expressive intent to flow through the interpretation of underdetermined outcomes \cite{10.1145/3690712.3690725}. 

