We recruited writers with prior experience in creating full-length fictional stories. Considering the domain of storytelling, we aimed to gather insights across diverse story types and genres. Furthermore, we aimed to diversify participants based on their total duration of creative writing experience to gain a comprehensive understanding from individuals relatively newer to storywriting to those with greater familiarity. Lastly, we recruited participants with and without formal creative writing education, as well as those with and without publication experience. This inclusive approach was informed by Gero et al.’s work \cite{10.1145/3544548.3580782}, which argues that formal education does not necessarily equate to expertise and that relying on publication experience as a metric for expertise could unnecessarily marginalize writers.

Participants were recruited through online writer communities, university communities, personal contacts, and social media posts uploaded by researchers and their acquaintances. All participants were selected through purposeful sampling \cite{palinkas2015purposeful} to ensure maximum variation across story types, genres, and total duration of creative writing experience.

In total, 14 participants (F=8, M=6) were recruited (\autoref{table:formative-demo}). Story types ranged from film (N=11), drama (N=10), novel (N=10), plays (N=3), comics (N=2), and animation (N=1). The total number of writing genres they had experience with reached 32, including examples such as comedy, romance, and historical fiction. The duration of their creative writing experience ranged from 2 to 26 years, with an average of 11.93 years. All participants resided in South Korea. 

\subsection{Procedure}
\label{sec:3.2}
\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figure/fig-formative-process.png}
    \caption{Procedure of the formative study.}
    \label{fig:formative-process}
\end{figure}

The formative study was divided into three parts: (1) Q\&A of overall creative experience, (2) memory-recall workshop, and (3) Q\&A of selected creative experience (\autoref{fig:formative-process}). The study with each participant was conducted either in person (N=5) or remotely via Zoom (N=9), following the participants’ preferences. 

\textbf{(1) Q\&A of overall creative experience (40 minutes)}: After obtaining informed consent, we introduced the goal of the study. To gain overarching insights into their interconnected character creation process, we asked participants to answer the questions (\autoref{table:formative-interview} in Appendix \ref{sec:Appendix-questions}) based on all their full-length stories. The questions inquired about their experiences in developing characters with differing roles, comparing characters, and building character relationships. 

\textbf{(2) Memory-recall workshop (20 minutes)}: To help participants recall their work in preparation for the next session, we offered worksheets that served as elicitation materials. Participants selected one of their previously created full-length stories and completed three types of worksheets: the Character List, Character Cards, and Character Relationship Map (\autoref{fig:formative-materials}). For online participants, the worksheets were shared via FigJam \cite{figjam2025}, while offline participants received printed copies. Further details of the workshop materials are described in §\ref{sec:3.3}.

\textbf{(3) Q\&A of selected creative experience (30 minutes)}: In this session, we aimed to gain a deeper understanding of their interconnected character creation process through concrete, situated examples. Thus, participants answered the same set of questions (\autoref{table:formative-interview} in Appendix \ref{sec:Appendix-questions}), but now contextualized to the specific story they had selected in the second session. All participants received compensation of 30,000 KRW (approx. 21 USD) for their participation.

\subsection{Materials for Memory-Recall Workshop}
\label{sec:3.3}
\begin{figure} [H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figure/fig-formative-materials.png}
    \caption{Materials for the memory-recall workshop. (A) Character List. (B) Character Cards. (C) Character Relationship Map.}
    \label{fig:formative-materials}
\end{figure}

The worksheets for the memory-recall workshop were designed to help participants reflect on the characters from one of their previously created full-length stories.  The \textbf{Character List} helped participants recall both main and supporting characters by listing their names. The \textbf{Character Cards} prompted focused reflection on 3–4 main characters by writing their descriptions, an approach inspired by character sheets used by storywriters \cite{10.1145/3706598.3713161}. The \textbf{Character Relationship Map} guided participants in outlining relationships and interactions among the main characters, inspired by character maps discussed in storywriting literature \cite{myers2022protagonist}. Acknowledging that these materials may not align with their usual practices, participants were encouraged to recall their work and creation process using alternative methods if preferred.


\subsection{Analysis}
\label{sec:3.4}
We transcribed over 1,260 minutes of audio recordings from interviews with 14 participants. The worksheets completed during the memory-recall workshop were used solely as reference materials to clarify participants’ responses during the transcription process.

The interview data were qualitatively analyzed through three cycles of coding. In the first cycle, the first author used structural coding \cite{saldana2021coding} to categorize sections of the data in relation to our study’s goal (approaches, challenges, strategies), resulting in our initial codes. Based on these initial codes, the first author developed sub-codes through process coding \cite{miles2013qualitative} to identify patterns in participants’ conceptual and observable actions. These sub-codes were then iteratively refined through discussions among the researchers. Finally, the first author used pattern coding \cite{saldana2021coding} to cluster the sub-codes into three overarching categories that explain participants’ interconnected character creation practices. This last analysis process was also reviewed by the other researchers. The entire analysis process was conducted over two months.

\subsection{Findings}
\label{sec:3.5}
We report participants’ approaches to interconnected character creation, the challenges they face during this process, and the strategies they use to address these difficulties. These insights are organized into three overarching categories that encapsulate their practices.
    \subsubsection{Branching Out Related Characters}
    \label{sec:3.5.1}
    \paragraph*{\textbf{Approach}} Writers would often start creating their main characters, then branch out new characters in relation to these existing characters (P1, P3, P4, P5, P6, P7, P8, P9, P10, P11). One specific method involved splitting the traits of the main character and distributing them among other characters (P7, P9). P9 mentioned that this method would ensure that these characters would form a cohesive whole, as each character would reflect different aspects of the main character’s personality. Another method was to think of new characters that would complement existing characters or have opposing characteristics to create conflict (P1, P5, P6, P7, P9, P10). One last notable method was to imagine specific situations where the main characters would be placed and envision other characters that would naturally exist in the same environment (P1, P7, P9). 

    \paragraph*{\textbf{Challenges}} However, thinking of new characters, especially those who would influence the protagonist in specific ways, was often challenging for some writers (P4, P7). The guidance offered to the main character and the inner conflicts they experience depend on the supporting characters. Therefore, participants placed great importance on finding characters that could appropriately fulfill these roles. 

    \begin{quote}
        \textit{“The character needs to act in a certain way, but who could be the essential character to influence that character towards this direction? A friend, perhaps? What kind of personality and values does this friend have, and how would they influence the protagonist? It’s not just about what they say; it’s also about the kind of relationship they’ve had until now. But who could that be?”} (P4)
    \end{quote}
    P7 also shared her experience, mentioning that the process of adjusting supporting characters so they fit these roles seems \textit{“roughly correct at first, but roughly correct is not enough. One must continuously refine these characters and repeat this process.”}

    \paragraph*{\textbf{Strategies}} In response to this challenge, P4 mentioned that he would collaborate with a fellow writer to brainstorm a wide range of characters. P7 added that she would sometimes even change the settings of existing characters when things were not working out.

    % \vspace{1em}
    % \noindent
    % \begin{tcolorbox}
    % \textbf{Takeaway:} writers often expand their character casts in relation to the main characters. However, coming up with new characters that could appropriately influence these core characters was challenging. This process led to the ideation of a broad spectrum of characters as well as the iterative modification of their characters to create the perfect match. Thus, the tool should offer a wide selection of related characters with key descriptions, allowing writers to quickly explore options and iteratively adapt characters when needed. 
    % \end{tcolorbox}

    In summary, writers often expand their character casts in relation to the main characters. However, coming up with new characters that could appropriately influence these core characters was often challenging. This process led to ideating a broad spectrum of potential characters and iteratively refining them to create the perfect match. Thus, the tool should offer a wide selection of related characters with key descriptions, allowing writers to quickly explore options and iteratively adapt characters when needed. 

    \subsubsection{Balancing Similarities and Differences}
    \label{sec:3.5.2}
    \paragraph*{\textbf{Approach}} Many participants explained that they would often construct characters by comparing their identities (P1, P2, P3, P5, P7, P8, P10, P13). This practice serves two main purposes. First, it helps ensure that each character has distinct roles and personalities. For example, P1 would start by thinking of ways their characters would contrast visually and progressively alter several attributes of each character, such as their gender, when the participant thought their identities overlapped. Second, this approach enables participants to create characters who not only contrast but also share values and beliefs. P10 emphasized the importance of this practice for creating complex characters who both attract and clash with each other.

    \paragraph*{\textbf{Challenges}} Despite these initial approaches, many writers found it challenging to create distinct characters, leading to overlapping character identities (P1, P2, P5, P7, P8, P10, P11, P13). For instance, P2 worried that her characters would reflect her own values and thoughts too much, further explaining that individuals created from a single source would inevitably result in similar personalities. In addition, writers explained that balancing character identities that would contrast and align in different aspects was also challenging (P7, P8, P13). 

    \begin{quote}
    \textit{“What feels more compelling to me is exploring the sparks that fly when two equally powerful beings, each acting based on their beliefs or convictions, come into conflict. So, it's not just about aligning their social status but also about balancing the scale of their beliefs and actions, which is what I find challenging.”} (P8) 
    \end{quote}

    \paragraph*{\textbf{Strategies}} To create distinct character identities, writers aimed to capture essential traits that could clearly define their characters through expert interviews, reading other stories, and observing social media, among others. In addition, writers (P1, P2, P3, P5, P7, P8, P9, P10) expressed the importance of becoming immersed in the character’s inner world and deeply empathizing with them, such as by writing character backstories in the first person. To create characters that hold contrasting and similar identities, writers (P1, P2, P3, P4, P7, P8, P9) would place characters in identical situations to further establish their personalities. Some writers used predefined questionnaires and answered these questions for each character in the initial stage of characterization. Others would create such situations as scenes within their stories to continuously refine and ideate details for their characters. 

    % \vspace{1em}
    % \noindent
    % \begin{tcolorbox}
    % \textbf{Takeaway:} Writers often compared character identities to create both distinguishable and similar characters. Yet, balancing character similarities and differences remained a persistent challenge. Strategies to tackle this issue included examining how several characters would respond to a common situation, as well as deeply empathizing with and understanding characters’ inner worlds. Therefore, the tool should also allow writers to explore how each character interprets identical contexts in ways that reveal their internal thought processes and subjective perspectives. 
    % \end{tcolorbox}

    In short, writers often compared character identities to create both distinguishable and similar characters. Yet, balancing character similarities and differences remained a persistent challenge. Strategies to tackle this issue included examining how several characters would respond to a common situation, as well as deeply empathizing with and understanding characters’ inner worlds. Therefore, the tool should also allow writers to explore how each character interprets identical contexts in ways that reveal their internal thought processes and subjective perspectives. 
 \clearpage
    \subsubsection{Fleshing Out Relationships}
    \label{sec:3.5.3}
    \paragraph*{\textbf{Approach}} Several writers mentioned using character relationship maps in the form of diagrams to organize and explore new connections between characters (P1, P5, P9, P13, P14). For example, P5 explained that she uses these diagrams to get a broader view of character relationships and often eliminates characters who remain isolated and unconnected to others. Similarly, P13 and P14 noted that these diagrams help discover new relationships between characters that they had not previously considered. 

    \paragraph*{\textbf{Challenges}} Despite these approaches, intricately fleshing out character relationships remained a challenge for several writers (P5, P7, P9, P11, P13). For example, P9 explains: 

    \begin{quote}
        \textit{“Characters A and B may be defined as close friends, but within that, there might be nuances, like one of them not attending the other's wedding. Even though they might all be grouped together as close friends from the outside, the deeper you delve, the more you uncover different circumstances and histories that make the relationships more complex.”} (P9)
    \end{quote}

    \paragraph*{\textbf{Strategies}} To tackle these challenges, one major strategy used by writers (P9, P13, P14) was to ideate a wide range of character interactions to deepen their understanding of these relationships, even if they did not make it into the final narrative. For example, P13 referred to this technique as \textit{“hypothetical situations”} and employed it to determine whether the main characters would get along before putting her story into action. In addition, P9 mentioned that the emotions and interactions between characters would slowly accumulate through these activities and naturally evolve into specific dialogues or episodes within the story. 

    % \vspace{1em}
    % \noindent
    % \begin{tcolorbox}
    % \textbf{Takeaway:} Character relationship maps were utilized to identify and organize connections between characters. However, writers struggled to thoroughly build upon these relationships. Therefore, participants imagined how characters would interact with one another in various situations, regardless of the story’s overall flow. This implies that the tool should also aim to reveal character relationships through open-ended reactions towards each other. 
    % \end{tcolorbox}


    To summarize, character relationship maps were utilized to identify and organize connections between characters. However, writers struggled to thoroughly build upon these relationships. Therefore, participants imagined how characters would interact with one another in various situations, regardless of the story’s overall flow. This implies that the tool should also aim to reveal character relationships through open-ended reactions towards each other. 

\subsection{Design Goals}
\label{sec:3.6}
Based on these findings, we identified three design goals (DG) to build a tool to support storywriters’ interconnected character creation. 

\begin{enumerate}[label=DG~\arabic*.,  % prints “DG 1.”, “DG 2.”, …
                 labelsep=0.6em,       % space after the label
                 itemsep=0.8\baselineskip] % vertical gap between items
\item \textbf{Suggest diverse characters related to existing ones.}
      To support writers in branching out related characters, the tool should focus on broadly recommending a wide range of related characters with core traits, rather than extensive information about a single character (§\ref{sec:3.5.1}). This goal focuses on the breadth of information.  


\item \textbf{Support the comparison and investigation of in-depth character identities.}
      To assist writers in balancing the similarities and differences between characters, the tool should illustrate how each character interprets the same context in ways that reveal their inner mindscapes (§\ref{sec:3.5.2}). This goal emphasizes the depth of information.  

\item \textbf{Manifest character relationships through exploratory interactions.}
 To help writers flesh out character relationships, the tool should show open-ended reactions between characters as testbeds to build and refine their dynamics (§\ref{sec:3.5.3}). This goal emphasizes speculative information rather than narrative progression. 

\end{enumerate}
Through these three design goals, we aim to provide a plethora of resources that storywriters can mold into concrete forms, use to understand their work deeply, or draw inspiration from while leaving the generated results as-is. 

