\section{Constella}
\label{sec:4}
With the derived design goals, we developed Constella, a creativity support tool designed to support storywriters’ interconnected character creation process through LLM-MA. Its core features include FRIENDS DISCOVERY, JOURNALS, and COMMENTS. FRIENDS DISCOVERY generates three characters in relation to a given character, aiding writers to expand their ensemble with diverse additions (\textbf{DG 1}). JOURNALS produces diary entries from multiple characters according to a shared theme, revealing their internal emotions, thoughts, and behaviors (\textbf{DG 2}). COMMENTS enables characters to respond to other journal entries, surfacing additional layers of character relationships (\textbf{DG 3}). Here, we first discuss our source of inspiration when translating the design goals into system features (§\ref{sec:4.1}). We then describe the system’s components (§\ref{sec:4.2}), present an example use scenario (§\ref{sec:4.3}), and detail the implementation and prompt design (§\ref{sec:4.4}).

\subsection{Metaphor and Interaction Concept}
\label{sec:4.1}
In this section, we explain the rationale behind adopting social media as an overarching metaphor to address the design goals. In addition, we further illustrate how interaction concepts from social media inspired the design of our LLM-MA tool’s main features.

Metaphors have been utilized to provide conceptual models that influence the perception of and interaction with AI systems \cite{10.1145/3635636.3664250, 10.1145/3313831.3376275, 10.1145/3613904.3642697}. Social media platforms, in particular, contain intuitive interaction concepts that facilitate social interaction. When used as a metaphor, it offers a familiar language for modeling and communicating the affordances \cite{10.1145/301153.301168} of a system that naturally fosters networked connections, polarized identity expression, and relational communication—all of which reflect our design goals. 

Specifically, social media offers unique features that allow people to easily connect with individuals beyond their usual acquaintances, creating an environment where everything becomes a potential point of connection \cite{papacharissi2010networked}. This characteristic aligns with \textbf{DG 1}. Drawing inspiration from this, we designed FRIENDS DISCOVERY, a feature that allows writers to specify the types of connections (e.g., Harry's long-lost friend) they wish to seek for a given character. Subsequently, the feature offers character candidates with relevant ties grounded in the defined connection point and the seed character’s personal details. 

Social media often reveals personal details about people’s lives, helping to build social bonds and reinforce a sense of identity \cite{10.1145/2317956.2317994}. Furthermore, when anonymity is guaranteed, individuals are more likely to engage in self-disclosure \cite{pohan2022use}. This often results in a shared environment where honest and at times even polarizing \cite{lee2023makes} reactions to a single event are openly expressed and compared. These traits match \textbf{DG 2}. Inspired by this, we designed JOURNALS, a feature that allows writers to first define a theme (e.g., Dumbledore has vanished), then generate diary entries in which selected characters articulate their inner thoughts on that shared topic. 

Lastly, social media is frequently used to exhibit and strengthen relationships between users. For instance, comment threads commonly serve as a space to express interpersonal affection, remark on some mutual events, or proclaim relational status \cite{ballantine2015influence}. These characteristics map with \textbf{DG 3}. Based on this, we designed COMMENTS, a feature in which writers can select characters to freely respond to each other’s journal entries, allowing their relationships to unfold through open-ended interactions generated by this feature.

