The Comments view below each journal entry can be used to create new comment threads (\autoref{fig:fig12}-A) in two modes. In Manual Writing Mode, users select characters and manually write comments from their perspective. In Automatic Generation Mode, the selected characters automatically generate comments in response to the journal entry. Users can also add new comments to existing threads (\autoref{fig:fig12}-B) using either Automatic Reply or Manual Reply Modes (\autoref{fig:fig12}-C). All comments are editable and deletable. 

Any character other than the journal’s author can be selected to initiate a new comment thread. The journal author and the selected commenter then take alternating turns to reply to each other’s comments within that thread. This structure allows for a broader range of reactions to the journal entry without compromising the depth that comes from focused one-on-one interactions.

\subsection{An Example Use Scenario}
\label{sec:4.3}
To demonstrate our system, we present how Jina, an animation writer, uses Constella for her latest project, “The Conqueror.” 

\subsubsection{Character Profiles}
\label{sec:4.3.1}
Jina has roughly conceptualized two of her main characters, “Binggu” and “Chorong.” She opens two New Character Panels (\autoref{fig:fig5}) in Constella and adds several Attributes (e.g., age, goal, backstory, personality) for each character. For Binggu’s personality, she writes, “Binggu tries to appear outwardly violent and fearless, but internally, he is actually very anxious.” For Chorong she writes, “Chorong is a warm-hearted alien who cherishes peace and happiness.” She saves both characters. 
Next, Jina opens their Profile Panels (\autoref{fig:fig6}) and creates their Relationship Attributes by making them follow each other (\autoref{fig:fig7}). Within Binggu’s profile, she writes, “Binggu finds Chorong annoying,” and decides that Binggu knows Chorong’s character description, personality, occupation, and age. Within Chorong’s profile, she states, “Chorong follows Binggu around like a puppy” and specifies that Chorong knows Binggu’s character description, personality, and current status.

\subsubsection{FRIENDS DISCOVERY}
\label{sec:4.3.2}
Jina now thinks it would be intriguing to have these two characters crash-land on Earth from outer space. To create a character who causes this crash, she uses FRIENDS DISCOVERY. She enters “Binggu’s greatest enemy” (\autoref{fig:fig8}-A) as the input and receives three suggestions: “Metal Monster,” “Starlight Witch,” and “Scrap Metal Prince” (\autoref{fig:fig8}-B). After browsing through the Mini Profiles, Jina is drawn to Metal Monster. As he was the one who wrecked Binggu’s home in the past, Metal Monster would be the perfect villain to stir up Binggu’s emotional journey. Without hesitation, Jina locks him in as the story’s antagonist by saving the character.

\subsubsection{JOURNALS}
\label{sec:4.3.3}
Jina is now curious about how Binggu and Chorong would react during their crash landing on Earth. Using JOURNALS, she selects both characters (\autoref{fig:fig10}-C) and generates their journal entries by entering the theme “Binggu and Chorong’s spaceship crashes to Earth after being hit by Metal Monster’s bomb” (\autoref{fig:fig10}-D). Through the generated entries (\autoref{fig:fig11}), Jina discovers that Binggu experiences overwhelming stress and frustration in the moment of the blast. Meanwhile, Chorong views the situation with a hint of optimism, tinged even with admiration for the surreal experience. Inspired by this contrast, Jina decides to further amplify their differences by making Chorong even more naive and wonder-filled. Chorong would then perhaps even marvel at the sparkly colors of Earth’s atmosphere during the crash and express excitement about the unexpected adventure that lies ahead. Jina updates Chorong’s Attributes to reflect these changes. 

\subsubsection{COMMENTS}
\label{sec:4.3.4}
Jina is now eager to explore how Metal Monster would respond to Binggu’s journal entry. Using COMMENTS, Jina generates Metal Monster’s comment (\autoref{fig:fig12}-A) and finds that Metal Monster is still harboring resentment toward Binggu even after his bomb landed successfully (\autoref{fig:fig12}-B). Seeing this, Jina starts to wonder, what unresolved emotions are fueling his hostility toward Binggu? Motivated by this question, she updates Metal Monster’s Relationship Attributes, adding details about the simmering jealousy that has quietly shaped his attitude toward Binggu all along.
\subsubsection{Future Usages}

\label{sec:4.3.5}
In the future, Jina can discover new friends Binggu meets on Earth using FRIENDS DISCOVERY, investigate how Metal Monster and Chorong would each react when tasting candy for the first time through JOURNALS, and witness Chorong's teasing interrogation of Metal Monster's journal entry through COMMENTS. Example generation results for each feature, based on this future usage, are also included in Appendix \ref{sec:Appendix-prompts}.

\subsection{Implementation and Prompt Pipeline}
\label{sec:4.4}
    \subsubsection{Implementation}
    \label{sec:4.4.1}
    Constella was developed as a web-based application with a front-end client built with React.js and a Node.js back-end. User-uploaded images were stored in Google Cloud Storage, while all other application data was managed in MongoDB. 

    As the participants in our user study were South Korean, we aimed to use an LLM with strong proficiency in both Korean language and cultural understanding. At the time of our research, however, there were no widely published evaluations of state-of-the-art LLMs, such as OpenAI’s GPT-4o \cite{hurst2024gpt} and the Claude 3 family \cite{anthropic2024claude3}, with regard to Korean-specific performance. To address this, we referred to \cite{corca2024gpt4o_click}, which evaluated GPT-4 and GPT-4o on CLIcK \cite{kim-etal-2024-click}, a dataset specifically designed to assess cultural and linguistic intelligence in Korean. Their results showed that GPT-4o outperformed GPT-4 and other models tested in the original CLIcK paper. Based on this, we selected GPT-4o and accessed it via the OpenAI API.

% \clearpage
    \subsubsection{Prompt Design}
    \label{sec:4.4.2}
Our prompt design was informed by OpenAI’s prompt engineering guide \cite{openai2025textgen} and prior research \cite{10.1145/3613904.3642545, 10.1145/3613904.3642406, 10.1145/3613904.3642472} that disclosed prompts for persona-based roleplaying. This section outlines the key elements of the prompts used for each AI feature in our system, focusing on their static instructions, provided context, and expected outputs (see \autoref{tab:prompt-design} for a summary). We provide the full set of prompts in Appendix \ref{sec:Appendix-prompts}.

