\paragraph*{\textbf{FRIENDS DISCOVERY}} The static instruction guides the model to roleplay as a storywriter and generate three distinct fictional characters based on a specified relationship phrase and the given character’s Attributes. Each generated character is composed of its name, introduction, backstory, and bidirectional relationship descriptions. We deliberately chose to provide a minimal set of Attributes following \textbf{DG 1}. The outputs are formatted as JSON objects to ensure proper display as Mini Profiles within the user interface. 

\paragraph*{\textbf{JOURNALS}} The static instruction directs the model to roleplay as the given character, using both the provided character’s Attributes and Relationship Attributes. The model then generates a journal entry based on a specified theme. In alignment with \textbf{DG 2}, each entry begins with the phrase “Dear Diary,” thereby establishing the entry as an intimate monologue that reveals the character’s inner self to an imagined confidant. When multiple characters are selected, this process is executed in parallel, and the resulting entries are presented together for simultaneous viewing. 

\paragraph*{\textbf{COMMENTS}} The static instruction places the model in the character’s role by making it refer to the character's Attributes and Relationship Attributes. Taking account of the open-endedness emphasized in \textbf{DG 3}, the model is given freedom to focus on a specific aspect of the journal entry when generating a response. In a threaded reply, the model is further guided by the preceding comment history to maintain continuity while focusing on the most recent comment in the thread. 

In addition, our prompt design adopts a stateless generation approach; characters do not retain memory of previously generated journal entries beyond the current one, nor of comments outside the current thread. This design choice was motivated by the assumption that character Attributes would be iteratively revised during writers’ creative process. Persisting prior outputs could thus introduce inconsistencies with the updated character definitions and potentially burden users to micromanage previously generated content. Thus, we believed that a deliberate forgetting strategy \cite{10.5555/AAI29327510} would help writers iterate more freely in favor of emergent ideas and reduce creative pressure by eliminating a sense of finality.



