\section{User Study}
\label{sec:5}
We conducted a 7–8 day system deployment study to evaluate Constella. Our aim was to understand how Constella integrates into storywriters’ existing practices and how it supports their interconnected character creation process. 

CSTs are often evaluated through a mix of observing user actions during the creative process and subjective experience reports \cite{10.1145/3635636.3656204} (e.g., Creativity Support Index (CSI) \cite{10.1145/2617588}). Given that Constella was designed to assist storywriters’ creative processes within the context of full-length story creation, we chose to evaluate our tool by exploring its use through a system deployment study. This approach provides participants with the temporal and spatial flexibility to use the tool over an extended period within their natural environments and established processes \cite{10.1145/3357236.3395474}. To closely track their experiences throughout this period, we collected system interaction logs that stored all our participants’ actions (e.g., generations, edits, deletions) performed within Constella. We also employed a diary study, along with follow-up interviews, to revisit participants’ experiences in greater depth. Lastly, we selected the CSI to gather quantifiable subjective experience reports. The CSI is a widely used and psychometrically validated survey instrument that measures how well a system supports creative activities. We specifically used the survey to identify the strengths and weaknesses of Constella in its role as a CST. The study process was approved by the university’s Institutional Review Board (IRB No. concealed for anonymity).

