We recruited participants with prior experience in creating full-length fictional stories and aimed to capture insights across diverse story types, genres, and creative writing experiences. We recruited participants with and without formal creative writing education, as well as those with and without publication experience. Additionally, writers who had participated in the formative study were excluded from the recruitment pool. This was to explore the broad applicability of our tool with writers whose practices may differ from those of the formative study participants. Recruitment channels and the sampling method were identical to our formative study.

As 2 of the 13 participants initially recruited withdrew from the study due to personal reasons, we report details (\autoref{table:userstudy-demo}) for our final group of 11 storywriters (F=7, M=4). Story types ranged from drama (N=6), novel (N=6), film (N=4), comics (N=2), plays (N=1), and animation (N=1). The total number of writing genres they had experience with reached 20. The duration of their creative writing experience ranged from 1 to 20 years, with an average of 10.82 years. While not part of the inclusion criteria, participants also reported their levels of experience with LLMs, as their proficiency in prompting could potentially influence how they interact with the tool. All participants resided in South Korea. 

\subsection{Procedure}
\label{sec:5.2}
    \subsubsection{Tasks}
    \label{sec:5.2.1}
    Creating a full-length story is an extensive process that can take several months or even years. Therefore, we split the process into a set of tasks focused enough to explore system usage while aligning well with participants’ usual writing practices. 

    \paragraph*{\textbf{(1) Characters}} We asked participants to create at least five characters (profiles) within Constella. Although the number of characters in fictional stories varies widely, Truby \cite{truby2008anatomy} proposed that characters can be classified based on their roles: the hero, opponent, ally, fake-ally opponent, and subplot character. While not all stories require characters for each of these five roles, some may feature multiple characters fulfilling the same role. Thus, we did not prescribe specific character roles but requested a minimum number of characters. 

    \paragraph*{\textbf{(2) Character Backstory}} We asked participants to write a central character’s backstory. This is a foundational activity according to storywriting literature (§\ref{sec:2.1}) and one that was also utilized by our formative study participants (§\ref{sec:3.5.2}). Given that relationships often form the basis of backstories \cite{barker2019build}, we asked participants to include how their central character influenced or was influenced by other characters in their backstory. For this task, we asked writers to use FRIENDS DISCOVERY, as this feature was designed to support writers in discovering related characters. We included this task to ensure FRIENDS DISCOVERY could be utilized non-intrusively, eliminating the need for participants to incorporate entirely new characters in their narratives, as they may have already set clear goals for their character cast. In addition, we only asked participants to write a backstory for one character, as this task was sufficient for trying FRIENDS DISCOVERY. 

   \paragraph*{\textbf{(3) Scenes}} Participants were asked to write three types of scenes: Scene Type A, B, and C.

    \textit{\textbf{Scene Type A.}} Writers were requested to write a scene where several characters show contrasting or aligned perspectives. The former can be interpreted as conflict and the latter as unity, both essential elements in stories \cite{egri1972art}. Therefore, we believed that this scene could naturally be integrated into their narrative. We encouraged participants to use JOURNALS when writing this scene, as the feature is intended to help writers delve into and compare their characters’ thoughts, making it relevant to this task.

     \textit{\textbf{Scene Type B.}} Participants were instructed to create a scene that shows the relationship between two characters. As character relationships are also central components in stories, we expected these requirements to cause minimal deviation from their narrative goals. We asked writers to use COMMENTS when writing this scene, as this feature was designed to help authors elaborate upon character relationships. We also note that while participants could include any number of multiple characters per scene, we asked them to focus on two-character relationships in Scene Type B, as COMMENTS mainly supports dyadic interactions (§\ref{sec:4.2.3}).

     \textit{\textbf{Scene Type C.}} Participants were asked to create a scene that involved several characters. For this task, participants were informed to use at least one of the system’s AI features. We provided minimal requirements for this scene to explore additional system usage when writers were given more freedom to use Constella according to their own needs. 

    \paragraph*{\textbf{(4) Story Outline}} We asked participants to complete a story outline for a full-length story featuring at least five characters (profiles). Before completion, we asked writers to use at least one of Constella’s AI features. This task was included because this study aimed to explore system usage within the context of writing a full-length story. The act of writing a story outline provided this overarching context, while its compressed nature, such as lacking details compared to the entire full-length story, was complemented by the aforementioned tasks. 

    \subsubsection{Supplementary Task Guidelines and Ethical Protocols}
    \label{sec:5.2.2}
    We provided supplementary guidelines to support task flexibility, accommodate participants' preferred writing practices, and ensure ethical handling of intellectual property.

    \textit{\textbf{System Outcomes}.} Participants were asked to try the system's features for the tasks (§\ref{sec:5.2.1}), but the decision to actually use the system-generated outcomes and how to incorporate them was entirely at their discretion. 

    \textit{\textbf{Order}.} The aforementioned tasks are not sequential steps but a culmination of final results. For example, participants could begin outlining their story while creating character profiles, write Scene Type C, and then revise their outline. 

   \textit{\textbf{Length and Format}.} Participants could freely select the story type and genre for their narrative. These choices ultimately influenced the length and format of the tasks. For instance, some writers preferred a screenplay format, often writing more pages due to dialogue and spacing conventions. Others favored detailed prose, which tended to be more textually dense despite occupying fewer pages. 

    \textit{\textbf{Word Processor}.} Participants selected their preferred word processor (e.g., Microsoft Word) to complete the tasks.

    \textit{\textbf{Intellectual Property}.} Participants were informed, both during recruitment and before the start of the study, that their work (backstory, scenes, outline) would not be disclosed in its entirety to external parties. Instead, they would be used solely to provide context during our data analysis process. However, we clarified that all content, generated and manually written within Constella, could be fully disclosed for academic purposes. All participants agreed to the above terms. To formalize this process, we provided a non-exclusive intellectual property license agreement detailing these terms, which each participant chose to sign.

    \subsubsection{Study Procedure}
    \label{sec:5.2.3}

    \begin{figure}[H]
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figure/fig-userstudy-process.png}
        \caption{Procedure of the user study.}
        \label{fig:userstudy-process}
    \end{figure}
    The user study consisted of three parts: (1) introductory session, (2) main session, and (3) post-interview (\autoref{fig:userstudy-process}). We describe each part in detail below.


    \textbf{ (1) Introductory Session (60 minutes)}: We conducted individual introductory sessions with each participant via Zoom. After obtaining informed consent, we introduced the goal of our study and provided a live tutorial of the system. The tutorial included an overview of the system’s features and their purposes, followed by a hands-on session allowing participants to explore the tool themselves. We then provided guidelines for the diary study and explained the tasks for the main session. 

    \textbf{(2) Main Session (7-8 days)}: Over a 7-8 day period, participants were asked to complete the assigned tasks and record their thoughts in an online diary each day. For the diary, we utilized Notion \cite{notion2025}, a note-taking application, to enable quick and easy documentation. The diary included questions (\autoref{table:userstudy-diary} in Appendix \ref{sec:Appendix-questions}) to guide participants in documenting both their storywriting process and system usage experience. While our initial timeframe for the study was set at 7 days, a duration determined through a pilot study, six participants requested an additional day to complete their tasks. As a result, five participants took part in the main session for 7 days and six participants for 8 days. 

    \textbf{(3) Post-Interview (120 minutes)}: Finally, we conducted individual semi-structured interviews with each participant to gain an in-depth understanding of their experiences. Each interview was conducted via Zoom. We began by asking about their overall experience with the system, followed by specific questions about each feature (\autoref{table:userstudy-interview} in Appendix \ref{sec:Appendix-questions}). We then inquired about their Notion diary entries to understand the context and meaning behind their documented responses. Throughout the interview, participants were encouraged to reference their Notion diaries, the system, and completed tasks to recall and explain their experiences in detail. At the end of this session, participants completed the CSI. All participants received compensation of 350,000 KRW (approx. 251 USD) for their participation.


\subsection{Analysis}
\label{sec:5.3}
We transcribed over 1,320 minutes of audio recordings from interviews with 11 participants. We also gathered their Notion diary entries, completed tasks, Constella account data, system logs, and CSI results. The data from the interview transcriptions and diary entries were qualitatively analyzed through reflective thematic analysis \cite{braun2019reflecting}. 

Our analysis process was conducted through the following procedures. First, the first and second authors repeatedly read through the entire dataset of the diary entries and interview transcriptions. Then, these two authors developed initial codes by categorizing the data based on our tool’s features. After this process, the first and second authors reviewed the initial codes while cross-referencing system logs, participants’ Constella account data, and completed tasks to identify themes. These themes were then iteratively refined through discussions among all the authors. As a result of this six-month process, we constructed themes related to use cases for interconnected character creation, the tool’s impact on writers and their writing processes, and its potential future usages, suggested improvements, and limitations. We also note that we found no significant thematic variation based on participants’ duration of creative writing experience and thus did not pursue further analysis along that dimension.


