 \clearpage

    \paragraph*{\textbf{FRIENDS DISCOVERY}} Some writers (e.g., P9, P10) used this feature exclusively during the early stages of their process, suggesting a preference for establishing their entire character cast before delving into story composition (see yellow gold-colored bars in \autoref{fig:systemlog}). Others (e.g., P4, P6) used this feature throughout their writing process, indicating its utility in continuously introducing new characters as the narrative evolved. In total, 41 out of 89 characters, and on average 3.73 characters (SD=1.95) out of 8.09 characters (SD=1.81) featured in their work were initially created through FRIENDS DISCOVERY. These 41 characters spanned a wide range of identities, including a wizard, psychopath, astronomer, security guard, and aunt. They also served diverse narrative roles, such as supporting characters, antagonists, and sub-protagonists. Examples of prompts used by participants include “Hosu’s co-worker” (P3), “At first, they had a hostile relationship, but gradually, they came to understand and love each other” (P6), and “A relationship bound by secrets that must never be revealed” (P11). 

    \paragraph*{\textbf{JOURNALS}} This feature was the most consistently used AI feature (see olive green-colored bars in \autoref{fig:systemlog}). Although initially assigned for Scene Type A, several participants (e.g., P1, P2, P5) employed this feature daily. They expanded its use to tasks such as backstory writing and selectively applied it across other tasks as well. By the end of the study, participants’ Constella accounts contained a total of 539 individual journal entries. Of these, 445 were created using the feature’s option that allows multiple characters to produce an entry on the same theme. On average, participants’ accounts contained 49 entries (SD=42.51). These entries covered a range of themes, including characters’ backgrounds, daily lives, thoughts about each other, and situations closely related to their stories. Specific journal themes included “A lucky day” (P1), “Chaeyeon was caught by Hyunsu while trying to steal chemicals in the science lab and failed. What do you think?” (P2), and “Before Choi Ji-ah is eaten by the monster, Kim Chul-soo rescues her. He also saves Choi Ji-ah's parents. Because of this, Kim Chul-soo becomes a benefactor to Choi Ji-ah’s family. At this moment, Choi Ji-ah awakens as a hero. She then decides to help Kim Chul-soo seek revenge.” (P10).

    \paragraph*{\textbf{COMMENTS}} This feature was used more intermittently compared to JOURNALS (see red clay-colored bars in \autoref{fig:systemlog}). This suggests that participants employed the feature for relatively more focused or limited purposes. Upon completing the study, participants’ Constella accounts contained a total of 175 comment threads, with an average of 15.91 threads per participant (SD=14.28). In addition, each thread contained an average of 1.74 comments (SD=1.18), suggesting that COMMENTS was primarily utilized to observe brief character reactions to journal entries rather than for extended discussions. 


