    Constella received an average CSI score of 81.39 out of 100 (SD=12.02) (\autoref{table:csi-results}). Among the six CSI factors, “Enjoyment” received the highest individual score (M=17.45). Further analysis of our qualitative data shows that a key contributor to this score was the social media metaphor. Beyond helping participants (P2, P3, P4, P6, P8, P10, P11) intuitively understand tool usage and thereby feel more at ease, the social media metaphor was regarded by writers (P2, P4, P6, P10) to help foster a playful environment. As P2 noted, \textit{“The format of social media, such as showing when someone is online and including comments, made it feel playful to me.”} In addition, the specific interaction concepts of the social media metaphor also led participants to playfully envision fiction-writing techniques and narrative possibilities grounded in its unique affordances. For example, P10 speculated on using the asynchronous nature of COMMENTS to create dramatic irony, explaining,\textit{ “Until a reply is made, [the diary writer] doesn’t know the content of the comment, right? I think using that kind of setup could be really fun. [...] like when the reader and the protagonist know something, but the villain doesn’t.” }


    Conversely, “Immersion” was the lowest-scoring CSI factor (M=14.36). One explanation provided by our participants was the need to switch back and forth between their word processors and Constella, which disrupted their focus. We initially chose not to include a built-in word processing feature out of respect for authors’ existing tool preferences. However, based on our findings, we touch upon why integrating such functionality may be useful in §\ref{sec:6.4.2}.


\subsection{Use Cases for Interconnected Character Creation}
\label{sec:6.2}
We report several use cases for each key feature in Constella to illustrate how these features support storywriters’ interconnected character creation process. 

    \subsubsection{FRIENDS DISCOVERY}
    % \paragraph*{Contextualized Discoveries} Writers were able to create diverse characters connected to the central figure in contexts they desired. Additionally, the feature enabled them to uncover unexpected yet meaningful points of connection. 

    \noindent
    \begin{tcolorbox}[arc=0mm,boxrule=0.6pt]
    \textbf{Contextualized Discoveries:} Writers were able to create diverse characters linked to the central figure in contexts they desired. The feature also enabled them to uncover unexpected yet meaningful connection points. 
    \end{tcolorbox}

    All participants either explicitly stated or generally agreed that they were able to discover various characters in contexts they desired. For example, P10 described using this feature by first writing a detailed backstory in the protagonist’s profile. Then, by entering the simple relationship phrase “revenge” in the input field of FRIENDS DISCOVERY, he discovered three characters that not only aligned with but also expanded his preconceived conceptions of the antagonists.

    Additionally, participants (P3, P5, P6, P8) expressed astonishment when the tool surfaced connections they had not initially considered. P6 shared a case in which the tool suggested a character associated with the element of “swimming.” This unexpected detail turned out to be crucial for her story, offering a natural and meaningful way for the protagonist to meet the sub-protagonist, the suggested character, for the first time. 
    \begin{quote}
        \textit{“I had only briefly mentioned swimming as part of the protagonist's daily routine, which I included as a small detail while writing [the protagonist’s profile]. But when it suddenly emerged [in the suggested character], I realized that the system had considered even these minor details and made connections.”} (P6) 
    \end{quote}
    Finally, participants (P5, P11) expressed high satisfaction with the recommended characters, noting that they were stylistically consistent with their central characters. This coherence allowed new characters to be integrated smoothly, without disrupting the story’s overall mood or tone.
    \begin{quote}
        \textit{“When I wanted a character with a vibe similar to Character A  [...] it would provide recommendations for characters that matched the similar vibe I wanted but offered slightly different variations.”} (P5)
   \end{quote}

   % \paragraph*{Story World and Narrative Expansion} Generated characters often transcended their initially envisioned roles and contributed to the narrative in new and unforeseen directions.  
    \vspace{1em}
     \noindent
    \begin{tcolorbox}[arc=0mm,boxrule=0.6pt]
    \textbf{Story World and Narrative Expansion:} Generated characters often transcended their initially envisioned roles and contributed to the narrative in new and unforeseen directions.  
    \end{tcolorbox}

    Participants used FRIENDS DISCOVERY to identify new characters that aligned with their initial ideas, which they then further developed based on their narrative needs. However, there were also instances where participants (P1, P3, P4, P5, P7, P8, P11) derived entirely new ideas from the recommended characters, which in turn sparked the creation of new storylines. For example, P4 shared that the subplot she developed around a supporting character, initially generated through Constella, felt even more compelling than the protagonist's storyline she had initially conceived. P4 continued:
    \begin{quote}
        \textit{“Originally, creating main characters requires a lot of thought, so paying attention to supporting characters or secondary events usually happens later in the writing process. But this method feels new and helpful. As I included the recommended supporting characters in my story and they merged with my protagonist’s narrative arc, I was able to create a whole new story.”} (P4)
    \end{quote}
    Some participants (P3, P8, P11) even adopted the experimental approach of generating entire communities of related characters early in their ideation process, then built their narrative around these characters. For instance, P11 described how she built two distinct story worlds, populated with a village-like network of interconnected figures, each centered around a different character.
    \begin{quote}
        \textit{“In my case, I initially created many supporting characters around the main character, Yoo Ji-an. I kept imagining a world connected to her, and then at some point, I wanted to create a completely different world. [...] By continuing to generate related characters, I ended up with two very different worlds inhabited by distinct characters.”} (P11) 
    \end{quote}

     % \paragraph*{Stockpile Resources} Generated characters often served as stockpile resources, flexible components ready to be divided, combined, or set aside for use in unexpected situations. 
    \vspace{1em}
     \noindent
    \begin{tcolorbox}[arc=0mm,boxrule=0.6pt]
    \textbf{Stockpile Resources:} Generated characters often served as stockpile resources, flexible components ready to be divided, combined, or set aside for use in unexpected situations.  
    \end{tcolorbox}

    While generated characters were often used as individual entities, some participants (P1, P2, P3, P10, P11) selectively incorporated elements from the generated characters into existing ones or synthesized them into their ideal characters. P1 noted that this was possible because Constella provided multiple character recommendations at once.
    \begin{quote}
        \textit{“Since about three characters are created, I was able to combine various characteristics from them to shape the character I wanted, which was definitely helpful.”} (P1) 
    \end{quote}
    Furthermore, P2 saved suggested characters even if they were not immediately needed in her story, in case she might need them in the future. 
    \begin{quote}
        \textit{“By creating and saving characters, I could bring them back later as cameos when the story hit a dead end, allowing the narrative to take a different direction.”} (P2)
    \end{quote}

    \label{sec:6.2.1}
    \subsubsection{JOURNALS}

    % \paragraph*{Introspective Construction} JOURNALS helped writers delve into the inner worlds of their characters, including those who might have otherwise remained sidelined or underdeveloped.
    \noindent
    \begin{tcolorbox}[arc=0mm,boxrule=0.6pt]
    \textbf{Introspective Construction:} JOURNALS helped writers delve into the inner worlds of their characters, including those who might have otherwise remained sidelined or underdeveloped.  
    \end{tcolorbox}

    All participants mentioned that JOURNALS was helpful for delving into their characters’ psychology, thoughts, and emotions. In addition, some writers (P2, P4, P8, P9, P11) noted that the feature also facilitated the development of the supporting cast. For instance, P8 even wrote a scene from the villain’s perspective for his task, explaining that this was possible because the feature helped him deeply think about the character’s emotions and ideology. P8 further explains that using the feature led him to humanize the antagonist, endowing the villain with human qualities that made it difficult for him to view the villain solely with contempt. P9 similarly noted that JOURNALS was useful for concretizing the backgrounds of characters that might otherwise have been used in a throwaway manner. 
    \begin{quote}
        \textit{“I think there are characters who inevitably end up being used in a disposable way for the protagonist. While working on the detailed background of such characters [through JOURNALS], I found that I could effectively use these characters to shape scenes or plots that could appear later on.”} (P9)
   \end{quote}
    JOURNALS was also perceived to help writers (P4, P10, P11) deeply immerse themselves in several characters simultaneously. For instance, P11 noted that this helped her distribute attention and emotional investment more evenly across characters.
    \begin{quote}
        \textit{“When a writer keeps writing the dialogue or situation for two characters, they might end up becoming more immersed in one character or fail to fully understand the perspective of the other. [...] But by creating [journal entries] first and then reading through both perspectives, I found it helped me approach the process more objectively and distribute [my] emotions more evenly between the two characters.”} (P11)
    \end{quote}

    % \paragraph*{Relational Construction} JOURNALS helped writers create characters in relation to one another, either by allowing them to view one character through another’s perspective or by exploring how multiple characters respond differently to shared themes.
     \vspace{1em}
     \noindent
    \begin{tcolorbox}[arc=0mm,boxrule=0.6pt]
    \textbf{Relational Construction:} JOURNALS helped writers create characters in relation to one another, either by allowing them to view one character through others' perspectives or by exploring how multiple characters respond differently to shared themes.
    \end{tcolorbox}

    By viewing \textit{Character B} from \textit{Character A}’s perspective through the journal entries, participants (P2, P7, P9) were able to further develop \textit{Character B}. For example, P7 mentioned that viewing “Jane” through the eyes of “Taesung” helped him imagine Jane’s identity in greater depth. Similarly, P2 shared her experience about a journal entry as follows:
    \begin{quote}
        \textit{“As I started to look at the new situation of the security guard, a supporting character, through the [main character's] eyes, I found myself wondering, what might have happened to the man? The wind blew a lot, and it rained. Were there any difficult or troublesome complaints he had to deal with?”} (P2) 
    \end{quote}
    Participants (P1, P2, P4, P5, P7, P8, P9, P11) also stated that having two or more characters generate journal entries based on a shared theme helped them distinguish or create similarities between characters. Specifically, P1 noted that observing diverse thoughts across several characters aided in the creation of each of their dialogues, narrations, and non-verbal actions. P4 also explained that comparing journal entries also helped in plot creation. 
    \begin{quote}
        \textit{“When I asked the same question to multiple characters, I noticed differences between them within the journals, and by comparing those differences, I was able to create new scenes and storylines.”} (P4)
     \end{quote}

    % \paragraph*{Mise-en-Scène\footnote{ Mise-en-scène refers to the visual elements within a frame, including lighting, color, and the arrangement of objects \cite{kuhn2012dictionary}} of the Mind} The visual elements depicted in the journal entries were used to express the mindscapes within and between characters.
     \vspace{1em}
    \noindent
    \begin{tcolorbox}[arc=0mm,boxrule=0.6pt]
    \textbf{Mise-en-Scène\footnotemark{} of the Mind:} The visual elements depicted in the journal entries were used to express the mindscapes within and between characters.
    \end{tcolorbox}
    \footnotetext{Mise-en-scène refers to the visual elements within a frame,
    including lighting, color, and the arrangement of objects \cite{kuhn2012dictionary}. \textit{“Mise-en-scene can be a site of extraordinarily complex and subtle meanings, [...] in which mise-en-scene often provides ironic commentary on the characters and the worlds they inhabit”} \cite{kuhn2012dictionary}.}

    As JOURNALS often generated descriptive details of the characters’ surrounding environments, participants (P1, P2, P5, P9, P11) drew inspiration from the locations, landscapes, and weather described in the entries. These spatial and environmental cues served not only as narrative settings but also as symbolic reflections of characters’ internal states. For example, P11 used such imagery to internalize a character’s emotional condition and developed scenes based on her interpretive reflections:
    \begin{quote}
        \textit{“Descriptions of Jian’s feelings [in her journal entry], like wanting to return to a green forest but being trapped in a gray city, these types of phrases weren’t directly used as dialogues. Instead, they evoked certain imagery or moods for me. When I absorbed these, some kind of chemical reaction happened within me, and I was able to create scenes and scripts.”} (P11)
    \end{quote}
    P9 drew on the image of “sunlight” from a journal entry not to suggest emotional warmth, but to emphasize its absence, showing how even a bright day cannot dispel the chill between the characters.
     \begin{quote}
        \textit{“I think I spent a lot of time thinking about how to use ‘sunlight’ effectively. As I looked through the journal, I realized [the protagonist] had never felt warmth from Sunwoo. Their meetings had always been cold, happening at night. So I wanted to show how even that daytime setting gets overtaken by the same nighttime mood.”} (P9) 
    \end{quote}

    \label{sec:6.2.2}
    \subsubsection{COMMENTS}
    \label{sec:6.2.3}
    % \paragraph*{Extra Layers} COMMENTS was mainly used as an extension for the generated journal entries, allowing writers to further explore character relationships through additional responses. 

     \noindent
    \begin{tcolorbox}[arc=0mm,boxrule=0.6pt]
    \textbf{Extra Layers:} COMMENTS was mainly used as an extension for the generated journal entries, allowing writers to further explore character relationships through additional responses. 
    \end{tcolorbox}

    Some participants found COMMENTS more effective than JOURNALS for understanding the psychological interplay between characters (P2) or for establishing character relationships (P7). While JOURNALS supported the development of individual emotional arcs through monologue-like reflections, COMMENTS was more effective for exploring interpersonal emotional dynamics, as they mimicked communicative exchanges.

    In a similar vein, participants (P2, P4, P6, P7, P8, P10, P11) mentioned that observing character responses through COMMENTS helped them further elaborate on relational subtleties. For example, P4 used COMMENTS to detect subtle cues of hidden animosities or ill intent, which informed her decisions about whether those characters could plausibly collaborate as allies. In contrast, P10 generated comments to reinforce existing dynamics rather than introduce new shifts in relationships.
    \begin{quote}
        \textit{“I noticed through the comments that, even after such humiliation and having his Achilles tendon severed, [Lee Min-jun] doesn’t give up. That made me want to crush [Lee Min-jun] even more. I think those impressions ended up being reflected in the scenes.”} (P10)
    \end{quote}
