\subsection{Impact on Writers and Their Writing Process}
\label{sec:6.3}
    \subsubsection{Alignment and Changes to Writers’ Existing Practices}
    \label{sec:6.3.1}
      \paragraph*{\textbf{Alignment}} All participants (N=11) agreed that FRIENDS DISCOVERY aligned well with their established practices. They explained that this feature mirrored their usual approach of conceptualizing new characters in relation to existing ones. JOURNALS also aligned well with most writers’ existing practices (N=9). P4 specifically mentioned that this feature was similar to her established method of considering how different characters would each react to a shared situation. COMMENTS received mixed feedback in terms of effective practice alignment. While participants who expressed positive remarks (N=6) mentioned the usefulness of this feature to explore character relationships, others who gave negative feedback (N=5) pointed out that COMMENTS felt unnecessary or inefficient compared to JOURNALS. This was because JOURNALS could be utilized to serve similar purposes as COMMENTS, but conveyed more extensive details due to their longer format.

    \paragraph*{\textbf{Changes}} Constella also introduced notable shifts in participants’ existing writing practices. Writers (P2, P3, P8, P11) who typically initiated story ideation with conflicts or a logline reported that our tool nudged them to begin instead with character development. Participants also appreciated how Constella shifted their focus towards deeper engagement with a broader range of characters, including those they might not have even considered at all, at an earlier stage in their writing process. Furthermore, some writers (P8, P10) became curious about exploring the perspectives of characters not directly involved in the current story situation. P10, in particular, enjoyed using this newfound ability to speculate on how other unassociated characters might react alongside those more centrally involved, \textit{“since the rumor can spread to others.”} Lastly, Constella offered valuable insights for participants to refine their creative methods in the future. P3 came to appreciate the value of having a diverse pool of characters who could be flexibly integrated into any scenario. Other writers (P8, P9) expressed a desire to change their usual approach of organizing character attributes and relationships, inspired by the structure and layout of the Profile feature.

    \subsubsection{Preserving Authorial Control}
    \label{sec:6.3.2}
    Participants perceived Constella to play a complementary role in their writing process, with some (P7, P8, P10, P11) referring to it as an \textit{“assistant writer.”} Our analysis suggests that this perception was shaped by specific design aspects of Constella that supported writers in maintaining their creative voice within human-AI collaboration. Below, we examine these design elements in greater detail. 

    \paragraph*{\textbf{Constraining AI Involvement}} Since FRIENDS DISCOVERY generates characters only in relation to existing ones, the responsibility of creating profiles for the initial characters, typically the main protagonists, was placed on the writers. Participants (P7, P11) noted that this feature resembled their co-writing dynamics, where they delegate secondary character creation to collaborators. They also mentioned that creating descriptions for the main character themselves helped ensure a greater sense of ownership of their work (P10, P11). Furthermore, as the tool only provides core character attributes, writers (P3, P11) noted that this created room for them to build on the suggested characters with their own ideas.
    \begin{quote}
    \textit{“If the main characters were also created by the tool, I think it could be like someone addicted to gambling, just staring blankly at the app, completely absorbed. But since only supporting characters are generated and there’s a certain limit, it made me snap out and think, wait a minute, I need to create this part myself.”} (P11)
    \end{quote}

    \paragraph*{\textbf{Intermediary Materials}} We designed JOURNALS and COMMENTS to generate text in formats that resemble diary entries and responses to these entries. Participants (P1, P3, P9, P10) mentioned that they were initially surprised because they had only heard of AI tools that generate entire outlines or stories in output-ready formats. However, for our tool, writers (P1, P2, P7, P8, P9, P10) pointed out the importance of their own role in consciously extracting meaning from the generated results. When doing so, they found these resources could scaffold them to further envision and realize their characters and narratives themselves.  
    \begin{quote}
    \textit{“Usually, when people think of AI, they assume it just generates stories automatically when given a prompt. But in that case, AI is the one leading the process. In contrast, when I provide the input, this tool creates journals and other things, and through these journals, I create my scenes.”} (P10)
    \end{quote}


\subsection{Future Usages, Suggested Improvements, and Limitations}
\label{sec:6.4}
    \subsubsection{Potential while Writing the Entire Full-Length Story}
    \label{sec:6.4.1}
    Participants suggested that Constella would be utilized even more extensively when writing the main text of the story, rather than summarized outlines. When engaged in this process, writers (P1, P3, P7, P8, P10) noted they would use the tool to delve even deeper into characters’ backstories and thoughts, concretize their relationships, and create more supporting characters. P8 also shared that using Constella to develop identities for background characters, instead of simply treating them as extras, would even help imagine their visual appearances during the illustration process.

    In addition, participants (P1, P3, P8) mentioned that Constella would be more beneficial when used for large-scale stories that require expansive worldbuilding and complex relationships, such as serialized work and different stories that share the same fictional world. Considering such situations that require vast numbers of interconnected characters, updating FRIENDS DISCOVERY to generate characters associated with multiple individuals (P2), including character relationship maps (P1, P10, P11), adding a tagging feature (e.g., @name) to easily change and reference character names (P10, P11), and organizing characters into groups (P3, P6, P8) could further help writers expand and manage their character casts.

    \subsubsection{Additional Support for Writers’ Linear, Non-linear, and Iterative Processes}
     \label{sec:6.4.2}
    Participants (P2, P11) requested a feature that allows characters to recall previously generated journal entries and comment threads to explore character relationships that evolve over time. Additionally, a version control \cite{10.1145/3555756} feature to track and recover changes in character profiles was suggested (P3, P4, P7, P10). Specifically, P4 expressed interest in exploring characters non-linearly by aligning profile versions with different points along the story’s timeline. While writers often created journals for a specific purpose, they also derived new meanings when reviewing entries in sequence or looking back on them later (P3, P4, P9, P11). As a result, a feature that allows users to reorganize journal entries together into new categories or story timelines was also requested (P3, P4). To better support the iterative process of editing character profiles, participants (P3, P7) suggested integrating a word processor into Constella, allowing them to seamlessly make changes while writing their stories within a single interface.

    \subsubsection{Generation Quality and Intent Elaboration}
     \label{sec:6.4.3}
     Some writers (P5, P8) mentioned that JOURNALS and COMMENTS were sometimes overly constrained by character attributes, leading to a lack of novelty. For instance, P8 mentioned that the system tended to echo character descriptions too literally; if a character was described as disliking someone, that sentiment would appear in the generated text regardless of the overall context. 

    In addition, a common critique was that the generated results felt somewhat mundane or stereotypical (P1, P2, P3, P5, P6, P9), and the characters were depicted as excessively kind (P1, P2, P5, P6, P11). However, many of these writers (P1, P2, P9, P11) also mentioned that their perceptions shifted to varying degrees after creating more detailed character profiles or prompts. They also believed that additional efforts in doing so could have yielded better results (P1, P3, P5, P6, P11). That said, participants found the process of creating character profiles in detail challenging. Specifically, writers (P2, P3, P8, P11) mentioned a trade-off between the effort required in detailing character profiles and the benefit of obtaining consistent, high-quality generations. For example, writers (P2, P3, P8) did not input all character details (e.g., nationality, manner of speech) as they typically only write down key traits when working alone. They also mentioned that adding descriptions to achieve more aligned results felt frustrating since they had already mentally envisioned those characteristics. Continuously updating character profiles to incorporate updated information from the storyline also required considerable effort (P8, P11). We discuss potential methods to alleviate these issues in §\ref{sec:7.5} and §\ref{sec:8}.

    \subsubsection{Limited Narrative Advancement}
     \label{sec:6.4.4}
     While acknowledging that this was not Constella's intended purpose, some writers (P1, P5, P7, P11) expressed disappointment that the generated results for COMMENTS and JOURNALS were not presented in forms they found \textit{“directly applicable to the story” }(P7) or had limited ability to \textit{“progress the narrative”} (P11). This was explained because the outputs lacked sufficient action sequences (P5, P7, P11) or were inadequate for simulating in-the-moment conversational interactions mimicking the form of dialogues in stories (P1, P11). Instead, the outputs were focused more on characters’ thoughts, emotions, and reflections of past events. While this approach was perceived as a shortcoming by some, we offer our reflections on how it may have encouraged writers to exercise greater creative judgment in §\ref{sec:7.3}.

