\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:7}
In this section, we first identify opportunities for AI interaction design in storywriting. To do so, we begin by reflecting on our system’s design goals through revisiting patterns of user appropriation (§\ref{sec:7.1}) and by exploring how multi-agent interactions can be intentionally designed to encourage balanced attention across the character cast (§\ref{sec:7.2}). We then present broader design implications for AI-based writing support tools that value authorial agency by highlighting the value of deliberate constraints and intermediary materials (§\ref{sec:7.3}). We also discuss the opportunities and challenges of employing metaphors in AI-based CSTs (§\ref{sec:7.4}). Finally, we address the challenge of intent alignment, advocating for AI assistance that reduces cognitive load without diminishing opportunities for user reflection (§\ref{sec:7.5}). 


\subsection{Reflecting on the Design Goals}
\label{sec:7.1}
We reflect on our system design goals outlined in §\ref{sec:3.6}, assessing the extent to which they have been achieved through our user study. We also use participants’ acts of appropriation \cite{10.1145/3586183.3606831} and misuse  \cite{10.1145/3706598.3714068} uncovered through the study as resources to identify design opportunities for future AI-based storywriting support tools. 

\paragraph*{\textbf{DG 1: Suggest diverse characters related to existing ones}} FRIENDS DISCOVERY allows writers to discover three new characters in relation to a given character by specifying their relationship. In the study, all participants reported that our tool helped them discover various characters aligned within their desired relational contexts (§\ref{sec:6.2.1}). As the tool offered multiple variations, we also observed that several writers (N=4) engaged in a creative process of remixing suggested characters by combining several of their attributes. This emergent use highlights a potential area for improvement: generating new character variations based on remixed attributes. For instance, a writer might select the element of a “magician” from one suggested character and the element of “sleepwalking” from another. The system could then generate further character variations based on their combinations while still aligning with the original relationship prompt. This approach could potentially expand the diversity of characters writers can explore while preserving their relational ties to existing ones. 

\paragraph*{\textbf{DG 2: Support the comparison and investigation of in-depth character identities}} JOURNALS provide writers with characters’ introspective reactions toward a shared theme. All participants in our study regarded the feature as a valuable aid to delve into their characters’ minds and personal viewpoints (§\ref{sec:6.2.2}). In addition, writers (N=8) mentioned that having multiple characters reflect on the same theme helped them better understand and concretize contrasts and similarities between characters. Lastly, we found that several writers (N=5) drew inspiration from the visual and spatial descriptions within the journal entries, treating them as expressive cues that represent characters’ internal states. In light of these use cases, we propose expanding JOURNALS to support the externalization of characters’ mindscapes through image-generative AI. Such introspective visualizations can leverage the power of ambiguity \cite{10.1145/642611.642653, 10.1145/3706598.3713722} to facilitate writers’ meaning-making and interpretation while faithfully expressing the emotional texture and mood of each character’s inner world. 

\paragraph*{\textbf{DG 3: Manifest character relationships through exploratory interactions}} COMMENTS enables writers to explore open-ended character reactions toward others’ journal entries. While many writers (N=7) found the feature helpful for surfacing inter-character dynamics (§\ref{sec:6.2.3}), we also observed that JOURNALS was often used for similar purposes by defining themes that prompted characters to reflect on one another (§\ref{sec:6.1.1}, §\ref{sec:6.2.2}). Therefore, to further support relational development in ways that differentiate from JOURNALS, future work could expand COMMENTS to include non-verbal interactions. For example, characters could proactively delete or edit their comments, intentionally delay their responses, or passively view other comments multiple times without replying. These open-ended forms of interaction would allow writers to explore dimensions such as hesitation or one-sided emotional investment, which can provide further inspiration when defining and portraying character relationships.

\subsection{Balancing Attention through Multi-Agent Interactions}
\label{sec:7.2}
We introduced Constella, an LLM-based multi-agent tool that supports storywriters’ interconnected character creation process. Our CSI score (§\ref{sec:6.1.2}), along with qualitative findings that illustrate the diverse ways writers used the tool (§\ref{sec:6.2}), collectively suggest that Constella complemented a range of approaches to character creation. Furthermore, one notable finding was that our tool not only supported but also actively reshaped writers’ existing practices. In particular, Constella enabled writers to more evenly distribute their attention and creative effort across the character cast, rather than focusing predominantly on a single central character (§\ref{sec:6.3.1}). We coin this emergent phenomenon the \textit{balancing effect} and use this section to further examine the underlying features that contributed to it, as well as its implications as a design affordance for future storywriting support tools.

Several features of Constella played a critical role in fostering the balancing effect. FRIENDS DISCOVERY surfaced unexpected ties when generating new characters, prompting writers to explore interwoven narratives they had not initially envisioned (§\ref{sec:6.2.1}). We believe the process enabled supporting characters to take on firm and meaningful roles within the narrative, thereby underscoring their potential to shape story dynamics alongside central characters. Then, through JOURNALS, writers received a chorus of perspectives from their characters, encouraging them to shift focus more fluidly across the cast (§\ref{sec:6.2.2}). This process helped further deepen and humanize each character more equally, ultimately reinforcing the notion that every character, regardless of role, has a story worth telling. 
We believe this underscores the ability of multi-agent tools to guide writers towards the creation of narratives in which each character can potentially contribute more meaningfully to the evolving story. 

Thus, we invite future work to explore how multi-agent systems can be \textit{intentionally designed} to surface underdeveloped perspectives and support more balanced forms of storywriting. For example, multi-agent tools might reimagine a completed scene or even the entire story by exploring how the narrative would change if other supporting characters took the lead. Such generative reframings could be particularly valuable in polyphonic stories, which are characterized by multiple voices, perspectives, and distributed character agency. Given that creative practitioners often adapt their tool choices to intentionally accommodate specific ways of thinking and behaviors \cite{10.1145/3527927.3532870}, and that writers increasingly use AI tools to experiment with their creative processes \cite{guo2024pen, ensemblepark2025}, we believe such LLM-MA tools may hold value not only as creative aids that support well-established practices but also as educational or experimental scaffolds for writers seeking to explore more distributed, multi-perspective, non-traditional storytelling structures. 


\subsection{AI Design for Authorial Control and Expression}
\label{sec:7.3}
Our findings indicate that two design aspects of Constella played a critical role in helping writers preserve their creative voice: constraining AI’s generative role and offering intermediary forms of text.

\paragraph*{\textbf{Deliberate Constraints}} To align with the idiosyncratic values of writers, existing discourses have emphasized adaptability as a key principle for co-creative AI systems \cite{10.1145/3613904.3642105, 10.1145/3706598.3713146, 10.1145/3532106.3533506}. Yet, might there also be value in applying deliberate constraints on AI within these tools? Our findings show that these constraints, such as not generating the central character and providing only a minimal set of attributes, can prevent writers from delegating creative decisions entirely to the AI system (§\ref{sec:6.3.2}). These imposed boundaries also helped writers avoid over-reliance on AI and encouraged more intentional and thoughtful decisions. 

Echoing research advocating for meaningful limitations in technology \cite{10.1145/2598510.2598522, 10.1145/2207676.2208540, 10.1145/1394445.1394453}, we believe that carefully applied constraints can enable future writing support tools to create room for writers to exercise their creative agency while still drawing on AI’s generative potential. For example, one could imagine creating a tool that only generates the last sentence of a chapter, prompting writers to build the narrative leading up to that conclusion. Such approaches may also help address what has been described as \textit{“the dearth of the author”} \cite{10.1145/3690712.3690725}, an issue wherein AI systems facilitate large volumes of text with minimal authorial decision-making. Then, how to constrain AI capabilities to help writers maintain authorial control while offering enough meaningful support will be a critical research question to guide the design of future AI-based writing support tools.

\paragraph*{\textbf{Intermediary Materials}} As mentioned in §\ref{sec:6.4.4}, several writers expected our tool to create action sequences or dialogues in output-ready prose. However, writers also found that the format of JOURNALS and COMMENTS required them to actively engage with the generated materials and think critically about how to use the tool’s outputs in their own creative process (§\ref{sec:6.3.2}). 

Based on these findings, we suggest that a key experiential quality of the intermediary materials produced by our tool is its ability to introduce \textit{slowness}. Rather than propelling the narrative forward with polished, ready-made text, these materials encourage writers to pause, reflect, and develop a more profound understanding of their work or engage in active interpretation before deciding whether and how to incorporate the AI-generated content (§\ref{sec:6.2.2}, §\ref{sec:6.2.3}). This aligns with findings from \cite{10.1145/3635636.3656187}, which show that incoherent or grammatically imperfect AI suggestions can prompt reflection and result in more user-generated content.

Together with prior work that advocates for preserving \textit{“creative struggle”} \cite{zhou2023creative} in human-AI co-writing, these insights point toward the value of AI-generated \textit{“intermediate text”} \cite{10.1145/3635636.3656187}, outputs that are intentionally incomplete or ambiguous, as a means of fostering user reflection and adaptation \cite{kreminski2021reflective}. We thus call for future research to further explore alternative forms of AI-based CSTs that do not merely optimize for productivity or output-readiness but instead intentionally support slow, interpretive, creative processes.

\subsection{Applying Metaphors for Designing Interactions in AI-based CSTs}
\label{sec:7.4}

Constella was designed with the social media metaphor. In this section, we examine the broader opportunities and challenges that arise when metaphors are used to structure interactions in AI-based CSTs. 

Recent HCI research has increasingly explored metaphorical design in AI-powered CSTs, investigating how conceptual metaphors shape users’ mental models and interactions. In particular, intelligent writing assistants are frequently framed as \textit{“agents,”} \textit{“tools,” }or \textit{“hybrid”} (combinations of both) \cite{10.1145/3613904.3642697}. These metaphors reflect differing levels of autonomy and user control, with agents seen as collaborators and tools as extensions of user intent. Beyond these broad categories, more specific interaction metaphors have also emerged. For instance, \textit{Patchview} \cite{10.1145/3654777.3676352} utilized the generative dust and magnet metaphor to visualize high-level concepts as magnets and the generated story elements as dust, thereby encoding semantic proximity through spatial interaction. \textit{Toyteller} \cite{10.1145/3706598.3713435} introduced a toy-playing metaphor, allowing users to manipulate symbolic characters like toys in order to influence narrative generation in playful, embodied ways. As such, metaphors can unlock more imaginative approaches to designing AI systems \cite{10.1145/3635636.3664250} and bring forth emotionally resonant user experiences (§\ref{sec:6.1.2}) when drawing from playful domains such as our choice of social media.

However, metaphorical design also introduces new challenges. Anthropomorphic metaphors can inflate user perceptions of AI competence or distort users’ understanding of how AI systems function. For instance, a recent study \cite{jang2023anthropomorphism} has found that presenting agents with anthropomorphic traits can increase perceived AI creativity and possibly lead to further acceptance of AI-generated content. However, this may come at the cost of reduced critical reflection on the system’s actual generative processes. The collaboration metaphor, in particular, has been critiqued for masking the extensive and often invisible human labor embedded in the development of AI systems \cite{10.1145/3544549.3582735}. This concern becomes especially salient when models are trained on datasets drawn from the work of creative practitioners, potentially exacerbating the issue of unacknowledged appropriation of artistic labor without consent or compensation \cite{10.1145/3706598.3713287, 10.1145/3600211.3604681}. 

While prior HCI research has examined the potential of metaphors in interactive system design more broadly, we argue that their role in AI-based CSTs warrants deeper and more critical scrutiny. In this context, metaphors do more than frame interaction; they shape how users understand authorship, agency, and creativity itself. As such, we believe that metaphorical design must be approached not only as a creative opportunity but also as an ethical responsibility and thus call forth further research to examine its implications when used for designing AI systems. 

\subsection{Aligning with Evolving Intent}
\label{sec:7.5}
We found that participants encountered a trade-off when detailing character profiles: they could either invest more time to achieve generations better aligned with their envisioned characters or exert less effort and receive more generic outputs (§\ref{sec:6.4.3}). This trade-off may disrupt creative workflows, as writers are repeatedly forced to specify trivial details they already know instead of immersing themselves in the act of storywriting. 

Prior work \cite{kreminski2024intent} has also pointed out similar issues where AI systems fail to fully understand users’ intent, as users often provide minimal prompts without fully expressing their goals or because users themselves are uncertain about their creative goals during the early stages of creation. Our tool addresses the latter challenge by helping users refine and reflect on their evolving intent by manifesting their specified characters. However, our tool’s heavy reliance on manual inputs and edits to character profiles appears to fall short in addressing the former challenge, mainly because users sometimes found it a burden to actively type in and update character details.

One potential direction to support this process would be to treat AI-generated results as resources for continuously refining character profiles. For example, when users make edits to the generated outputs from JOURNALS and COMMENTS, the system could automatically reflect those changes in the original character profiles. Additionally, a retrieval-augmented generation approach \cite{10.5555/3495724.3496517} could help retain useful details for new outputs, reducing the need for users to specify some aspects in character profiles. These methods can also be applied to the writer’s story, as they may have expressed these character details within their narrative, even if they were not explicitly defined in the system. 

While such AI-assisted mechanisms could reduce manual burden, they also raise important questions about the impact of automation on user creativity. Manual tasks like updating character personas, while seemingly tedious, may often spark reflection or new ideas \cite{10.1145/3491101.3503549}. Writers may not just be inputting data; they may also be reflecting on the character’s arc, emotional consistency, and narrative role. If automation takes over that task, users may skip the opportunity to revisit their ideas, even when they initially feel fully formed or obvious. Future research should therefore explore how to best integrate these mechanisms in ways that preserve opportunities for writers to reflect through their acts of creation. 

