\section{Limitations and Future Work}
\label{sec:8}
\paragraph*{\textbf{Exploring Tool Usage During Entire Full-Length Story Creation}} In our system evaluation study, participants created characters, a backstory, scenes, and a story outline within the context of developing a full-length story. However, these activities may not fully capture the tool’s potential when used throughout the entire process of writing a complete story. This is because writing an entire full-length story is a more prolonged and complex process, during which writers must introduce additional characters and develop their relationships in greater detail. To assess the tool’s long-term value, future work could build on the recommendations in §\ref{sec:6.4.1} and conduct longitudinal studies of entire full-length or even serialized story creation, where participants expect the tool to provide even greater value.

\paragraph*{\textbf{Participant Cultural and Writing Backgrounds}} While we made efforts to recruit writers with diverse backgrounds, the participants in this study do not represent the full spectrum of storywriters. From a cultural standpoint, all participants were based in South Korea, and this shared cultural background may have influenced their perceptions toward AI technologies and character creation. In addition, while our recruitment aimed to include writers from a broad range of story types and genres, writers ultimately chose specific ones during our evaluation study. Future work could thus explore tool usage by focusing on writers from different cultural contexts and examining how the tool supports a broader range of genres and story types. When doing so, incorporating features suggested in §\ref{sec:6.4.2} may further help accommodate their varied writing processes.

\paragraph*{\textbf{Prompt Engineering and Impersonation Quality}} Constella currently uses prompt-based, specifically nonparametric prompting \cite{chen2024persona}, to guide each character’s behavior within a pre-trained large language model. This technique is a common practice for roleplaying agents, offering a flexible and lightweight method for assigning personas without requiring model retraining. However, vanilla prompt engineering is prone to what has been termed the \textit{“overuse problem”} \cite{kim-etal-2024-panda}, wherein the model shows rigid adherence to a character profile and exploits persona attributes, including those that deviate from the given topic. Future work may therefore explore integrating the \textit{PANDA framework} \cite{kim-etal-2024-panda}, which detects and mitigates persona overuse through fine-grained topic mapping and context-aware scoring. Another direction to improve impersonation quality would be to investigate fine-tuning strategies, as exemplified by \textit{CharacterGLM} \cite{zhou-etal-2024-characterglm}, which demonstrated more contextually adaptive character behaviors compared to prompt-based approaches alone.

Nevertheless, even with improved prompting strategies and fine-tuning methods, it is important to acknowledge that LLMs inherently carry biases rooted in their pre-training data \cite{10.1145/3582269.3615599, cheng-etal-2023-marked}. These underlying tendencies may lead to stereotypical, homogenized, and culturally skewed outputs \cite{10.1145/3635636.3656204, 10.1145/3630106.3658993, lucy-bamman-2021-gender, 10.1145/3706598.3713564}, potentially misaligning with the writer’s creative intent. Future efforts to mitigate these issues will be crucial for enabling more inclusive, contextually sensitive, and nuanced character portrayals across a broader range of contexts. 

