	\section{Introduction}
	Systematic Investment Plans (SIPs) have emerged as a cornerstone of retail investment in India's equity markets, offering a structured and disciplined path to long-term wealth creation. Designed to reduce the emotional and cognitive burden of investment decisions, SIPs enable individuals to invest fixed amounts at regular intervals, thereby benefiting from rupee-cost averaging and compounding returns over time. This approach is particularly appealing in India--a rapidly growing economy with a rising middle class seeking accessible wealth-creation tools \cite{WorldBank2021, RBI2022}. Among the available investment avenues, the Nifty 50 Index has emerged as a widely adopted benchmark for systematic investment plans (SIPs) in India. The index comprises 50 large-cap companies across 13 sectors and is considered a proxy for the Indian equity market and the broader economy \cite{nifty_methodology}.
	According to recent data from the Association of Mutual Funds in India (AMFI), as of February 2025, more than 100 million investors participate in SIPs. Among those, 4.456 million are new SIP accounts, further contributing approximately INR 25,999 crore monthly to mutual funds \cite{amfi_feb2024}. This marks a significant rise from early 2021, underscoring the growing popularity of the SIP route among retail investors \cite{amfi_historical}. While SIPs are lauded for their simplicity and disciplined approach to investing, the timing of these investments remains a relatively underexplored aspect. It is commonly observed that many SIPs are scheduled for execution on the first trading day of each month, a practice likely influenced by salary credit cycles and the perception that early-month investments are financially prudent. However, this uniformity in investment timing may inadvertently expose investors to suboptimal market entry points, potentially affected by institutional order flows and other market dynamics.

	Futures and Options (F\&O) expiry days—typically the last Thursday of each month—are characterized by heightened trading activity and market turbulence. Empirical studies indicate that expiry-day volumes surge significantly, with price pressures emerging around the event. Vipul (2005) finds that volumes increase abnormally and prices tend to dip just before expiry, followed by sharp gains the day after, largely due to arbitrage activity and constraints in the cash market \cite{Vipul2005}. These short-term dislocations may offer systematic entry opportunities, particularly for SIPs timed around expiry. While past research--such as the study by \cite{Venkataramani2021}--confirms the long-run outperformance of SIPs over lump-sum strategies, these studies do not evaluate the tactical potential of aligning SIP execution with F\&O expiry cycles. This gap persists in both academic and practitioner literature regarding whether systematic volatility exposure via expiry-day investments can enhance returns without increasing investor burden.

	This study addresses these gaps through a 22-year comparative analysis (2003--2024) of SIP performance under two execution schedules: (i) FTD-SIP (first trading day investments), and (ii) EXP-SIP (F\&O expiry day investments). Using the Nifty 50 index as the benchmark, we assess return differentials across horizons ranging from short-term (1--3 years) to long-term (20 years). Our findings reveal three critical insights:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item \text{Timing matters}--EXP-SIPs outperform FTD-SIPs by 0.5--2.5\% annually in shorter horizons, demonstrating tactical advantages from expiry-day volatility
		\item \text{Performance is timing-sensitive}--Market behavior at entry points dominates the impact of absolute investment amounts
		\item \text{Myths vs. reality}--The widely advertised 12-15\% historical returns in index fund \cite{Financediary2024, StockInsideOut2024, ETMarkets2024}, contrast sharply with empirical 20-year CAGR of 6.7\% (pre-tax), exposing discrepancies in industry narratives.
	\end{enumerate}
	While expiry-day SIPs offer measurable advantages over short horizons, this benefit fades beyond 10–12 years—underscoring that long-term, consistent participation remains the dominant driver of wealth accumulation. The remainder of this paper is structured as follows: Section 2 reviews theoretical and empirical literature on SIP performance and timing. Section 3 outlines the methodology applied to 22 years of Nifty 50 index data. Section 4 presents theoretical and empirical results comparative performance results across different investment durations. Section 5 discusses broader implications and limitations of the findings, and Section 6 concludes the study.
