	\subsubsection{Ten- and Twenty-Year Investment Returns}
	When SIP strategies are evaluated over extended periods of ten and twenty years, the differences between first-day and expiry-day investments become increasingly negligible, underscoring the dominant role of long-term compounding over market entry timing. In the ten-year investment windows—2005–2014 and 2015–2024—the final corpus values and CAGR figures from both strategies converge closely. The first slot shows CAGR values of 6.76\% for the first-day strategy and 6.83\% for the expiry-based one; the second records 6.99\% versus 7.04\%, respectively. In both cases, the differences remain within 0.07 percentage points and are statistically insignificant, as confirmed by high p-values ($=0.068 > 0.05$) in the associated t-tests. These findings emphasize that over a decade-long horizon, the initial variance introduced by timing the SIP installments is gradually averaged out by the overall market trajectory. This convergence becomes even more pronounced in the twenty-year horizon from 2005 to 2024—the longest duration in this analysis.The CAGR difference stands at a minimal 0.03 percentage points (6.65\% vs. 6.68\%), again insignificant. Over this extended timeline, both strategies effectively absorb all short-term market fluctuations, corrections, and recoveries, leading to nearly identical outcomes.

	These results affirm the foundational principle of long-term investing: consistency and discipline outweigh timing precision. While shorter windows may show minor advantages for expiry-based strategies—potentially due to systematic end-of-month market behavior—such advantages diminish to near irrelevance as the investment horizon lengthens. For long-term investors committed to systematic equity exposure, this analysis validates that the SIP execution date need not be optimized. Simply staying invested and allowing the power of compounding to unfold proves to be the most critical factor in wealth creation.


	\subsection{Consolidated Observations}

	The comparative analysis of SIPs initiated on the first trading day versus the F\&O expiry day of each month reveals a nuanced but consistent pattern. Across nearly all years and investment horizons, the expiry-day strategy tends to yield a marginally higher CAGR than the first-day approach. However, this edge is generally modest, with only a few instances—such as in 2003, 2004, 2005, 2013 and 2020—where the difference is both practically large ($>1.5\%$ ) and statistically significant (\textit{p} $< 0.05$).

	Short-term investments (1-year horizon) exhibit the greatest sensitivity to entry timing, likely due to heightened market noise and short-lived momentum or volatility clusters around expiry. For example, in 2003 and 2005, expiry-day SIPs benefited from pronounced dips and recoveries surrounding monthly rollovers, leading to an advantage exceeding 2.5\% in CAGR. However, this timing premium proves unstable—vanishing or even reversing in years of market crisis (e.g., 2008, 2020) or extreme bullishness with smooth trends (e.g., 2009).
	As the investment horizon extends—across 3-year, 5-year, 10-year, and especially 20-year windows—the CAGR difference between the two strategies steadily converges toward zero. This result underscores the principle that the SIP mechanism itself—by distributing entry points across time—acts as a volatility smoother, gradually diluting the impact of any single month's entry price anomaly.
	For long-term, goal-oriented investors, this suggests that entry timing becomes largely irrelevant beyond a certain time horizon. What matters more is disciplined, uninterrupted investing, rather than tactical entry attempts. Nonetheless, for momentum-oriented or expiry-aware traders, the small but repeatable edge observed in certain phases suggests that expiry-based SIPs can serve as a useful heuristic—though this should be contextualized within broader asset allocation and risk management frameworks.

	In essence, both strategies are statistically and practically equivalent in the long run. However, the expiry-based approach may offer a behaviorally aligned advantage for certain investor types, especially those who follow the market calendar closely. The overarching implication remains clear: SIP works best not because of when one invests in a month, but because one invests every month.
