	\section{Discussion}


	\subsection{The Myth of 12\% SIP Returns}

	Our results challenge the commonly cited benchmark of 12-15\% annualized returns from systematic investment plans (SIPs) in index fund \cite{Financediary2024, StockInsideOut2024, ETMarkets2024}—a figure frequently promoted by financial institutions and investment advisors. As detailed in Table \ref{tab:result}, the actual pre-tax SIP CAGR over the full sample period (2005–2024) ranges between 6.65\% and 6.68\%, revealing a significant gap between perception and reality. This divergence arises from three key distortions:

	\text{1. Survivorship and Selective Period Bias} :
	Industry narratives often highlight early high-growth years particularly 2003–2007, when equity markets delivered over 29\% annualized returns—while downplaying subsequent periods of stagnation, volatility, or crisis. This selective reporting parallels the survivorship and hindsight bias discussed in the literature on mutual fund performance illusion
	\cite{Brown1992 ,Wermers2000 }.
	When the full cycle is considered, the compounding effect of subsequent drawdowns sharply dilutes headline figures.

	\text{2. Fee Drag and Expense Ratio Compounding} :
	Even our frictionless CAGR estimates overstate real-world outcomes. Once expense ratios (typically ranging from 1.1\% to 1.8\% annually) are accounted for, the net return to investors declines further. SEBI’s own disclosures suggest the average post-fee CAGR for equity mutual funds in India over the last two decades is closer to 5.2\%, underscoring the cumulative impact of management costs \cite{SEBI2022}.

	\text{3. The Investor Behavior Gap (IRG)} :
	Investor-specific timing errors—such as panic redemptions during drawdowns or chasing returns after rallies—contribute an additional drag. This gap between the fund’s time-weighted return and the investor’s dollar-weighted return has been extensively documented by \cite{Dichev2011}, who estimate an average investor return gap of 0.7–1\% per year. In the context of SIPs, such behavioral frictions manifest as premature discontinuation or misaligned portfolio switches.
	Together, these factors explain why the frequently marketed 12\% figure is more myth than reality. A realistic expectation—net of fees and behaviorally adjusted—would place long-run SIP returns closer to 5–6\%, especially for broad-based index or actively managed equity funds. This has direct implications for financial planning, retirement projections, and public trust in investment advisory practices.

	\subsection{Investor Welfare Considerations}
	The entry timing penalty—while seemingly marginal—scales 
	into a substantial aggregate impact for the Indian mutual fund ecosystem. 
	With approximately 40 million active SIP 
	accounts \cite{amfi_feb2024}, 
	even a 0.19\% annual CAGR delta implies a cumulative wealth erosion of thousands of crore per annum—exceeding several key line items in the Union Budget. This inefficiency stems from three persistent structural asymmetries dominated by institutional investors, algorithmic traders and HNI. Rectifying disparities requires a shift in regulatory and industry design. We propose three interventions:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item Mandating SIP timing flexibility during onboarding, with expiry-day options;
		\item Requiring monthly expiry flow disclosures by AMCs, analogous to FPI flow reports;
		\item Publishing investor dashboards that report real SIP returns adjusted for fees, inflation, and discontinuation risk.
	\end{enumerate}
	Such reforms would enhance transparency, empower investor agency, and reduce the behavioral and structural drag that currently hampers long-term compounding.



	\subsection{Limitations and Research Frontiers}

	While this study offers significant insights into the temporal dynamics of SIP investments, it also opens several avenues for further investigation. First, the extent of sectoral heterogeneity remains unclear—do timing effects differ across industries (such as IT vs. FMCG) or market capitalizations (large-cap vs. mid-cap)? Second, the geographic generalizability of our findings warrants exploration. It is unknown whether similar expiry-related timing advantages exist in developed markets with differing microstructure dynamics, such as the U.S. or European Union. Finally, an important practical question remains under active exploration by the author: what is the optimal pattern of incremental monthly SIP contributions (e.g., fixed vs. progressive increase) that maximizes long-term compounded returns?

	Addressing these frontiers will likely require richer datasets—such as panel-level investment flows and investor demographics—as well as interdisciplinary approaches combining econometrics, behavioral finance, and simulation modeling. Insights from such research could inform more personalized SIP structures, smarter default options, and regulatory innovations to enhance investor welfare.



	\section{Conclusion}
	This paper provides a comprehensive analysis of SIP timing strategies in India’s Nifty 50 index, revealing three core insights with broad theoretical and practical implications. First, we find statistically significant short- to medium-term advantages for SIPs executed on F\&O expiry days, which outperform first-day investments by an average annual excess return of 0.58\% (\(\sigma = 1.12\%\), \(p < 0.05\)). This outperformance appears linked to structural liquidity distortions, driven by institutional hedging, month-end rebalancing, and predictable retail flow patterns.
	Second, our results directly challenge the widely circulated claim of 12\% SIP returns. The actual pre-tax CAGR over the 2005–2024 period lies between 6.65\% and 6.68\%, with the gap explained by survivorship bias (~3.2 percentage points), compounded expense ratios (~1.1–1.8\%), and a behavioral investor return gap (~0.7\%). These findings expose a critical disconnect between investor expectations and realized outcomes, emphasizing the need for greater transparency in return reporting.

	In response, we propose targeted regulatory reforms. SEBI should consider mandating expiry-day SIP options across all mutual fund platforms, implementing real-return dashboards that adjust for inflation and costs, and requiring rolling-period return disclosures to avoid misleading point estimates. For investors, expiry-day SIPs offer a simple, cost-free enhancement for short- to medium-term goals, while conventional strategies remain suitable for long-term wealth creation.

	Future research should investigate timing effectiveness across sectors and market caps, explore demographic or behavioral drivers of suboptimal SIP execution, and assess the generalizability of these patterns in other emerging and developed markets. 
	Ultimately, while market microstructure offers fleeting advantages to the informed, sustained long-term participation remains the most reliable and powerful strategy—an enduring truth that transcends any tactical timing edge.

