In certain instances, rather than directly handling the data, this threat manipulates it by segmenting it into blocks (Fig. \ref{fig:35}, Fig. \ref{fig:34} and Fig. \ref{fig:36}), each with a maximum size of \textit{65'535} bits. This approach is likely employed to evade network detection. For example, when exfiltrating logs (as illustrated in Figure 34), the threat constructs a corresponding \textit{BlockManager} for each file within the Logs folder. This method of data management aids in obfuscating the exfiltration process and avoiding detection by distributing the data across multiple smaller segments. 
The \textit{BlockManager} functions as a supervisor, overseeing the segmentation and proper mapping of each data block. For each segment into which a file is divided, an instance of the \textit{baseBlock} class is created and initialized with detailed information about the block. This includes the filename, the block's byte data, its index, any operational errors encountered, and the overall length of the file.

