\subsection{Gravitational Aharonov-Bohm effect and $\mathbb{Z}_2$  topology}\label{subsec:z2 gravitational berry phase}

Using the formal correspondence established in the previous section, let us evaluate the gravitational effect on the fermion system and investigate the origin of the $\mathbb{Z}_2$ topology of the time-reversal invariant vortex.
%we show that the origin of the $\mathbb{Z}_2$ topology of the time-reversal invariant vortex is the $\pi$-flux of the Rimeann curvature tensor.


Let us compute the curvature tensor. Since the $\Sigma_{12}$ component is the only nonzero contribution to the spin connection,  
the curvature tensor [Eq.~\eqref{eq:field strength}] is computed as 
\begin{align}
    R_{12}^{12}&=\frac{1}{2!}(\partial_1\omega_2^{12}-\partial_2\omega_1^{12})\notag\\
    &=n\pi\delta(\bm{r}),
\end{align}
where we have used $[\Omega_\mu,\Omega_\nu]=0$, and $\delta(\vb{r})$ is the Dirac delta function.
There is a $n\pi$-flux of the curvature tensor at the vortex core, although it is zero everywhere except at this point.
The behavior corresponds to the fact that the metric is singular at the vortex core and is locally flat at all other locations.

Nevertheless $R_{12}^{12}=0$ except at the origin, the fermion field at $r\neq 0$ receives a nontrivial gravitational contribution, that is nothing but the gravitational AB effect.
One can show this by integrating the spin connection $\Omega_\mu$ along a circle with radius $R$, giving
\begin{equation}
    \oint_{r=R} \Omega_\mu dx^\mu
    =\int_{r<R} d^2x R^{ab}_{12}\Sigma_{ab}  
    =-i n\pi(\sigma_3\otimes\sigma_3),
\end{equation}
where we used Stokes theorem and $\Sigma_{12}=-i/2(\sigma_3\otimes\sigma_3)$.
The obtained gravitatiional AB phase becomes nontrivial only when $n$ is an odd integer, representing the $\mathbb{Z}_2$ topology.


%One can show this by integrating the curvature on a disk with radius $R$. Noting that the only $SO(2)$ part, which is Abelian, is nonzero, we can use the Stoke's theorem to obtain
