\section{Basics of time-reversal invariant topological superconductors}\label{sec:review TSC}
In this section, we review the mean field description of topological superconductors. 

The two dimensional time-reversal invariant topological superconductors are described by the mean field Hamiltonain $H_\mathrm{BdG}=1/2\sum_{k}\psi_k^\dagger H_\mathrm{BdG}(\bm{k})\psi_k$, where $\psi_k=(c_k,c_{-k}^\dagger)^t$ is the Nambu spinor and $H_\mathrm{BdG}(\bm{k})$ is the BdG Hamiltonian in $\bm{k}$-space, giving
\begin{equation}
    H_\mathrm{BdG}(\bm{k})=
    \begin{pmatrix}
        -\mu & \Delta(k)\\
        \Delta^\dagger(k) & +\mu\\
    \end{pmatrix}.\label{eq:H(k)}
\end{equation}
Here $h(\bm{k})$ represents the normal Hamiltonian, and 
\begin{equation}
    \Delta(k)=\frac{1}{k_\mathrm{F}}\Delta_{a}^{~\mu}k_\mu\sigma^a(-i\sigma_2),\label{eq:delta_k}
\end{equation}
describes $p$-wave paring superconducting order ($a,\mu=1,2$). 
The characteristic feature of time-reversal invariant topological superconductors is that the order parameter $\Delta_a^{~\mu}=\Delta_0\delta_a^{~\mu}$ is real due to time reversal symmetry.
Here $\Delta_0$ represents the magnitude of the bulk order parameter, and $k_\mathrm{F}$ is a Fermi wave vector.

We summarize the symmetry of the system. 
The system has both the particle-hole symmetry (PHS) and the time reversal symmetry (TRS). They are expressed as 
\begin{equation}
    CH_\mathrm{BdG}(k)C^{-1}=-H_\mathrm{BdG}(-k),~TH_\mathrm{BdG}(k)T^{-1}=H_\mathrm{BdG}(-k),
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
    C=(\sigma_1\otimes1)K,~T=(1\otimes i\sigma_2)K.
\end{equation}
Here $K$ represents the operator that performs complex conjugation.

The BdG equation can be mapped to the Dirac equation in the $2+1$-dimensional fermion system as follows.
In the following, we set $k_\mathrm{F}=1$ and $\Delta_0=1$ for simplicity.
We define the gamma matrices as 
\begin{equation}
    \gamma^0=\sigma_3\otimes1,~\gamma^1=i\sigma_2\otimes\sigma_3,~\gamma^2=-i\sigma_1\otimes1.\label{eq:gamma matrices}
\end{equation}
They satisfy the Clliford algebra $\pb{\gamma^a}{\gamma^b}=2\eta^{ab}$, where $\eta_{ab}=\mathrm{diag}(+,-,-)$. 
The BdG Hamiltonian [Eq.~\eqref{eq:H(k)}] is reduced to the Dirac Hamiltonian
\begin{equation}
    H_\mathrm{BdG}=-\gamma^0\qty(\mu+\sum_{\mu=1,2}i\gamma^\mu\partial_\mu).
\end{equation} 
Then, the Schrodinger equation $i\partial_t\psi=H\psi$ reproduces the Dirac equation 
\begin{equation}
    (i\sum_{\mu=0}^2 \gamma^\mu\partial_\mu+m)\psi=0.
\end{equation}

