Panel A in Table \ref{tab:sharp_null} summarizes the available information on potential outcomes and missingness indicators, based on the observed data and the sharp null hypothesis in \eqref{eq:null_fisher_sharp}. From the table, we do not know the value of $Y_{i}^{\star}(0)$ for units whose outcomes are missing.
Intuitively, $t_{\R, \phi}(\bs{Z}, \bs{Y}^\sta(0))$ in Example \ref{eg:test_outcome_only} achieves its minimum value when the potential outcomes $Y_i(0)$ for treated units are as small as possible, while those for control units are as large as possible.
This motivates us to consider the following control potential outcome vector:
$\bs{Y}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}}^{\gen}(0) = (Y_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, 1}^{\gen}(0), \ldots, Y_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, n}^{\gen}(0))^\top,$
where the superscript $\gen$ indicates the general missingness mechanism, the subscript $\bs{\delta}$ represents the sharp null of interest in \eqref{eq:null_fisher_sharp}, and the subscript $\bs{Z}$ emphasizes its dependence on the observed treatment assignment, and
\begin{align}\label{eq:worst_gen}
    Y_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, i}^{\gen}(0) = \begin{cases}
        Y_{i} - \delta_{i} &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 1$ and $M_{i} = 1$}, \\
        -\infty &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 1$ and $M_{i} = 0$}, \\
        Y_{i} &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 0$ and $M_{i} = 1$}, \\
        \infty &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 0$ and $M_{i} = 0$}, \\
    \end{cases}
    \qquad \qquad
    (1\le i \le n). 
\end{align}
Obviously, $\bs{Y}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}}^{\gen}(0)$ is a ``feasible'' imputed value of $\bs{Y}(0)$ based on the observed data and the sharp null hypothesis. 
The theorem below shows that $\bs{Y}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}}^{\gen}(0)$ indeed yields the ``worst-case'' configuration of the control potential outcomes, leading to a valid p-value for the sharp null hypothesis $H_{\bs{\delta}}$.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:sharp_null_gen}
\rm 
Under the CRE and Assumption \ref{asmp:general}, 
a valid p-value for testing the sharp null hypothesis $H_{\bs{\delta}}$ in \eqref{eq:null_fisher_sharp} is 
\begin{align}\label{eq:p_val_sharp_null_orig}
    p_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}}^\gen  \coloneqq G_{\R, \phi}\left( t_{\R, \phi}\left(\bs{Z}, \bs{Y}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}}^{\gen}(0) \right) \right),
\end{align}
where $t_{\R, \phi}$ is defined in either \eqref{eq:test_stat_R_phi} or \eqref{eq:test_stat_mann_whitney}, $G_{\R, \phi}$ is defined in \eqref{eq:G_R_phi}, and $\bs{Y}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}}^{\gen}(0)$ is defined  in \eqref{eq:worst_gen}. That is, under $H_{\bs{\delta}}$, $\mathbb{P}\left( p_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}}^\gen \leq \alpha \right) \leq \alpha$ for any $\alpha \in (0, 1)$.
\end{theorem}

Our worst-case inferential procedure in Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp_null_gen} has a similar flavor with the worst-case identification approach in the partial identification literature \citep{robins1989probability, robins1989estimability, manski1990nonparametric, horowitz2000nonparametric}. This approach assumes that 
(i) the missing values in the treated group are at the lower bound of the support of the outcome and (ii) the missing values in the control group are at the upper bound of the support of the outcome. 
This can then give us a partially identified set for the parameter of interest, such as the average treatment effect. Although we are studying a conceptually different problem, namely, inference for sharp null hypothesis, the worst-case scenario for the p-value nevertheless coincides with the worst-case identification problem.
Moreover, our approach has the advantage that it can still give informative inference results even when the support of the outcome is unbounded. 

We then consider testing the sharp null $H_{\bs{\delta}}$ in \eqref{eq:null_fisher_sharp} using the test statistic 
$t(\bs{Z}, \tilde{\bs{Y}}_b^{\sta}(0))$
in Example \ref{eg:test_outcome_miss}. 
Similar to before, we use a distribution-free statistic in \eqref{eq:test_stat_R_phi} or \eqref{eq:test_stat_mann_whitney}, and we seek to minimize the realized value of the test statistic $t_{\R, \phi}(\bs{Z}, \tilde{\bs{Y}}_b^{\sta}(0))$. 
Panel A in Table \ref{tab:sharp_null} also summarizes the information on the composite potential outcomes based on the observed data and the null hypothesis on treatment effects.
From Panel A in Table \ref{tab:sharp_null}, 
we do not know the composite potential outcomes of the treated units. 
In particular, the composite potential outcome of each treated unit whose outcome is observed can take only two values (either $b$ or $Y_{i} - \delta_i$), while that of each treated unit with missing outcome can take any value on the whole real line. 
By the same logic as Theorem \ref{thm:sharp_null_gen}, 
we want the values of $\tilde{Y}_{b,i}^{\sta}(0)$ to be as small as possible for treated units.  
This motivates us to consider the following ``feasible'' values for the composite potential outcome vector:
$\tilde{\bs{Y}}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, b}^{\gen}(0) = (\tilde{Y}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, b, 1}^{\gen}(0), \ldots, \tilde{Y}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, b, n}^{\gen}(0))^\top$ with 
\begin{align}\label{eq:worst_gen_YM}
    \tilde{Y}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, b, i}^{\gen}(0)
    & 
    = \begin{cases}
        \min\{Y_{i} - \delta_i, b\} &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 1$ and $M_{i} = 1$}, \\
        -\infty &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 1$ and $M_{i} = 0$}, \\
        Y_{i} &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 0$ and $M_{i} = 1$}, \\
        b &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 0$ and $M_{i} = 0$}, \\
    \end{cases}
    \qquad \qquad
    (1\le i \le n). 
\end{align}
The theorem below shows that $ \tilde{\bs{Y}}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, b}^{\gen}(0)$ indeed yields the worst-case configuration of the composite potential outcomes, leading to a valid p-value for testing the sharp null hypothesis $H_{\bs{\delta}}$. 

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:sharp_null_gen_comp}
\rm 
Under the CRE and Assumption \ref{asmp:general}, 
a valid p-value for testing the sharp null hypothesis $H_{\bs{\delta}}$ in \eqref{eq:null_fisher_sharp} is 
\begin{align}\label{eq:p_val_sharp_null_comp_gen}
    \tilde{p}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, b}^\gen  \coloneqq G_{\R, \phi}\left( t_{\R, \phi}\left(\bs{Z}, \tilde{\bs{Y}}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, b}^{\gen}(0)  \right) \right),
\end{align}
where $t_{\R, \phi}$ is defined in either \eqref{eq:test_stat_R_phi} or \eqref{eq:test_stat_mann_whitney}, 
$G_{\R, \phi}$ is defined in
\eqref{eq:G_R_phi}, 
and $\tilde{\bs{Y}}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, b}^{\gen}(0)$ is defined in \eqref{eq:worst_gen_YM}. 
That is, under $H_{\bs{\delta}}$, $\mathbb{P}\left( \tilde{p}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, b}^\gen \leq \alpha \right) \leq \alpha$ for any $\alpha \in (0, 1)$.
\end{theorem}

The p-value in Theorem \ref{thm:sharp_null_gen_comp} is valid for any pre-specified constant $b$ in the construction of composite potential outcomes. 
We now discuss how the value of $b$ affects the power of the test in Theorem \ref{thm:sharp_null_gen_comp}. 
Given the form of the rank-based test statistic, 
the p-value in \eqref{eq:p_val_sharp_null_comp_gen} depends crucially on the relative magnitude of the worst-case configuration $\tilde{\bs{Y}}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, b}^{\gen}(0)$ for treated and control units. 
Let $i$ and $i'$ be two treated units with observed and missing outcomes, respectively, i.e., $Z_i=Z_{i'}=1$, $M_i=1$ and $M_{i'}=0$, 
and let $j$ and $j'$ be two control units with observed and missing outcomes, respectively, i.e.,  $Z_j=Z_{j'}=0$, $M_j=1$ and $M_{j'}=0$. 
From the construction in 
\eqref{eq:worst_gen_YM},
$\tilde{Y}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, b, i'}^{\gen}(0) = -\infty$ must be less than or equal to both $\tilde{Y}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, b, j}^{\gen}(0)$ and $\tilde{Y}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, b, j'}^{\gen}(0)$ for the control units.  
In addition, $\tilde{Y}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, b, i}^{\gen}(0) = \min\{Y_i -\delta_i, b\}$ is always less than or equal to $\tilde{Y}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, b, j'}^{\gen}(0) = b$, and it is more likely to be less than $\tilde{Y}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, b, j}^{\gen}(0) = Y_j$ as $b$ decreases. 
From the above and ignoring ties, as $b$ decreases, the coordinates of $\tilde{\bs{Y}}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, b}^{\gen}(0)$ for treated units are more likely to be smaller than those for control units. 
Therefore, to ``maximize'' the test statistic value under the worst-case configuration, or, equivalently, to ``minimize'' the p-value, we suggest choosing $b$ as large as possible, i.e., $b=\infty$. 

Panel A of Table \ref{tab:sharp_null} summarizes the worst-case configurations of both the original control potential outcomes and the composite potential outcomes for any fixed $b \in \mathbb{R}$, including the suggested choice $b = \infty$.
When $b = \infty$, the p-value in \eqref{eq:p_val_sharp_null_comp_gen} coincides with that in \eqref{eq:p_val_sharp_null_orig}, since $\tilde{\bs{Y}}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, b}^{\gen}(0)$ reduces to $\bs{Y}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}}^{\gen}(0)$.
Accordingly, Theorem \ref{thm:sharp_null_gen} is a special case of Theorem \ref{thm:sharp_null_gen_comp} with $b = \infty$, and both yield our proposed p-value for testing the sharp null hypothesis.
Although the test statistic in Example \ref{eg:test_outcome_miss} offers no advantage over that in Example \ref{eg:test_outcome_only} under general missingness, it can be useful under specific missingness mechanisms, as discussed in the following subsection.

\subsection{Monotone missingness mechanisms}\label{sec:monotone_sharp_null}

\subsubsection{Randomization tests for sharp null under monotone missingness mechanisms}

We now consider testing the sharp null hypothesis in \eqref{eq:null_fisher_sharp} under the missingness mechanisms that the treatment has a monotone effect on the missingness indicator. 
We consider two cases as shown in Assumptions \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0} and \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_le_M0}, depending on whether the treatment effect on the missingness indicator is non-negative or non-positive. 
Similar to Section \ref{sec:sharp_null_gen}, we consider the two forms of test statistics in Examples \ref{eg:test_outcome_only} and \ref{eg:test_outcome_miss} which involve either the original or composite potential outcomes, 
and utilize the distribution-free rank statistics defined in \eqref{eq:test_stat_R_phi} and \eqref{eq:test_stat_mann_whitney}. 
By similar logic, we need to find the worst-case configuration of the original or composite potential outcomes, based on the observed data, the sharp null $H_{\bs{\delta}}$, and the monotone missingness mechanisms invoked here.

We first consider testing the sharp null hypothesis $H_{\bs{\delta}}$ in \eqref{eq:null_fisher_sharp} using the test statistic $t_{\R, \phi}(\bs{Z}, \bs{Y}^\sta(0))$ from Example \ref{eg:test_outcome_only} and \eqref{eq:test_stat_R_phi} or \eqref{eq:test_stat_mann_whitney}. Panels B and C of Table \ref{tab:sharp_null} summarize the information on control potential outcomes implied by the observed data, the sharp null $H_{\bs{\delta}}$, and Assumptions \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0} or \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_le_M0}. This information is the same as that in Panel A under general missingness, where $Y^\sta_i(0)$ is known if and only if unit $i$’s outcome is observed. As a result, the worst-case configuration of $\bs{Y}^\sta(0)$ remains as in \eqref{eq:worst_gen}, and the resulting p-value is the same as that in Theorem \ref{thm:sharp_null_gen}. Thus, the monotone missingness assumptions do not improve the power of tests based on $t_{\R, \phi}(\bs{Z}, \bs{Y}^\sta(0))$.

We next consider the test statistic $t_{\R, \phi}(\bs{Z}, \tilde{\bs{Y}}_b^\sta(0))$ from Example \ref{eg:test_outcome_miss} and \eqref{eq:test_stat_R_phi} or \eqref{eq:test_stat_mann_whitney}. Panels B and C of Table \ref{tab:sharp_null} summarize the available information on the composite potential outcomes implied by the observed data, the sharp null $H_{\bs{\delta}}$, and Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0} or \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_le_M0}. Compared to Panel A under Assumption \ref{asmp:general}, we gain additional information by imputing more missingness indicators $M_i(0)$s for treated units based on the monotone missingness assumptions. Specifically, under Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0}, $M_i(0) = 0$ for treated units with missing outcomes, since $M_i(0) \le M_i(1) = M_i = 0$; under Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_le_M0}, $M_i(0) = 1$ for treated units with observed outcomes, since $M_i(0) \ge M_i(1) = M_i = 1$. Still, the full composite potential outcomes remain unknown, so we identify their worst-case configuration to ensure validity of the randomization test. As in Section \ref{sec:sharp_null_gen}, we aim to minimize treated units' composite outcomes and maximize those of control units.

These motivate us to consider the following ``feasible'' configurations of the composite potential outcomes under Assumptions \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0} and \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_le_M0}, respectively, which are also summarized in Panels B and C of Table \ref{tab:sharp_null}. Under Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0}, we define $\tilde{\bs{Y}}^{\monp}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, b}(0) = (\tilde{Y}^{\monp}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, b, 1}(0), \tilde{Y}^{\monp}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, b, 2}(0), \ldots, \tilde{Y}^{\monp}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, b, n}(0))^\top$, with the superscript indicating that the treatment has a monotone positive (more precisely, nonnegative) effect on the missingness indicator: 
\begin{align}\label{eq:worst_YM_mon_p}
    \tilde{Y}^{\monp}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, b, i}(0)
    & 
    = \begin{cases}
        \min\{Y_{i} - \delta_i, b\} &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 1$ and $M_{i} = 1$}, \\
        b &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 1$ and $M_{i} = 0$}, \\
        Y_{i} &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 0$ and $M_{i} = 1$}, \\
        b &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 0$ and $M_{i} = 0$}, \\
    \end{cases}
    \qquad \qquad
    (1\le i \le n). 
\end{align}
Under Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_le_M0}, we define $\tilde{\bs{Y}}^{\monn}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta},b}(0) = (\tilde{Y}^{\monn}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta},b, 1}(0), \tilde{Y}^{\monn}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta},b, 2}(0), \ldots, \tilde{Y}^{\monn}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta},b, n}(0))^\top$, with the superscript indicating that the treatment has a monotone negative (more precisely, nonpositive) effect on the missingness indicator:
\begin{align}\label{eq:worst_YM_mon_n}
    \tilde{Y}^{\monn}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta},b, i}(0) 
    & 
    = \begin{cases}
        Y_{i} - \delta_i &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 1$ and $M_{i} = 1$}, \\
        -\infty &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 1$ and $M_{i} = 0$}, \\
        Y_{i} &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 0$ and $M_{i} = 1$}, \\
        b &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 0$ and $M_{i} = 0$}, \\
    \end{cases}
    \qquad \qquad
    (1\le i \le n). 
\end{align}
The following theorem shows that \eqref{eq:worst_YM_mon_p} and \eqref{eq:worst_YM_mon_n} indeed give the worst-case configurations under Assumptions \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0} and \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_le_M0}, respectively, and thereby lead to valid p-values for testing the sharp null hypothesis.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:sharp_null_mon}
\rm 
Under the CRE and Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0} or \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_le_M0}, 
a valid p-value for testing the sharp null hypothesis $H_{\bs{\delta}}$ in \eqref{eq:null_fisher_sharp} is 
\begin{align}\label{eq:p_val_sharp_null_test_stat_outcome_miss_mono}
    \tilde{p}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta},b}^{\mon \square}  \coloneqq G_{\R, \phi}\left( t_{\R, \phi}(\bs{Z}, \tilde{\bs{Y}}^{\mon \square}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta},b}(0)  )\right)
    \text{ with } 
    \square = 
    \begin{cases}
        \text{p} & \text{under Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0}},\\
        \text{n} & \text{under Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_le_M0}},
    \end{cases}
\end{align}
where $t_{\R, \phi}$ is defined in either \eqref{eq:test_stat_R_phi} or \eqref{eq:test_stat_mann_whitney}, $G_{\R, \phi}$ is defined in \eqref{eq:G_R_phi}, and $\tilde{\bs{Y}}^{\monp}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta},b}(0)$ and $\tilde{\bs{Y}}^{\monn}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta},b}(0)$ are defined in \eqref{eq:worst_YM_mon_p} and \eqref{eq:worst_YM_mon_n}.
\end{theorem}

We now discuss the choice of $b$, which is important for the power of the test.
Consider the worst-case configuration of the composite potential outcomes in \eqref{eq:worst_YM_mon_p} under Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0}. In this case, the composite outcomes of control units with observed outcomes do not depend on $b$. Moreover, regardless of the value of $b$, the composite outcomes of treated units are always less than or equal to those of control units with missing outcomes (which are $b$). As $b$ increases, however, treated units become more likely to exceed the outcomes of observed control units. Therefore, we recommend choosing $b$ as large as possible in Theorem \ref{thm:sharp_null_mon} with $\square = \text{p}$; that is, using the p-value $\tilde{p}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta},b}^{\monp}$ in \eqref{eq:p_val_sharp_null_test_stat_outcome_miss_mono} with $b = \infty$.

We then consider Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_le_M0}, where the worst-case configuration of the composite potential outcomes is shown in \eqref{eq:worst_YM_mon_n}. In this case, the composite outcomes of treated units are more likely to exceed those of control units as $b$ decreases. Therefore, we recommend choosing $b$ as small as possible in Theorem \ref{thm:sharp_null_mon} with $\square = \text{n}$; that is, using the p-value $\tilde{p}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta},b}^{\monn}$ in \eqref{eq:p_val_sharp_null_test_stat_outcome_miss_mono} with $b = -\infty$.

Because of the effect increasing property of the rank-sum statistics in \eqref{eq:test_stat_R_phi} and \eqref{eq:test_stat_mann_whitney}, we can show that the p-value in \eqref{eq:p_val_sharp_null_test_stat_outcome_miss_mono} from Theorem \ref{thm:sharp_null_mon} under either Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0} or \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_le_M0} is smaller than or equal to $\tilde{p}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, b}^\gen$ under the general missing mechanism assumption. This holds for any prespecified value of $b$, as well as for the values of $b$ suggested by the paper. This can be seen from the last two columns of Table \ref{tab:sharp_null}. In other words, Theorem \ref{thm:sharp_null_mon} based on the composite potential outcomes yields more powerful p-values by using the monotonicity in the missingness mechanism.

\begin{remark}\label{rmk:sharp_miss}
    In the special case of sharp missingness, where $M_i(1) = M_i(0)$ for all $i$, all composite potential outcomes can be imputed from the observed data, the sharp null hypothesis, and the sharp missingness assumption. In addition, we can exclude units with missing outcomes and perform randomization tests using only the observed outcomes--analogous to the approach in Theorem~\ref{thm:mar_miss}. This also avoids the need to specify the value of $b$ in the composite potential outcomes.
\end{remark}

\subsubsection{A two-step procedure that can further improve the power of the test}\label{sec:two_step_sharp}

We can further improve the power of the test in Theorem \ref{thm:sharp_null_mon} by using information about the distribution of potential missingness. To fix ideas, suppose Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0} holds and we use the imputed composite control potential outcomes with the suggested value of $b$ (i.e., $+ \infty$) in \eqref{eq:worst_YM_mon_p}. In this case, we trim the imputed composite control potential outcomes for units that are both observed and treated so that they are equal to their imputed control potential outcomes under $H_{\bs{\delta}}$. In other words, we are considering the worst-case potential missingness for these units by assuming that all of their $M_{i}(0)$ values are 1. However, this approach may be unnecessarily conservative. We have some information about the proportion of units for whom $M_{i}(0) = 0$, because we can construct a lower confidence limit for this proportion using the observed missingness indicators in the control group. This, in turn, provides information about the proportion of treated units with observed outcomes who would not be observed under control. By leveraging this information, we can improve the worst-case imputation under Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0} and $H_{\bs{\delta}}$.

The above observations suggest a two-step procedure for testing $H_{\bs{\delta}}$ under Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0}. The first step constructs a lower confidence limit for the proportion of units for whom $M_{i}(0) = 0$ at a small significance level $\beta \in (0, \alpha)$. The second step then uses this lower confidence limit to provide information about the worst-case imputation for the composite control potential outcomes. Importantly, our framework accounts for the possibility that the true proportion of units for whom $M_{i}(0) = 0$ may not lie within the confidence interval, using a Bonferroni-type correction. The procedure is formally stated below.
\begin{algorithm}\label{alg:mp_two_step_sharp}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[]
    \item For $\beta \in (0, \alpha)$, construct a random set $[0, \hat{M}]$ such that $\P\left( 0 \leq \sum_{i = 1}^{n} M_{i}(0) \leq \hat{M} \right) \geq 1 - \beta$, following, for example, the method in \cite{Wang:2015}. It can be shown that, with probability at least $1-\beta$, the number of treated units with observed outcomes who would be missing under control is at least $\underline{m}\coloneqq n_{11} + n_{01} - \hat{M}$, where $n_{11} = \sum_{i = 1}^{n} Z_i M_i$ and $n_{01} = \sum_{i = 1}^{n} (1 - Z_i) M_i$ denote the numbers of treated and control units with observed outcome    
    \item For each unit $i$ in the treated group whose outcomes are observed, compute $A_{i}$, $B_{i}$ and $D_{i}$ by:
    \begin{align*}
    A_i &\coloneqq \sum_{j:Z_j=0} \psi_{i,j}( \infty, Y_j M_j +  \infty (1-M_j) ), \\
    B_i &\coloneqq \sum_{j:Z_j=0} \psi_{i,j}( Y_i - 
    \delta_i
    , Y_j M_j +  \infty (1-M_j) ), \\
    D_i &\coloneqq \phi(A_{i}) - \phi(B_{i}).
    \end{align*}    
    \item Let $\{i: Z_i=M_i=1, 1\le i \le n\} = \{l_1, l_2, \ldots, l_{n_{11}}\}$ such that $D_{l_1} \le D_{l_2} \le \ldots \le D_{l_{n_{11}}}$.
    \item Impute the composite control potential outcomes by: for $1\le i \le n$,
    \begin{align}\label{eq:wc_mp_twostep}
    \tilde{Y}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, \infty, i}^{\monp, \underline{m}}(0)
    & 
    = \begin{cases}
        \infty &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 1$, $M_{i} = 1$ and $i \in \{l_1, \ldots, l_{\underline{m}}\}$}, \\
        Y_{i} - \delta_i &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 1$, $M_{i} = 1$ and $i \notin \{l_1, \ldots, l_{\underline{m}}\}$}, \\
        \infty &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 1$ and $M_{i} = 0$}, \\
        Y_{i} &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 0$ and $M_{i} = 1$}, \\
        \infty &\quad \text{if $Z_{i} = 0$ and $M_{i} = 0$}. \\
    \end{cases}
    \end{align}
    \item Construct a p-value by:
    \begin{align}\label{eq:mp_two_step_sharp_p_val}
        \tilde{p}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, +\infty}^{\monp, \text{two-step}} \coloneqq G_{\R, \phi}\left( t_{\R, \phi}\left(\bs{Z}, \tilde{\bs{Y}}^{\monp, \underline{m}}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, \infty}(0)  \right)\right) + \beta,
    \end{align}
    where $\tilde{\bs{Y}}^{\monp, \underline{m}}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, \infty}(0) \coloneqq (\tilde{Y}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, \infty, 1}^{\monp, \underline{m}}(0), \tilde{Y}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, \infty, 2}^{\monp, \underline{m}}(0), \ldots, \tilde{Y}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, \infty, n}^{\monp, \underline{m}}(0))^\top$, $t_{\R, \phi}$ is defined in \eqref{eq:test_stat_mann_whitney}, and $G_{\R, \phi}$ is defined in \eqref{eq:G_R_phi}.
    \item If $\tilde{p}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, +\infty}^{\monp, \text{two-step}} \leq \alpha$, reject $H_{\bs{\delta}}$; otherwise, do not reject.
\end{enumerate}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:sharp_null_mon_two_step}
\rm Under the CRE and Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0}, Algorithm \ref{alg:mp_two_step_sharp} yields a valid p-value. That is, under $H_{\bs{\delta}}$, $\P\left( \tilde{p}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, +\infty}^{\monp, \text{two-step}} \leq \alpha \right) \leq \alpha$ for any $\alpha \in (0, 1)$.
\end{theorem}

In the two-step procedure, we consider only the Mann–Whitney-type U-statistics in \eqref{eq:test_stat_mann_whitney}, excluding the rank-sum statistics in \eqref{eq:test_stat_R_phi} for computational reasons. The worst-case configuration under the statistic in \eqref{eq:test_stat_R_phi} may not admit a simple closed-form solution.

There are two similarities between the Zhang-Rubin-Lee bounds (see \cite{zhang2003estimation} and \cite{lee2009training}) and our inference procedure. 
First, neither approach requires an exclusion restriction in Heckman correction \citep{heckman1979sample}.
Second, the worst-case imputation in \eqref{eq:wc_mp_twostep} under the two-step procedure is also connected to the Zhang--Rubin--Lee bounds (see \cite{zhang2003estimation} and \cite{lee2009training}). To illustrate the idea, we ignore the uncertainty from the first step and assume that we know the number of treated units with observed outcomes who would be missing under control, denoted again by $\underline{m}$ (with a slight abuse of notation).
Due to randomization, we expect the proportion of $\infty$ in \eqref{eq:wc_mp_twostep}---equivalently, the proportion of units with $M_i(0) = 0$---to be about the same in the treated and control groups. Recall the definitions of $n_{11}$ and $n_{01}$ in Algorithm~\ref{alg:mp_two_step_sharp}. In the randomization test and ignoring ties, we are essentially comparing the $(n_{11} - \underline{m})/n_{11}$ fraction of the observed treated units with the smallest outcomes to the observed control units.
This comparison mirrors that used in the Zhang--Rubin--Lee lower bound on the average treatment effect among units whose outcomes would be observed under both treatment and control. Specifically, $n_{11} - \underline{m}$ represents the number of treated units with $M_i(0) = 1$, which is approximately $n_1 \cdot n_{01} / n_0$. This implies that the fraction being compared is roughly $(n_{01} / n_0) / (n_{11} / n_1)$, which matches the expression in \citet[Table 6, Row 2]{zhang2003estimation} and \citet[Proposition 1a]{lee2009training}.

\begin{remark}\label{rmk:mn_twostep}
    In the supplementary material, 
    we also discuss the two-step procedure under the monotone missingness in Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_le_M0}, where the improvement is less substantial compared to that under Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0}. 
    Therefore, from Remark \ref{rmk:form_test_stat}, when Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_le_M0} holds, we suggest to switch treatment labels, change outcome signs and perform the two-step approach in Theorem \ref{thm:sharp_null_mon_two_step}; this is equivalent to use test statistics based on treatment potential outcomes and missingness.    
\end{remark}

\subsection{Missing at random mechanisms}\label{sec:mar_sharp_null}

We now consider the missing at random assumption (see Assumption \ref{asmp:mar}), under which the potential missingness is independent of potential outcomes. Unlike Sections \ref{sec:sharp_null_gen}--\ref{sec:monotone_sharp_null}, which treat potential missingness as fixed, here $(M_i(1), M_i(0))$ is treated as a pair of random variables under Assumption \ref{asmp:mar}.

Assumption \ref{asmp:mar} does not restrict the dependence between $M(1)$ and $M(0)$, so the information on potential missingness and outcomes remains the same as under general missingness mechanisms (see Panel A of Table \ref{tab:sharp_null}). However, a key implication of Assumption \ref{asmp:mar} is that, after appropriate conditioning, the experiment can be treated as a CRE involving only units with observed outcomes—allowing standard randomization tests without concern for missing data. Specifically, we can show that
\begin{align}\label{eq:imp_mar}
    \bs{Z}_{\S} \mid \bs{Y}(1), \bs{Y}(0), \bs{M}, \bs{Z}_{\S^\complement}, n_{\S1}, n_{\S0} 
    \ \sim \ 
    \CRE(\S, n_{\S1}, n_{\S0}), 
\end{align}
where $\S = \{i: M_i = 1, 1\le i \le n\}$, $\bs{Z}_{\S}$ and $\bs{Z}_{\S^\complement}$ denote the subvectors of $\bs{Z}$ corresponding to units in $\S$ and $\S^\complement$, respectively, $n_{\S1} = \sum_{i\in \S} Z_i$, and $n_{\S0} = |\S|-n_{\S1}$. That is, after proper conditioning, the treatment assignment for units in $\mathcal{S}$ follows the same distribution as that of a CRE, which randomly assigns $n_{\mathcal{S}1}$ units from $\mathcal{S}$ to treatment and the remaining $n_{\mathcal{S}0}$ to control.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:mar_miss}
\rm 
Under the CRE and Assumption \ref{asmp:mar}, 
a valid p-value for testing the sharp null hypothesis $H_{\bs{\delta}}$ in \eqref{eq:null_fisher_sharp} is 
$
p_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, \S}
$,
where $p_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, \S}$ is defined as:
\begin{align}\label{eq:pval_sharpnull_mar}
    p_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}, \S} = G_{\R, \phi, \S}( t_{\R, \phi, \S}(\bs{Z}, \bs{Y} - \bs{\delta}\circ \bs{Z})),
\end{align}
where $t_{\R, \phi, \S}(\cdot)$ and $G_{\R, \phi, \S}(\cdot)$ are defined as:
\begin{align*}
   t_{\R, \phi, \S} (\bs{z}, \bs{y})
   & = 
   t_{\R, \phi} (\bs{z}_{\S}, \bs{y}_{\S}), 
   \ \ 
   \text{and}
   \ \ 
   G_{\R, \phi, \S}(c)
   = \Pr 
   \left( t_{\R, \phi, \S} (\bs{A}, \bs{y}) \ge c
    \right) \text{ for } c \in \mathbb{R},
\end{align*}
with $t_{\R, \phi}$ defined in either \eqref{eq:test_stat_R_phi} or \eqref{eq:test_stat_mann_whitney},  
$G_{\R, \phi, \S}(\cdot)$ defined based on random assignment $\bs{A}_{\S}$ from $\CRE(\S, n_{\S1}, n_{\S0})$, 
and $\bs{z}_{\S}, \bs{y}_{\S}$ and $\bs{A}_{\S}$ being subvectors of $\bs{z}, \bs{y}$ and $\bs{A}$ for units in $\S$.
\end{theorem}

Theorem \ref{thm:mar_miss} is essentially testing the following ``modified'' sharp null hypothesis for units in $\mathcal{S}$ whose outcomes are observed: 
\begin{align}\label{null_fisher_sharp_nonmissing}
    H_{\bs{\delta}, \mathcal{S}}: \tau_{i} = \delta_{i}, \quad \text{for $i \in \mathcal{S} \coloneqq \{j: M_j = 1, 1\le j \le n\}$}.
\end{align}
The sharp null $H_{\bs{\delta}}$ implies $H_{\bs{\delta}, \mathcal{S}}$, so testing $H_{\bs{\delta}}$ reduces to conducting a randomization test for $H_{\bs{\delta}, \mathcal{S}}$ using only units with observed outcomes. Note that $\mathcal{S}$ is a random subset of units that generally depends on the treatment assignments. Moreover, the procedure in Theorem \ref{thm:mar_miss} also works when using a generic test statistic, such as the difference-in-means test statistic, since there will be no missing outcomes once we focus only on units in $\S$.

\section{Randomization inference for quantiles of individual treatment effects under sample attrition}\label{sec:quantile_null}

So far, we have discussed randomization tests for a sharp null hypothesis under sample attrition. However, sharp nulls are often criticized as being too restrictive. 
Therefore, it is natural to consider more general, non-sharp null hypotheses. 
One class of economically relevant non-sharp null hypotheses concerns the quantiles of individual treatment effects—for example, testing whether the median individual treatment effect is non-positive or testing whether the proportion of individuals who benefit from the treatment is bounded by a certain threshold. This type of null is especially important when treatment benefits are non-transferable \citep{heckman1997making}.

We first point out that the tests in Theorems \ref{thm:sharp_null_gen}--\ref{thm:mar_miss} are also valid for the bounded null hypothesis $\bs{\tau} \vecle \bs{\delta}$, i.e., $\tau_i \le \delta_i$ for all $i$. Setting $\bs{\delta} = c \bs{1}_n = (c, c, \ldots, c)^\top$ implies that the maximum individual treatment effect is bounded above by $c$. Thus, Theorems \ref{thm:sharp_null_gen}--\ref{thm:mar_miss} yield valid tests for extreme quantiles (e.g., maximum, or minimum once we perform label switching and outcome sign change) of individual treatment effects.

In this section, we generalize the previous theorems to make inference about all quantiles of individual treatment effects (or simply, effect quantiles). To facilitate the discussion, let $\tau_{(1)} \le \tau_{(2)} \le \ldots \le \tau_{(n)}$ denote the sorted individual treatment effects. We also introduce $\tau_{\texttt{t}(k)}$s and $\tau_{\texttt{c}(k)}$s to denote the sorted individual treatment effects among treated and control units, respectively. 

\subsection{Null hypotheses on treatment effect quantiles}\label{sec:null_quantile}

We consider testing null hypotheses on treatment effect quantiles among treated units, recalling that $\tau_{\texttt{t}(k)}$ is the sorted individual effect at rank $k$ among treated units: 
\begin{align}\label{eq:H_k_c_treat}
    H_{k,c}^\texttt{t}: \tau_{\texttt{t}(k)} \le c \Longleftrightarrow \sum_{i=1}^n Z_i \I\{ \tau_i > c \} \le n_1 - k, 
    \quad (1\le k \le n_1; c \in \mathbb{R}).
\end{align}
The null hypothesis in \eqref{eq:H_k_c_treat} is different from traditional null hypotheses, since it is random depending on the observed data. 
A p-value $p$ is said to be valid for testing $H_{k,c}^\texttt{t}$ if and only if $\mathbb{P}\left(p\le \alpha \text{ and } H_{k,c}^\texttt{t} \text{ holds}\right) \le \alpha$ for $\alpha \in (0,1)$. As discussed shortly, the standard test inversion based on such a valid p-value can lead to valid prediction intervals for treatment effect quantiles among treated units.

Before proceeding, we make two remarks about $H_{k,c}^\texttt{t}$. First, by treatment label switching and outcome sign change, we can also test treatment effect quantiles among control units. 
We can further combine inference for treated and control units to infer treatment effect quantiles among all units. 
Alternatively, we can infer effect quantiles among all units from those among only treated units, leveraging the fact that the treated group is a simple random sample from all units \citep{chen2024enhanced}. Therefore, testing treatment effects only on the treated units involves no loss of generality.

Second, we can also test one-sided null hypotheses stating that the treatment effect quantiles are bounded from below by any constant. 
We can combine the two one-sided tests to construct a two-sided test. 
Analogously, we can combine two one-sided confidence (or prediction) intervals for treatment effect quantiles to obtain two-sided confidence (or prediction) intervals.

\subsection{Treatment effect quantiles under general and monotone missingness mechanisms}\label{sec:quantile_genmon}

We consider inference for treatment effect quantiles under general and monotone missingness. While the methods apply to test statistics in Examples \ref{eg:test_outcome_only} and \ref{eg:test_outcome_miss}, and to any prespecified $b$ in the composite potential outcomes, we focus on the statistic in Example \ref{eg:test_outcome_miss} with the recommended $b$ for each missingness mechanism. This choice typically yields more powerful tests, as discussed in Sections \ref{sec:sharp_null_gen} and \ref{sec:monotone_sharp_null}, and supported by simulations in Section \ref{sec:sim}. Specifically, we define
\begin{align}\label{eq:sugg_worst_case}
    \tilde{\bs{Y}}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}}^{\gen}(0)
    = 
    \tilde{\bs{Y}}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, \infty}^{\gen}(0), 
    \quad 
    \tilde{\bs{Y}}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}}^{\monp}(0)
    = 
    \tilde{\bs{Y}}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, \infty}^{\monp}(0), 
    \quad 
    \tilde{\bs{Y}}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}}^{\monn}(0)
    = 
    \tilde{\bs{Y}}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, -\infty}^{\monn}(0)
\end{align}
to denote the suggested worst-case configurations of the composite potential outcomes under the general and monotone missingness mechanisms. 

Unlike $H_{\bs{\delta}}$, the null hypothesis $H_{k,c}^\texttt{t}$ in \eqref{eq:H_k_c_treat} is non-sharp, under which the individual potential outcomes are not imputable even in the absence of missing outcomes. A valid p-value for testing $H_{k,c}^\texttt{t}$ can be obtained by maximizing the p-values from Theorems \ref{thm:sharp_null_gen_comp} and \ref{thm:sharp_null_mon}, over all sharp null hypotheses satisfying $H_{k,c}^\texttt{t}$:
\begin{align*}
    \sup_{\bs{\delta} \in \mathcal{H}_{k,c}^\texttt{t}} \tilde{p}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}}^{\square},
\end{align*}
where $\mathcal{H}_{k,c}^\texttt{t} \coloneqq \left\{ \bs{\delta} \in \mathbb{R}^n: \sum_{i=1}^n Z_i \I(\delta_i > c) \le n_1 - k \right\}$ and $\square = \gen, \monp, \monn$.
By the distribution-free property of the test statistic, this reduces to minimizing
$t_{\R, \phi}(\bs{Z}, \tilde{\bs{Y}}^{\square}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}}(0))$ over $\bs{\delta} \in \mathcal{H}_{k,c}^\texttt{t}$. This minimization has a closed-form solution for test statistics of the forms \eqref{eq:test_stat_R_phi} and \eqref{eq:test_stat_mann_whitney}. Before presenting the formal theorem, we first provide some intuition.

Intuitively, when finding the worst-case configuration of $\tilde{\bs{Y}}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}}^{\square}(0)$ for $\square = \gen, \monp$ or $\monn$ under any hypothesized individual treatment effect vector $\bs{\delta}$, only the components of $\bs{\delta}$ for treated units with observed outcomes are relevant. Let $\mathcal{J} \coloneqq \{i : Z_i = M_i = 1\}$ denote this set of units, whose size is $n_{11} = \sum_{i=1}^n Z_i M_i$. 
For each unit $i\in \mathcal{J}$, by definition, the corresponding $i$th coordinate of $\tilde{\bs{Y}}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}}^{\square}(0)$ is 
$\tilde{Y}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, i}^{\square}(0) = Y_i - \delta_i$.
Therefore, to minimize the ranks of the treated units' $\tilde{Y}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\delta}, i}^{\square}(0)$s, a natural approach is to let $n_1 - k$ units (or all units if $n_1 - k \ge n_{11}$) in $\mathcal{J}$ have arbitrarily large hypothesized individual effects (i.e., $\delta_i \to \infty$) and let the remaining units in $\mathcal{J}$ have hypothesized individual effects equal to $c$ (i.e., $\delta_{i} = c$). That is, the intuitive solution assigns arbitrarily large hypothesized effects to units in $\mathcal{J}$ with the largest observed outcomes, setting the remaining effects to a constant $c$. It turns out that this intuitive worst-case configuration of $\bs{\delta}$ yields the minimum value of the test statistic $t_{\R, \phi}\left( \bs{Z}, \tilde{\bs{Y}}^{\square}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}}(0) \right)$ over all $\bs{\delta} \in \mathcal{H}_{k,c}^\texttt{t}$, for test statistics of either form \eqref{eq:test_stat_R_phi} or \eqref{eq:test_stat_mann_whitney}.

We now formally present valid procedures for testing $H_{k,c}^\texttt{t}$ in \eqref{eq:H_k_c_treat} under general or monotone missingness mechanisms. We first sort the units in $\mathcal{J}$ based on their observed outcomes. That is, $\mathcal{J} = \{j_1, j_2, \ldots, j_{n_{11}}\}$ such that $\psi_{j_{l+1}, j_l}(Y_{j_{l+1}}, Y_{j_l}) = 1$ for $1 \le l \le n_{11}-1$, where $\psi$ is defined in \eqref{eq:psi_ij}. For $1\le L \le n_{11}$, let $\mathcal{J}_{L} \coloneqq \{ j_{n_{11}-L+1}, j_{n_{11}-L+2}, \ldots, j_{n_{11}} \}$ be the set of indices of the treated units with the largest $L$ observed outcomes. Let $\mathcal{J}_{0} \coloneqq \emptyset$ for convenience. Define $\bs{\xi}_{k, c} = (\xi_{k,c,1}, \xi_{k,c,2}, \ldots, \xi_{k,c,n})^\top$ for some $\kappa \in \mathbb{R}^{+}$ such that 
\begin{align}\label{eq:xi_kc}
    \xi_{k,c,i} = 
    \begin{cases}
        \max_{i:Z_i=M_i=1} Y_i - \min_{i:Z_i=0, M_i=1} Y_i + \kappa, 
        & \text{if } i \in \mathcal{J}_{\min\{n_1-k, n_{11}\}}, 
        \\
        c, & \text{otherwise}. 
    \end{cases}
\end{align}
For $\square = \gen, \monp \text{ or } \monn$, $1\le k \le n_1$ and $c\in \mathbb{R}$, define further 
\begin{align}\label{eq:Y_comp_ks}
    \tilde{\bs{Y}}_{\texttt{t}, k, c}^{\square}(0)  \coloneqq \tilde{\bs{Y}}_{\bs{Z},\bs{\xi}_{k,c}}^{\square}(0), 
    \text{ 
    which 
    is defined as in \eqref{eq:sugg_worst_case} with $\bs{\delta}$
    equal to $\bs{\xi}_{k,c}$ in \eqref{eq:xi_kc}}. 
\end{align}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:p_t_kc_genmon}
\rm 
    Under Assumption \ref{asmp:general}, \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0} or \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_le_M0}, 
    for any $1\le k \le n_1$ and $c\in \mathbb{R}$, 
    a valid p-value for testing the null hypothesis $H_{k,c}^\texttt{t}$ in \eqref{eq:H_k_c_treat} is 
    \begin{align}\label{eq:p_t_kc_genmon}
    \tilde{p}_{\texttt{t}, k, c}^{\square}  \coloneqq G_{\R, \phi}\left( t_{\R, \phi}\left(\bs{Z}, \tilde{\bs{Y}}^{\square}_{\texttt{t}, k, c}(0)  \right) \right)
    \text{ with } 
    \square = 
    \begin{cases}
        \gen & \text{under Assumption } \ref{asmp:general}, 
        \\
        \monp & \text{under Assumption } \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0}, 
        \\
        \monn & \text{under Assumption } \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_le_M0}, 
    \end{cases}    
    \end{align}
    where $t_{\R, \phi}$ is defined in either \eqref{eq:test_stat_R_phi} or \eqref{eq:test_stat_mann_whitney}, $G_{\R, \phi}$ is defined in \eqref{eq:G_R_phi}, and $\tilde{\bs{Y}}_{\texttt{t}, k, c}^{\square}(0)$ is defined in \eqref{eq:Y_comp_ks}. 
\end{theorem}

As shown in the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:p_t_kc_genmon}, $\bs{\xi}_{k,c}$ in \eqref{eq:xi_kc} lies in $\mathcal{H}_{k,c}^\texttt{t}$ and minimizes $t_{\R, \phi}\left(\bs{Z}, \tilde{\bs{Y}}^{\square}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}}(0) \right)$ over $\bs{\delta} \in \mathcal{H}_{k,c}^\texttt{t}$, where $\square = \gen, \monp \text{ or } \monn$. In other words, $\tilde{\bs{Y}}^{\square}_{\bs{Z}, \bs{\delta}}(0)$ is the worst-case configuration of the composite potential outcomes under $\mathcal{H}_{k,c}^\texttt{t}$. Thus, the p-values in \eqref{eq:p_t_kc_genmon} are equivalently the supremum of the p-values in Theorems \ref{thm:sharp_null_gen_comp} and \ref{thm:sharp_null_mon} over all sharp null hypotheses satisfying $H_{k,c}^\texttt{t}$.

In the worst-case configuration of individual treatment effects in \eqref{eq:xi_kc}, we cannot assign infinite effects to units in $\mathcal{J}_{\min\{n_1-k, n_{11}\}}$ as in \citet{caughey2021randomization}, because this would yield composite potential outcomes equal to negative infinity. These values could tie with those of units whose outcomes are missing under the monotone negative missingness in Assumption~\ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_le_M0} (see Panel C of Table \ref{tab:sharp_null}), which is undesirable.

We can then use Theorem \ref{thm:p_t_kc_genmon} and test inversion to construct prediction intervals for the treatment effect quantiles among treated units. These prediction intervals are simultaneously valid, as shown in the following theorem.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:ci_genmon_treat}
\rm
    Under Assumption \ref{asmp:general}, \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0} or \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_le_M0},
    for any $1\le k \le n_1$, 
    $\mathcal{I}^{\alpha}_{\texttt{t}, k} = \{c: \tilde{p}_{\texttt{t}, k, c}^{\square} > \alpha \}$ is a $1-\alpha$ prediction set for $\tau_{\texttt{t}(k)}$, 
    where $\tilde{p}_{\texttt{t}, k, c}^{\square}$ is defined in \eqref{eq:p_t_kc_genmon},
    and it must be a one-sided interval of form $[\hat{\tau}_{\texttt{t}(k)}, \infty)$ or $(\hat{\tau}_{\texttt{t}(k)}, \infty)$, 
    with $\hat{\tau}_{\texttt{t}(k)} = \inf \mathcal{I}^{\alpha}_{\texttt{t}, k}$. 
    Moreover, these prediction intervals are simultaneously valid across all quantiles, in the sense that 
    $\mathbb{P}\left( \tau_{\texttt{t}(k)} \in \mathcal{I}^{\alpha}_{\texttt{t}, k} \text{ for all } 1\le k \le n_1 \right) \ge 1-\alpha$. 
\end{theorem}

We can also construct simultaneous prediction intervals for treatment effect quantiles among control units by applying Theorem \ref{thm:ci_genmon_treat} with switched treatment labels and changed outcome signs. Switching labels reverses the monotone missingness mechanism. To fix ideas, suppose Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0} holds. Then, Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_le_M0} holds after switching treatment labels. Therefore, we will use Theorem \ref{thm:ci_genmon_treat} with $\square = \monp$ to construct prediction intervals for effect quantiles among treated units, and apply the same theorem with $\square = \monn$, switched treatment labels and changed outcome signs to construct prediction intervals for effect quantiles among control units.

To construct confidence intervals for effect quantiles among all units, we can either combine prediction intervals for treated and control units or extrapolate based on prediction intervals for only treated or control units in the same way as described in \citet[][Theorem 2 and Remarks 2--4]{chen2024enhanced}.

\subsection{Treatment effect quantiles under sharp and random missingness}\label{sec:ci_sharp_random}

By the same logic as in Remark~\ref{rmk:sharp_miss} and Theorem~\ref{thm:mar_miss}, under either the sharp missingness mechanism, where $M_i(1) = M_i(0)$ for all $i$, or the missing at random mechanisms (see Assumption~\ref{asmp:mar}), we can exclude units with missing outcomes and perform randomization inference following the approaches of \citet{caughey2021randomization} and \citet{chen2024enhanced} to infer treatment effect quantiles among only units with observed outcomes.
This inference can be further extrapolated to treatment effect quantiles among all units; however, the procedure differs under the two missingness mechanisms. See the supplementary material for details.

\section{Simulation studies}\label{sec:sim}

\subsection{A simulation study for testing sharp null hypothesis}\label{sec:sim_sharp_null}

We first conduct a simulation study to evaluate the finite-sample validity of the proposed randomization test under various missingness mechanisms. Specifically, we compare three inferential procedures: (1) the worst-case inferential procedure using the outcome-only test statistic in Example \ref{eg:test_outcome_only}; (2) the worst-case inferential procedure using the outcome-missingness composite test statistic in Example \ref{eg:test_outcome_miss} with different choices of $b$; and (3) the naive procedure that drops units with missing outcomes and applies the standard randomization test to the observed sample. 

We generate potential outcomes as $Y_i(0) = Y_i(1) \overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0, 1)$ with $N = 500$, and assign treatment via a CRE allocating half the units to treatment and half to control. We test Fisher's sharp null hypothesis of no treatment effect for any unit, formally stated as $H_0: \bs{\tau}=\bs{0}$, at $10\%$ nominal level. We consider five types of missingness mechanisms, each evaluated under two scenarios in which approximately 5\% and 10\% of units having missing outcomes:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textsf{(Threshold missingness, regarded as general missingness)}  $M_i(0) = \mathbbm{1}\{ Y_i(0) \leq \text{qnorm}(p) \}$ and $M_i(1) = \mathbbm{1}\{ Y_i(0) \geq \text{qnorm}(q) \}$, with $p = 0.95, q = 0.05$ (5\% missing) or $p = 0.9, q = 0.1$ (10\% missing).

    \item \textsf{(Monotone missingness in Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_ge_M0})}
    $M_{i}(1) = \I\{ Y_{i}(0) \leq \text{qnorm}(p + q) \}$ and $M_{i}(0) = \I\{ Y_{i}(0) \leq \text{qnorm}(p - q) \}$, with $p = 0.95, q = 0.03$ (5\% missing) or $p = 0.9, q = 0.05$ (10\% missing). Note that, by construction,  $M_i(1) \geq M_i(0)$ for all $i$. 

    \item \textsf{(Monotone missingness in Assumption \ref{asmp:mo_miss_M1_le_M0})} $M_{i}(1) = \I\{ Y_{i}(0) \geq \text{qnorm}(p + q) \}$ and $M_{i}(0) = \I\{ Y_{i}(0) \geq \text{qnorm}(p - q) \}$, with $p = 0.05, q = 0.03$ (5\% missing) or $p = 0.1, q = 0.05$ (10\% missing). Note that, by construction,  $M_i(1) \leq M_i(0)$ for all $i$. 

    \item \textsf{(Missing at random)}  $(M_{i}(0), M_{i}(1))$s are independent of $(Y_{i}(0), Y_{i}(1))$s. Specifically, $M_i(0) \overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \text{Bernoulli}(p)$, $M_i(1) \overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \text{Bernoulli}(p)$, and $M_i(0)$s are independent of $M_i(1)$s, with $p=0.95$ (5\% missing) or $p=0.9$ (10\% missing).
\end{enumerate}

Table~\ref{tab:sim_sharp_null_Type1_Error} reports the empirical Type I errors of various tests under the missingness mechanisms described above, based on $10^4$ Monte Carlo simulations. Specifically, Column 3 presents the Type I errors for the proposed tests based on control potential outcomes, while Columns 4--7 show the Type I errors for the proposed tests using composite potential outcomes with different values of $b$. For comparison, Column 8 reports the Type I errors of the ``naive'' method that excludes units with missing outcomes. All the tests use the Wilcoxon rank-sum test statistic.

Table~\ref{tab:sim_sharp_null_Type1_Error} yields several key findings. First, the proposed worst-case inference procedures control the Type I error at or below the nominal level across all missingness mechanisms, confirming their validity. In contrast, the ``naive'' method exhibits substantial Type I error inflation under the threshold and monotone missingness mechanisms, with Type I error rates exceeding $75\%$ in several cases, even when the overall proportion of missing outcomes is small. This highlights an important concern for empirical practice: using an inferential procedure that ignores the missingness mechanism can result in severe size distortions and misleading conclusions. Note that, under the missing at random mechanism, the ``naive'' method achieves valid Type I error control, consistent with our theoretical results.

Second, the choice of $b$ affects the conservativeness (and, analogously, the power) of the proposed tests based on composite potential outcomes under both the threshold and monotone missingness mechanisms. Moreover, the optimal choice of $b$ varies depending on the missingness mechanism. Specifically, the test becomes less conservative as $b$ increases under threshold missingness (viewed as a general missingness mechanism) and under monotone missingness with $M_i(1) \geq M_i(0)$ for all $i$. In contrast, it becomes less conservative as $b$ decreases under monotone missingness with $M_i(1) \leq M_i(0)$ for all $i$. Accordingly, we recommend choosing $b = \infty$ in the former cases and $b = -\infty$ in the latter. These recommendations align with the theoretical guidance discussed in Sections~\ref{sec:sharp_null_gen} and~\ref{sec:monotone_sharp_null}.

