\subsection{A simulation study for inferring quantiles of individual treatment effects}

We next conduct a simulation study to investigate the power of different rank-based test statistics in detecting positive quantiles of individual treatment effects. We generate potential outcomes as $Y_{i}(0) \overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0, 1)$ and $Y_{i}(1) \overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$ with sample size $N=500$, where $Y_i(0)$s are independent of $Y_i(1)$s. 
We consider again the CRE which assigns half of the units to the treated group and the remaining half to the control group. We test the null hypothesis that the $95\%$ quantile of individual treatment effect among treated units is at most $c$, formally stated as $H_0: \tau_{\texttt{t}(k)} \leq c$ with $k=\lceil 0.95N_1 \rceil$, $N_1 = N/2$ and $c=0$, at the $10\%$ nominal level. We generate the potential missingness indicators in the same way as in Section \ref{sec:sim_sharp_null}. 
We use test statistics based on the composite potential outcomes with the suggested optimal choice of $b$ under the general or monotone missingness mechanisms. We consider both the rank-sum and Mann-Whitney-type U-statistics with various choices of $s$ in \eqref{eq:test_stat_R_phi} and \eqref{eq:test_stat_mann_whitney}. 
Note that both test statistics tend to place greater weights on larger outcomes as $s$ increases, and they become equivalent to the Wilcoxon rank-sum statistic when $s=2$.

Tables~\ref{tab:sim_quantile_power_thresh_mp_mn} reports the simulated power of different test statistics under various missingness mechanisms, based on $10^3$ simulations. The Mann--Whitney-type U-statistics in \eqref{eq:test_stat_mann_whitney} generally exhibit slightly better power than the rank-sum statistics in \eqref{eq:test_stat_R_phi}. 
Nevertheless, the choice of the parameter $s$ plays a more critical role. In many of the scenarios, the test using the traditional Wilcoxon rank-sum statistic with $s = 2$ shows poor power, whereas larger values of $s$ yield substantial power improvements. This is because, in the corresponding worst-case imputation of the original or composite potential outcomes, treated units tend to have larger values---reflecting the fact that their potential outcomes are drawn from normal distributions with larger variances and heavier right tails. In such cases, assigning greater weight to larger outcomes (via larger $s$) enhances power, consistent with the findings in \citet{caughey2021randomization}.

However, under threshold missingness with a higher proportion of missing units, or under monotone missingness where $M_i(1) \ge M_i(0)$, the power of the tests deteriorates substantially as $s$ increases from 6 to 10. This decline is driven by the presence of positive infinite values in the worst-case imputation of composite potential outcomes for control units. In such cases, assigning excessive weight to larger outcomes can reduce the test's power.

