These results can also be used for labeled molecules that do not undergo chemical reactions themselves but are part of a multicomponent mixture in which reactions can occur. The dynamics of such molecules follow Eqs.~\eqref{eq:effective_rate_A}, \eqref{eq:effective_rate_B}, with $r_{A\to B}=r_{B\to A}=0$.
\paragraph*{Single-molecule dynamics.}
As discussed in Ref.\cite{Bo2021a}, taking the limit of labeling very few molecules we can interpret Eq. \eqref{eq:effective_rate_A} as describing the evolution of the probability density of of having a single labeled molecule of species $A$ in position $x$.
We can then write the mixed master-Fokker-Planck equation for
the probability density of of having a single labeled molecule of species $\alpha$ in position $x$, $P_\alpha$ as: 
\begin{align}\label{eq:FP}
\partial_t P_\alpha &= -\nabla\cdot \VEC{J}_\alpha+K_{ji}P_\beta-K_{\alpha\beta}P_\alpha \,, 
\end{align} 
where  $K_{\alpha\beta}=r_{i\to j}/\phi_\alpha$ is the single-molecule reaction probability rate of a reaction transforming species $\alpha$ into $\beta$ (see Ref. \cite{gillespie1992rigorous}), and \\
\textbf{change in notation for v!!! update!!!}
\begin{align}
    \VEC{J}_\alpha=-D_\alpha\nabla P_\alpha-(D_\alpha/k_BT) (\nabla W_\alpha)P_\alpha + \VEC{v}_\alphaP_\alpha
\end{align}
is the single-molecule probability flux, which was derived in \cite{Bo2021a},
with the effective potential $W_\alpha=-k_BT\ln {\phi_\alpha} $, the drift velocity $\VEC{v}_\alpha=\VEC{j}_\alpha/\phi_\alpha$, the single-molecule diffusion coefficient $D_\alpha$ as in Eq.~\ref{eq:d}, and no summation is performed over the repeated indices. \\

