   \end{align}
where $h=
\sum_{\langle ij\rangle}\chi_{\alpha\beta}\phi_\alpha\phi_\beta +\sum_{i=0}^n\omega^0_\alpha\phi_\alpha$ includes all the energetic contribution from the internal energies and the interaction energies.
The chemical potential then reads
\begin{align}
\mu_\alpha = k_B T \ln  \left( \phi_\alpha \phi_0^{-\nu_\alpha/\nu_0} \right)
%\mu_\alpha =  k_B T \ln  \left( \frac{\phi_\alpha}{\phi_0}\right) 
+h_\alpha \, ,
\end{align}
where 
\begin{align}
    h_\alpha&=\nu_\alpha\frac{\delta h}{\delta \phi_\alpha}\\
   &= \nu_\alpha\left[\sum_{j=1}^n  (\chi_{\alpha\beta}-\chi_{i0}-\chi_{j0}) \phi_\beta
+ \chi_{i0} +  \omega_\alpha^0-  \omega_0^0\right] \,,
\end{align} so that we obtain
\begin{align}
\begin{split}\label{eq:gradmu}
\nabla \mu_\alpha &=
\frac{k_B T}{\phi_\alpha \phi_0}
\Big[\phi_0\nabla \phi_\alpha + \frac{\nu_\alpha}{\nu_0}\phi_\alpha \sum_{j=1}^n \nabla \phi_\beta \Big]  +\nabla  h_\alpha\,,
\end{split}\\\label{eq:gradmu_diff}
\nabla (\mu_\alpha - \mu_\beta) &=
\frac{k_B T}{\phi_\alpha \phi_\beta\phi_0} \left[\phi_0\left(\phi_\beta \nabla \phi_\alpha - \phi_\alpha \nabla \phi_\beta\right)+\phi_\alpha\phi_\beta\frac{\nu_\alpha-\nu_\beta}{\nu_0} \sum_{k=1}^n\nabla\phi_k\right]+ \nabla (h_\alpha - h_\beta)\,.
\end{align}
The diffusive flux appearing in Eq.~\eqref{eq:cont}, $\boldsymbol{j}_\alpha =-  \nu_\alpha\sum_\beta M_{\alpha\beta}\nabla\mu_\beta $, is driven by gradients in chemical potentials $\mu_\alpha=\nu \delta F[f]/\delta \phi_\alpha$ multiplied by the mobility matrix  
$M_{\alpha\beta}$:
\begin{gather}\label{eq:Mii}
M_{ii} =   m_{i0} \phi_\alpha\phi_0 +
\sum_{j\neq i}^n  m_{\alpha\beta} \phi_\alpha \phi_\beta \\\nonumber
M_{\alpha\beta} = -m_{\alpha\beta} \phi_\alpha \phi_\beta, \;\;\; \forall i \neq j 
\end{gather}

with coefficients $m_{\alpha\beta}=m_{ji}$ containing information on how quickly molecules of species $\alpha$ and $\beta$  exchange position \cite{Kramer1984,Mao2020,Bo2021a,Cotton2022}. %Note that $M_{\alpha\beta}$ is symmetric but the coefficient $\nu_\alpha M_{\alpha\beta}$ is only symmetric in the case of equal molecular volumes.
In the following, it will prove convenient to rewrite $M_{ii}= m_{i0}\phi_\alpha\big[(1-\phi_\alpha) - \sum_{j\neq i}^n  \xi_{\alpha\beta} \phi_\beta \big]$ where we have introduced
\begin{align}\label{eq:xiij}
   \xi_{\alpha\beta}=1-m_{\alpha\beta}/m_{i0} \, ,
\end{align}
 with $\xi_{\alpha\beta} \leq 1$. For the flux of component $\alpha$, we then have
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
\boldsymbol{j}_\alpha = &-\nu_\alpha m_{i0}\left(\phi_\alpha(1-\phi_\alpha)-\sum_{j\neq i}\phi_\beta\right)\nabla\mu_\alpha+\\
&-\nu_\alpha\phi_\alpha\sum_{j\neq i}^n m_{\alpha\beta}\phi_\beta\nabla (\mu_\alpha - \mu_\beta)\,.
\end{split}
\end{align}
 This expression shows how this choice of mobility gives a flux that is proportional to the product of the volume fractions of the two species that are to be exchanged and the gradient of their exchange chemical potential ({\bf clarify}). Such mobility matrix was used, for instance, in Ref.~\cite{Kramer1984} to describe the interdiffusion of polymer couples and in~\cite{Bo2021a}).
Plugging in the expression for the chemical potential gradients of Eqs.~\eqref{eq:gradmu} and ~\eqref{eq:gradmu_diff}, 
we can express the fluxes as 
\begin{align}\label{eq:j_fin}
\begin{split}
\boldsymbol{j}_\alpha = &-\nu_\alpha m_{i0} \left(k_B T\nabla \phi_\alpha +  \phi_\alpha\nabla h_\alpha - \phi_\alpha \sum_{j= 1}^n \phi_\beta \nabla h_\beta\right)\\
&-m_{i0}\sum_{j=1}^n \xi_{\alpha\beta}
\left[ k_B T (\nu_0\phi_\alpha \nabla \phi_\beta- \nu_\alpha\phi_\beta \nabla \phi_\alpha) - \nu_\alpha\phi_\alpha \phi_\beta (\nabla h_\alpha - \nabla h_\beta)\right]\\
&+\phi_\alpha\frac{\nu_\alpha}{\nu_0}\left(\sum_{k=1}^n\nabla \phi_k\right)\left(m_{i0}(\nu_\alpha-\nu_0)-
\frac{1}{\phi_0}\sum_{j=1}^nm_{\alpha\beta}(\nu_\alpha-\nu_\beta)\right)\,.
\end{split}
\end{align}
\textbf{double check expression!! make it more compact with effective D?}
where the coefficient $\xi_{\alpha\beta}$ quantifies the difference in mobility for exchanges of particles of species $\alpha$ and $\beta$ with respect to exchanges between $\alpha$ and the solvent as given in Eq.~\eqref{eq:xiij}. 
Equation \ref{eq:j_fin} generalizes the results of Ref.~\cite{Bo2021a} to the case where different species have different molecular volumes.
\subsection{Labeling multicomponent diffusive fluxes}
Since the labeled and unlabeled molecules have the same physical properties they must have the same exchange attempt rate with the solvent as in the case without labeling  $m_{k_U0}=m_{k_L0}=m_{k0}$, where $k$ refers to either $A$ or $B$. The same holds for exchanges with the other species  $m_{i L}=m_{iU}=m_{in}$ for any $i<n$. 
 Only the terms concerning the exchange between labeled and unlabeled molecules require additional knowledge. The exchange attempt rate among these species has to be specified and is contained in the coefficient $\xi_{LU}$, defined in Eq.~\eqref{eq:xiij}. These exchanges do not affect the sum of the fluxes of labeled and unlabeled molecules, but they affect their specific molecular diffusion constants. 
Concerning the free energy, the fact that the non-entropic contributions to the chemical potential for labeled and unlabeled molecules are the same gives $h_n=h_{L}=h_{U}$. 
The procedure to determine the diffusion flux of labeled molecules is the same as for the case without chemical reactions, discussed in \cite{Bo2021a}. The flux of labeled molecules of type $A$ gives Eq.~\eqref{eq:j_multi} in the main text.
\section{Reaction rates for interacting mixtures.}\label{appendix:rates}
As shown in Ref. \cite{Bauermann2022a}, the difference between the forward and backward rates gives the the net chemical rate per volume $r_\alpha = r_\alpha^{+}-r_\alpha^{-}$.
Similarly,  we introduce the stoichiometric matrix  $\sigma_{i \alpha} = \sigma_{i \alpha}^{-}-\sigma_{i \alpha}^{+}$ .
The  the chemical rates $r_\alpha$ and the stoichiometric matrix  $\sigma_{i \alpha}$ determine the  source rates $r_\alpha$ 
\begin{align}
r_\alpha = \sum_{\alpha=1}^S \nu_\alpha \sigma_{i \alpha}  r_\alpha \,,
\label{eq:flux_gener}
\end{align}
where reactions that conserve volume, the stoichiometric coefficients $\sigma_{i \alpha}$  must obey
$\sum_{i=0}^M \nu_\alpha \sigma_{i \alpha}  = 0$.
The Gibbs free energy
\begin{align}\label{eq:gibbs}
\Delta \mu_\alpha
= \sum_{i=1}^M \sigma_{i \alpha} \mu_\alpha \,,
\end{align}
is the free energy change due to a single event of reaction $\alpha$. It can be expressed in terms of the exchange chemical potential $\mu_\alpha$ given in Eq.~\eqref{eq:chem_pot}, and it is what drives chemical reactions. The rates of systems that are microscopically reversible satisfy the detailed balance condition
\begin{align}
\frac{r_\alpha^{+}}{r_\alpha^{-}} = \exp\left(- \frac{\Delta \mu_\alpha}{k_B T} \right) \,, \label{eq:detailed_balance_gen}
\end{align}
which ensures relaxation toward equilibrium in the absence of driving.
Such rates can be written as
\begin{align}
r_\alpha^\pm = k_\alpha  \exp\left(\frac{\sum_{i=1}^M\sigma_{i \alpha}^\pm \mu_\alpha}{k_B T}\right) \,, \label{eq:reactionrate_plusminus}
\end{align}
where $k_\alpha$ is a kinetic coefficient, which can depend on composition.
Plugging the expression of the exchange chemical potentials~\eqref{eq:chem_pot} in the the definition of the source rate~\eqref{eq:flux_gener}, we obtain Eq~\eqref{eq:reaction_rates} in the main text.
