\section{Introduction}
\noindent
First proposed by Landau and Ginzburg long before the formulation of the BCS theory, the topics of phase transitions and symmetry breaking in Landau-type models have been extensively studied in the literature. Although this area of research remains intriguing, finding a new model that satisfies these properties is challenging because extensive exploration has already been conducted in this field.

Well known as the energy rate of change of materials with temperature, specific heat is also a powerful tool that enables investigations into the physical properties of condensed materials \cite{1}. Random behavior of the specific-heat jump (SHJ) is observed at the transition temperature in some high-$T_{c_0}$ superconductors, ranging from a complete disappearance \cite{2, 3} to an exponential growth \cite{4}, through a scaling behavior $\Delta{C_p} = AT^n_{c_0}$, where $A$ is a constant,  $n$ a scaling order and $T_{c_0}$ the varying mean-field critical temperature. The power-law dependence of the SHJ is due to thermal fluctuations and mass renormalization around the critical point. Quantum fluctuations of spin-density waves that contribute to mass renormalization are generally less significant than those arising from thermal fluctuations. However, this trend can be reversed as the dimensionality of the system is reduced. Thermodynamics, through its various principles, enables the acquisition of valuable information on the critical states of matter, providing insights into phenomenological phenomena.

Based on the scaling behavior, $n = 3$ corresponds to the properties of thermodynamics of a system with strong critical quantum interactions, where electrons responsible for superconductivity contribute a $T^3$ term to the specific heat. The gradual increase in the scaling behavior of $\Delta{C_p}$ at $T_{c_0}$ originates from the fact that the superconducting gap opens up exponentially fast \cite{5, 6}. Generally observed from the electron-doped compounds, expressly for a family of doped $Ba(Fe_{1 - x}TM_x)_2As_2$ compounds with $TM = Co, Ni$, the case when $n = 2$ corresponds to an ideal Bud'ko, Ni, and Canfield (BNC) scaling \cite{7} that is constrained to a very narrow range of variation in $T_{c_0}$ \cite{8, 9}. The Landau and BCS theories yield the result $\Delta{C_p} = \gamma_0T_{c_0}$ where $\gamma_0$ is the mean-field Sommerfeld coefficient of the normal state. However, for multiband superconductors, taking into account inter- and intra-band pairing interactions, a substantial deviation from the standard single-band BCS result is observed. These illustrations show that a complete understanding of the specific heat anomaly at the superconducting transition remains a challenging task. 

The energy gap characteristic of the superconducting state is essential for developing the microscopic theory of superconductivity \cite{10}. The zero-field specific heat of normal metals exhibits a temperature dependence given by the expression $ C_p = \gamma_0{T} + \beta{T^3}$. While, $C_e = \gamma_0{T}$ describes the electronic contribution, $C_L = \beta{T^3}$ corresponds to the lattice contribution \cite{11}. Below the critical temperature, the specific heat is mainly affected by the electronic component. Tunneling experiments conducted by Giaever illustrate this phenomenon \cite{12}, which examines the change in the density of electron states as a metal transitions from its normal state to its superconducting state. As a metal is cooling below $T_{c_0}$, the electronic contribution of a superconductor increases abruptly. Then, it decays rapidly with decreasing temperature, falling off to zero exponentially as $T \rightarrow 0$, highlighting a superconducting energy gap. 

While the conventional BCS mechanism explains well the high-$T_{c_0}$ superconductivity observed in superconductors by highlighting a pairing glue related to magnetic fluctuations, the Ginzburg-Landau theory (GLT) considers spatially non-uniform situations where the order parameter (OP) depends on position and the free energy depending on the gradient of the OP. If these two theories account for the existence of a SHJ, they do not explain its disappearance or the random variations caused by specific intrinsic material parameters. Several effects are associated with significant changes in the SHJ at $T_{c_0}$; we can cite, for instance, the multiband superconductivity \cite{13, 14}, effects associated with a normal state pseudogap \cite{15} or effects of paramagnetic impurities \cite{5, 16}. Despite extensive theoretical efforts to understand the pairing mechanism at $T_{c_0}$, the open issue concerning the temperature dependence of the specific heat measured through $T_{c_0}$ is one property that needs to be resolved. There is no universal theory to explain the random behavior of the SHJ at $T_{c_0}$ due to very different configurations of materials. We can nevertheless put forward some theoretical hypotheses to justify some experimental observations. Calculations using the GLT adequately reveal the behavior $C_e/T_{c_0} = \gamma_0$ at $T_{c_0}$. However, deviations from asymptotic behavior observed in several high-$T_{c}$ superconductors recommend some correction of the GLT to make it more appropriate for newly observed phenomena at the transition point.

Within the framework of this paper, we investigate both the disappearance and the anomalously enhanced SHJ at $T_{c_0}$ from the perspective of strong fluctuation corrections to the OP in the GLT. These strong OP fluctuations lead to strong coupling superconductivity, characterized by a relatively high or low transition temperature, depending on the spatial arrangements and interactions between electrons that influence the symmetries of the system. The SHJ can either disappear or become large depending on the crossover of the type of OPs. This crossover in the framework of the disappearance of the SHJ leads to the loss of a large portion of the entropy at $T_{c_0}$ \cite{3, 17}

It is well known that reducing dimensionality causes a loss of long-range order due to thermal fluctuations, as stated in the Mermin-Wagner-Hohenberg theorem \cite{18, 19}. If thermal fluctuations diminish over long distances in three-dimensional systems, they are more likely to give rise to topological defects, such as vortices, in two-dimensional systems \cite{20, 21}. Scalapino \textit{et al.} \cite{22} showed that improper treatment of fluctuations is the cause of the failure of the GLT to account for microscopic interactions in physical systems. If the GLT predicts a finite critical temperature, the Mermin-Wagner-Hohenberg theorem, on the other hand, stipulates that continuous symmetry cannot be spontaneously broken in condensed matter with short-range interactions in dimensions greater than two \cite{18, 19}. The GLT does not allow for the prediction of the exact transition temperature but only the behavior of the system around the transition. These observations indicate that the limitations of the GLT in accounting for specific experimental facts in low-dimensional materials are not solely linked to the accuracy of critical exponents, as detailed by renormalization group theory  \cite{23, 24, 25, 26, 27, 28, 29, 30}, but also to the transition temperature $T_{c_0}$ that should not be viewed as a transition temperature; instead, it acts more as a characteristic temperature scale associated with thermal fluctuations \cite{4,31}. Any physical model within the framework of phase transitions should incorporate these principles to accurately describe the behavior of a material as its dimensionality is reduced.

In the GLT, the quadratic coefficient contains all useful information about the physics of the system. An appropriate description of this thermodynamic quantity makes it possible to gain phenomenological insights even when a more microscopic understanding is absent \cite{5}. In contrast to the standard GLT, this paper provides a correction term for the quadratic coefficient. It presents exact expressions for this quantity, taking into account strong fluctuation considerations, scattering state contributions, and the system's dimensionality \cite{32}. We express the temperature and dimensional dependencies of the quadratic coefficient through nonlinear polynomial equations. These equations account for anisotropy and the intrinsic characteristics of the system by incorporating a scale energy parameter. This parameter represents the free energy barrier between a metastable state and a state of lower free energy.\cite{33, 34}. 

The structure of the paper is outlined as follows: Section II presents the GLT, emphasizing its limitations and the need to make corrections through a process that highlights the crucial idea of dimensionality and the system's intrinsic parameters in the manifestation and analysis of transitions. This work will serve as a valuable resource for understanding the random behavior of the SHJ, particularly in the context of strong fluctuation corrections of the GLT. It enables us to develop a dimensional phenomenology of critical behavior. In section III, alternative exact expressions of the specific heat, containing more information, are derived. The model's results improve simulation variations of the SHJ and accurately predict specific physical constraints. In section IV, we compare the results of the calculations with the experimental data of Iron-, Yttrium-, and bismuth-based superconductors, as well as $\beta$-pyrochlore oxides $KOs_2O_6$. Finally, in section V, we give a conclusion.

