\begin{abstract}
% Retrieval Augmented Generation systems, where an LLM is augmented with external knowledge source have rapidly emerged as popular solutions to improve reliability of LLM generations. 
% % are being extensively deployed to overcome challenges in deploying LLM based assistants. 
% However, efficient serving of these systems is challenging, due to  heterogeneous compute pipeline consisting of LLMs, databases and additional compute blocks. To improve efficiency of serving RAG's we present \system. \system is an end-to-end deployment RAG serving system.First it, introduces a flexible interface allowing users to specify RAG pipelines. Next, \system deploys the user provided pipeline user provided pipeline and realizes a corresponding distributed inference pipeline while accounting for scalability properties of each RAG component. Finally, \system includes an online scheduler which continuously tracks the load and progress of requests and minimizes SLO violation by request re-ordering and autoscaling resources. We implement four different RAG systems, compared to commerically available RAG serving systems, \system provides a gain in throughput of over 48\%. Further, \system also reduces SLO violation by around 24\%.
% This heterogeneity in inference pipelines makes inference challenging due to disparate resource requirement and unpredictable execution times. 

Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) has emerged as a new paradigm for enhancing Large Language Model reliability through integration with external knowledge sources. However, efficient deployment of these systems presents significant technical challenges due to their inherently heterogeneous computational pipelines comprising LLMs, databases, and specialized processing components.
We introduce \system, a comprehensive end-to-end RAG serving framework designed to address these efficiency bottlenecks. \system's architecture offers three key innovations: First, it provides a flexible specification interface enabling users to implement custom RAG pipelines. Secondly, it deploys these pipelines as distributed inference systems while optimizing for the unique scalability characteristics of individual RAG components. Third, \system incorporates an online scheduling mechanism that continuously monitors request load and execution progress, dynamically minimizing SLO violations through strategic request prioritization and resource auto-scaling.
Our experimental evaluation across four distinct RAG implementations demonstrates that \system delivers substantial performance improvements over commercial alternatives, achieving throughput gains exceeding 48\% while simultaneously reducing SLO violations by $\sim$24\%.


\end{abstract}

