In addition to method development, algorithm, and theory, our study also contributes to the finance literature, particularly the field of equity premium prediction, in the following aspects. First, we demonstrate that the widely used OLS models can be problematic as the data show clear signs of heteroscedasticity and possible heavy-tailed errors. 	
Second, in terms of prediction, our empirical results show that the proposed method significantly outperforms mean regressions based on either least square or Huber loss for robust estimation, especially for the out-of-sample analysis. %Our method also outperforms several existing quantile regression methods including quantile regression with Lasso penalty and the globally concerned quantile regression \citep{zheng2015globally}.%, and a nonsparse quantile regression with non-crossing quantile constraints \citep{bondell2010noncrossing} \bfred{(include this???)}.
%Compared to the traditional quantile regression with Lasso penalty, the proposed method provides a coherent model that not only selects the same set of predictors across all quantiles, but also significantly reduces the issue of crossing quantiles. 
Finally, our empirical findings offer several insights that may shed new light for domain experts. First, we find little out-of-sample predictability for the full evaluation period (1965-2021) that includes several recessions, while the predictability is modest for periods without major recessions. 
%The selection of some variables are contradictory between proposed method and the stepwise selection approach for OLS model. For example, the term spread (tms) has a selection rate of 99\% based on the proposed method but only 1.5\% based on the stepwise approach. 
Second, in evaluating prediction quantiles, we find that the predictions for higher quantiles are generally the best compared to middle and lower quantiles. Third and most interestingly, we are able to identify reversed relationships between excess return and several predictors from lower to upper quantiles. For instance, we find that \textit{stock variance (svar)} is negatively associated with excess return at lower quantiles but positive at higher quantiles, while existing studies only show a positive relationship \citep{guo2006out}. 

