Recent advances in large language model (LLM) capabilities have enabled the deployment of LLM agents across a wide range of real-world domains, including software engineering, travel planning, and e-commerce \citep{yao2022webshop}. 
As these agents become more prevalent, they will increasingly interact with one another in complex environments, often competing or cooperating to achieve their goals. 
This widespread adoption of multi-agent systems brings new challenges, particularly the risk of \textit{collusion}, in which agents cooperate or reach agreements to limit competition within a market or manipulate outcomes to their advantage, often at the expense of other participants or the market as a whole \citep{klemperer2004auctions, marshall2012economics, mcafee1992bidding, porter1993detection}.

Collusion among AI agents is particularly concerning in market-based settings such as auctions, where the actions of just a few agents can have outsized impacts on prices, resource allocation, and overall market health \citep{calvano2020artificial, dou2024ai, lin2024strategic, shakhgildyan2024algorithmic, banchio2022adaptive, chen2016amazon, brogaard2014highfrequency, foxabbott2024collusion, motwani2024detection}. 
In these settings, agents may engage in bid suppression, deliberately refraining from competitive bidding to allow colluding partners to win at lower prices.
Agents may also engage in market division, where agents allocate specific customers, products, or geographic regions among themselves, each acting as a monopolist in their assigned segment \citep{lin2024strategic, calvano2020artificial}. 
Recent experimental and theoretical work demonstrates that even without explicit communication, reinforcement learning and other adaptive algorithms can learn to coordinate tacitly, achieving supracompetitive profits and undermining market efficiency \citep{calvano2020artificial, dou2024ai, banchio2022adaptive, lin2024strategic, shakhgildyan2024algorithmic, foxabbott2024collusion}. 
These behaviors are difficult to detect, as algorithms can use subtle signals or simply adjust to each other's strategies over time, bypassing traditional safeguards designed to catch human colluders \citep{courthoud2021detection, efimov2024detecting, motwani2024detection}. 

Algorithmic collusion can trigger cascading failures in interconnected markets, as coordinated behavior in one domain may propagate instability or inefficiency to others, as seen in simulations of algorithmic flash crashes and real-world high-frequency trading \citep{dou2024ai, brogaard2014highfrequency, calvano2020artificial, foxabbott2024collusion}. 
For example, the 2010 Flash Crash in US financial markets exemplified this vulnerability; algorithmic trading exacerbated stock price declines and contributed to rapid, systemic market disruption, underscoring how automated, algorithm-driven markets remain susceptible to cascading failures and manipulative behaviors \citep{lange2021systemic}.

% The adaptive and context-aware nature of LLM agents amplifies the risks from collusion. 
% LLM agents dynamically synthesize real-time market data, competitor behavior, and unstructured signals to refine strategies iteratively  \citep{lin2024strategic, dou2024ai}. 
% This enables LLM agents to discover novel coordination mechanisms-such as inferring collusive equilibria from historical price patterns or adjusting bids to avoid detection-while maintaining plausible deniability \citep{courthoud2021detection, agashe2024llm}. 
% Their ability to generalize across tasks and environments further complicates detection, as strategies learned in one market can transfer seamlessly to others  \citep{liu2024llmtrading}.

We focus on simulating continuous double auctions (CDAs), a market mechanism in which multiple buyers and sellers can submit bids and asks at any time, and transactions occur whenever a buyer’s bid meets or exceeds a seller’s ask. 
CDAs serve as an important testing ground for understanding collusion risks because agents can continuously observe and respond to market activity in real time, enabling both the emergence of tacit collusion through price signaling and the detection of such strategies through retaliatory mechanisms \citep{cliff1997zero, brown2009collusion, kwasticasherstyuk2007, vytelingum2006structure}. 
CDAs are widely used in digital platforms and financial markets to match buyers and sellers efficiently \citep{vytelingum2006structure}.  
% Digital platforms like Facebook and Google employ real-time bidding auctions to allocate ad space, while eBay facilitates consumer-to-consumer sales through competitive price discovery \citep{zhang2025trustworthy, dinerstein2018consumer}. 
Stock exchanges rely on double auction formats to determine prices and execute trades, supporting trillions of dollars in transactions each year \citep{vytelingum2006structure, brogaard2014highfrequency}. 
As LLM agents become increasingly integrated into these systems, understanding how their interactions might lead to subtle or overt forms of collusion becomes essential. 
Such behaviors can erode trust in digital marketplaces, reduce economic efficiency, and ultimately harm consumers by driving up prices, limiting choice, and stifling innovation \citep{calvano2020artificial, lin2024strategic, dou2024ai, foxabbott2024collusion}.

In this work, we explore collusion in a continuous double auction environment with multiple buyers and sellers. We focus on collusion in the context of seller behavior, as we are mostly concerned with whether collusive behavior from LLM agents might lead to market outcomes that disproportionately disadvantage buyers.  

We specifically investigate the following research questions:
\begin{enumerate}[label={\textbf{(RQ\arabic*)}}, leftmargin=*, align=left]

\item How does natural language communication between seller agents affect their propensity to collude compared to environments with no communication?
\item How is collusion impacted by model variation?
\item How does environmental pressure, which can take the form of induced pressure from an authority figure or regulatory oversight for collusion, impact seller behavior in simulated markets?
\end{enumerate}

We find that sellers collude significantly more when they are allowed to communicate using natural language (RQ1). 
Additionally, mixed-model groups of seller agents are not always less prone to collusion than single-model groups of sellers (RQ2). 
However, \claude sellers are significantly more likely to compromise with buyers than \gpt sellers are, suggesting that model heterogeneity can still lead to improved buyer outcomes in simulated markets. 
Finally, regulatory oversight reduces the propensity of sellers to collude (RQ3). 
However, agents collude sooner and at higher levels when pressed to make more profit by an authority figure, and they tend to prioritize this over evading oversight.
Our findings highlight important factors that affect the propensity of language models to collude in market environments, which we hope will inform the design of future deployment environments and the development of mitigation techniques for LLM agent collusion.


\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:methodology}

\subsection{Environment}
\label{sec:environment}

\begin{figure*}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{figures/cda-overview.pdf}
    \caption{Overview of the continuous double auction environment. In each round, buyers and sellers can plan and send messages before submitting bids and asks. The bid and ask queues get updated, and trades are executed according to the auction mechanism. Outcomes from round $r$ are provided as input to the buyers and sellers in round $r+1$.}
    \label{fig:cda-env}
\end{figure*}

We model a continuous double auction environment (\autoref{fig:cda-env}) where LLM agents play the role of buyers and sellers. 
Each agent represents a unique industrial corporation participating in the auction of unspecified heavy metals at the Chicago Mercantile Exchange.
Sellers value each lot of metal at \$80, while buyers value each lot at \$100.\footnote{We left the nature and quantity of metals underspecified on purpose, to prevent conflation with real-world market prices, which the LLM agents may be aware of.}

Following prior work \citep{fish2024algorithmic, lin2024strategic, wu-etal-2024-shall}, the default goal given to agents is to secure maximum profitability for the entity they represent.

