\section{Results and Discussion}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Seller Communication}

\paragraph{Sellers collude more when they are allowed to communicate.}

Sellers tend to attempt coordination significantly more often when they are allowed to send messages (\autoref{fig:coord-scores}). 
Additionally, when communication is enabled, sellers on average consistently set high ask prices, above the competitive equilibrium price, with ask dispersion decreasing over time (\autoref{fig:ask-dispersion}).
In other words, sellers' ask prices become increasingly similar throughout the auction, suggesting cooperative behavior in terms of pricing behavior, in addition to coordination dispositions in their CoTs. 
% Finally, we find that sellers on average make more profit at the end of 30 rounds when they are not allowed to communicate, even though their average trade prices and profits per round are lower. 
Finally, we find that although sellers that are able to communicate make less total profit by the end of the 30 auction rounds (\autoref{tab:trade-profits}), 
they are able to convert more of their higher ask prices to profit towards the end of the auction (\autoref{fig:profit-price-ratio}).

Our results in this experiment align with existing research on the role of natural language communication in similar settings. 
Similar to \citet{wu-etal-2024-shall}, we find that in scenarios where communication among sellers is allowed, sellers converge at significantly higher prices than cases in which sellers are not allowed to communicate.
However, we find that without communication enabled, sellers do not price supracompetitively after the first 10 rounds of the auction (though they do display cooperative tendencies, as indicated by the average coordination scores). 


\subsection{Model Variation}

\paragraph{Seller coordination and pricing behavior is affected by model choice.} 
We find that \claude sellers are more willing to compromise with buyers than \gpt sellers, such as by setting lower ask prices to secure trades. 
\gpt sellers coordinate with each other more compared to \claude sellers, and also achieve more profit than \claude sellers.
Furthermore, \gpt sellers consistently price supracompetitively (\autoref{fig:ask-dispersion}).
% When comparing \gpt and \claude buyers, Claude buyers consistently allow for a higher clearing price than GPT buyers. 
% More generally, in situations with \gpt sellers and \claude buyers or vice versa, \claude agents allow for \gpt agents to extract more surplus. 
When there is a combination of \gpt and \claude sellers, sellers are able to achieve the highest total profit overall while maintaining higher dispersion over the first third of the session due to differing pricing strategies, with both the seller ask prices and average trade price converging just below the competitive equilibrium. 
Prior work has shown that seller heterogeneity could lead to more robust market outcomes overall \citep{hammond2025multi}, a finding that our results from this experiment complement.

