\section{Reliability of LLM Judgments}
\label{app:llm-reliability}
\subsection{Motivation}

In our setup, we employ an LLM-as-a-judge to score the level of seller coordination based on their reasoning traces. 
These scores are assigned on an ordinal scale from 1 to 4 and influence both our per-round coordination metrics and overall conclusions about agent behavior. 
Prior work has demonstrated the potential and limitations of using LLMs as evaluators \citep{zheng2023judging, bedemariam2025potentialperilslargelanguage, thakur2025judgingjudgesevaluatingalignment}, and emphasizes the importance of measuring not just validity but reliability—the consistency of judgments across repeated evaluations.

Following best practices in recent literature \citep{schroeder2024trust, yamauchi2025empiricalstudyllmasajudgedesign}, we treat the LLM as a stochastic evaluator and quantify its reliability using two complementary classes of metrics: internal-consistency reliability and inter-replica agreement.

\subsection{Methodology}

To assess reliability, we randomly sampled 500 uncorrelated rounds from our full corpus of 11,250 simulation rounds and obtained 10 independent judgments for each using \texttt{GPT-4.1-mini}, the same evaluator model employed throughout our main experiments. 
All judgments were generated at a temperature of 0.1 (as in the main experiments), using a different random seed for each of the 10 replications per round. 
The ordinal scores from the judgments were then compiled into a $500 \times 10$ matrix.

\paragraph{(A) Internal-Consistency Reliability}  
We compute \textbf{McDonald’s Omega ($\omega$)} as our primary internal consistency measure. 
Omega reflects the proportion of variance in the aggregate score that can be attributed to a common latent factor—in this case, the LLM's scoring rubric. Compared to Cronbach's Alpha, Omega handles non-tau-equivalent loadings and provides a more accurate estimate in factor-analytic settings \citep{McNeish2018-wm}.

We compute $\omega$ using the \texttt{reliabiliPy} Python package \citep{rafael_valero_fernandez_2022_5830894}, which estimates polychoric correlations and fits a single-factor model to yield both $\omega_{\text{total}}$ and Cronbach’s $\alpha$.

\paragraph{(B) Inter-Rater Agreement}
To complement internal-consistency metrics, we calculate \textbf{Krippendorff’s Alpha ($\alpha$)} with ordinal distance weighting. 
This measure evaluates agreement among the 10 replica raters, correcting for chance and accounting for the ordinal scale. 
Unlike Cohen’s or Fleiss’ $\kappa$, Krippendorff’s $\alpha$ generalizes to multiple raters and gracefully handles missing values, making it especially suitable for LLM-generated outputs. 
We computed this measure using the Fast Krippendorff Python library \citep{castro-2017-fast-krippendorff}.

\subsection{Results}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{McDonald’s Omega ($\omega_{\text{total}}$)}: 0.996
  \item \textbf{Cronbach’s Alpha ($\alpha$)}: 0.995
  \item \textbf{Krippendorff’s Alpha (ordinal)}: 0.948
\end{itemize}

These values indicate extremely high reliability. The $\omega$ and $\alpha$ scores confirm that our LLM judge applies a stable rubric across replications, while Krippendorff’s $\alpha$ suggests that seed-to-seed agreement remains well above chance, even on a moderately fine-grained ordinal scale.

Our findings indicate that the judge model behaves reliably and deterministically under our prompt and temperature setting. 
This supports our use of an LLM as a judge for measuring seller coordination. 
Moreover, it demonstrates that meaningful judgment reliability can be achieved without human raters, provided the LLM prompt is well-calibrated.


