\subsection{The wealth process}
We now work towards developing self-financing wealth dynamics for the wealth process $W^Q(t) = Q(t)^\top P(t)$. Unlike the frictionless setting, there is ambiguity regarding what dynamics a self-financing wealth equation should satisfy as the orders themselves influence the execution price. To derive the wealth equation we follow an approximation approach by first analyzing absolutely continuous trading strategies and then continuously extending the wealth equation to general semimartingale trading strategies.\footnote{This approach is, by now, standard in the literature (see \cite{ackermann2021cadlag,hey.al.23,brokmann2024efficient}).} Indeed, a strategy of the type $dQ_t = \dot Q_tdt$ for some trading rate $\dot Q_t$ has unambiguous wealth dynamics
\[dW^Q(t) = Q(t)^\top dP(t) = Q(t)^\top dS(t) + Q(t)^\top dI(t) = Q(t)^\top dS(t) + d(Q(t)^\top I(t))-I(t)^\top dQ(t),\]
where in the last equality we integrated by parts. This relationship for absolutely continuous trading strategies can be derived by taking a continuous-time limit from a discrete-time trading model. For the reader's benefit a self-contained derivation is contained in Appendix~\ref{app:wealth_proofs}. Using the definition of $I_i$ and the dynamics of $Q_i$ we obtain 
\[dW^Q(t) = Q(t)^\top dS(t) + d(Q(t)^\top I(t)) - \sum_{i=1}^d \frac{h_i(t,J_i(t))}{K_i(t,t)}(dJ_i(t)-b^J_i(t,Q_{[0,t]})dt). \]
Moreover, $J_i$ is itself an absolutely continuous process because $Q_i$ is. 
To make further progress we note that the dynamics of the process $H_i(t,J_i(t))/K_i(t,t)$, where $H_i(t,x) = \int_0^x h_i(t,y)dy$ are given by
\[d\left(\frac{H_i(t,J_i(t))}{K_i(t,t)}\right) = \frac{h_i(t,J_i(t))}{K_i(t,t)}dJ_i(t) + \left(\frac{\partial_t H_i(t,J_i(t))}{K_i(t,t)} - \frac{\partial_t K_i(t,t) + \partial_sK_i(t,t)}{K_i^2(t,t)}H_i(t,J_i(t))\right)dt. \] Subbing in for the wealth dynamics yields
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:wealth_smooth}
\begin{split}
    dW^Q(t) & =  Q(t)^\top dS(t) +d(Q(t)^\top I(t)) - \sum_{i=1}^d d\left(\frac{H_i(t,J_i(t))}{K_i(t,t)}\right) \\
    & \quad - \sum_{i=1}^d\left(\frac{\partial_t H_i(t,J_i(t))}{K_i(t,t)} - \frac{\partial_t K_i(t,t) + \partial_sK_i(t,t)}{K_i^2(t,t)}H_i(t,J_i(t)) -\frac{h_i(t,J_i(t))}{K_i(t,t)}b^J_i(t,Q_{[0,t]})\right)dt. 
\end{split}
\end{equation}
We have now found a representation for the wealth equation that doesn't involve integrals against $Q_i$ or $J_i$. As such we are able to use equation \eqref{eqn:wealth_smooth} to continuously extend the wealth equation to continuous semimartingale strategies $Q$. Then, reversing the above integration by parts and chain rule computations using It\^o's formula, leads us to the general wealth dynamics, which we state in the following proposition.
% We note that placing bulk trades at time zero, $\Delta Q(0)$, is permitted so we denote by $Q(0-),J(0-)$ and $W^Q(0-)$ the initial holdings, initial impact state and investor's wealth prior to any trades being placed.  
The proof of this proposition is contained in Appendix~\ref{app:wealth_proofs}.

\begin{prop}[Wealth equation] \label{thm:wealth}
Let $Q$ be a continuous semimartingale holdings process and suppose the investor has initial wealth $w = W^Q(0) > 0$. The the impact process $I$ of \eqref{eqn:impact} is also a continuous semimartingale and for $T \geq 0$ the investor's wealth is given by
\begin{equation} \label{eqn:wealth}
    W^Q(T) = w + \int_{0}^T Q(t)^\top dP(t) + \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^d [I_i,Q_i](T). 
    \end{equation}
\end{prop}

