\section{Additively functionally generated portfolios} \label{sec:functionally_generated}
In this section we start by introducing additive functionally generated trading strategies. We again assume that the price process is strictly positive. 
\begin{defn}[Additive functional generation of trading strategies] We say that a trading strategy $Q$ is \emph{time-dependent additively functionally generated} by a function $G:[0,\infty) \times \Delta^{d-1}_+ \to \R$ if the relative wealth process admits the pathwise decomposition
\begin{equation} \label{eqn:master_formula}
    V^Q(t) = 1 + G(t,\mu(t)) - G(0,\mu(0)) + \Gamma(t); \quad t \geq 0,
\end{equation}
for some process $\Gamma$ of finite variation satisfying $\Gamma(0) = 0$. If $G$ is independent of the first argument we simply say $Q$ is \emph{additively functionally generated}.
\end{defn}
 An attractive feature of functionally generated portfolios, as can be observed from the  so called master formula \eqref{eqn:master_formula}, is that its relative portfolio wealth can be decomposed into (i) a deterministic function of the current state of the market $\mu(t)$ and (ii) a finite variation term capturing the historical evolution. In particular, stochastic integration has been removed and this allows for the study of the wealth process using techniques that are not available for general portfolios. We take up this analysis in Section~\ref{sec:relarb}, which concerns constructing relative arbitrage with respect to the market portfolio in the presence of price impact.

\subsection{The frictionless setting} \label{sec:frictionless}
Additively generated portfolios were introduced in \cite{karatzas2017trading} for the frictionless setting. We briefly review some of the main results without price impact before tackling the general case in the following subsections. Without trading frictions the relative wealth dynamics \eqref{eqn:relative_wealth} simplify to
\begin{equation} \label{eqn:frictionless_relative_wealth}
    V^Q(T) = 1 + \sum_{i=1}^d\int_0^T \frac{Q_i(t)\overline \bfp(0)}{wN_i} d\mu_i(t). 
\end{equation}
% Since there is no impact, $P= S$ is a continuous semimartingale and, consequently, so is $\mu$.
From here it is clear that if $\frac{\overline \bfp (0)}{wN_i} Q_i(t) = \partial_i G(t,\mu(t))$ for some function $G$ and every $i$ then by applying It\^o's formula to $G(t,\mu(t))$ we will obtain that $Q$ is functionally generated. Moreover, since $\sum_{i=1}^d d\mu_i = 0$, the more general form $Q(t) =  \frac{wN_i}{\overline \bfp(0)}\nabla G(t,\mu(t)) + C(t){\bf 1}_d$ for some scalar process $C$ and where ${\bf 1}_d$ is the $d$-dimensional vector of all ones would lead to the same result. The inclusion of an appropriate scalar process $C$ is important to ensure the self-financing condition $Q(t)^\top P(t) = W^Q(t)$ holds.

This brings us to the following result, which is effectively a time-dependent version of Propositions~4.3 and 4.5 from \cite{karatzas2017trading} in the case of a smooth generating function. In the sequel we write  $G \in C^{1,k}((0,\infty)\times \Delta^{d-1}_+)$ if $G$ is once continuously differentiable in the first argument and can be continuously extended to a open set in $\R^d$ containing $\Delta^{d-1}_+$, with the continuous extension being $k$-times continuously differentiable in the second argument. The continuous extension is needed because $\Delta^{d-1}_+$ is a $(d-1)$-dimensional manifold in $\R^d$.
\begin{prop}[Additive generation in frictionless markets] \label{prop:additive_frictionless}
Let $G \in C^{1,2}([0,\infty)\times \Delta^{d-1}_+)$ be given and suppose that $Q$ is given by
\begin{equation} \label{eqn:additive_holdings}
    Q_i(t) = \frac{wN_i}{\overline \bfp (0)}(\partial_iG(t,\mu(t)) + V^Q(t) - \nabla G(t,\mu(t))^\top \mu(t)), \qquad i=1,\dots,d, \quad t\geq 0.
\end{equation}
Then $Q$ is time-dependent additively generated by $G$ with $\Gamma$ process
\begin{equation} \label{eqn:Gamma_frict}
    \Gamma(t) = -\int_0^t \partial_tG(s,\mu(s))ds -\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i,j=1}^d\int_0^t  \partial_{ij}G(s,\mu(s))d[\mu_i,\mu_j](s).
\end{equation}
In particular, if $G$ is nonincreasing in the first argument and concave in the second then $\Gamma$ is a nonnegative process. If, additionally, $G(0,\mu(0))\leq 1$ and $G(t,\cdot)$ is a nonnegative function for every $t \geq 0$ then $V^Q$ is nonnegative and $Q_i$ is nonnegative for every $i=1,\dots,d$. 
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
    The functional generation claim follows directly from \eqref{eqn:frictionless_relative_wealth} and It\^o's formula. If $t\mapsto G(t,\mu)$ is nonincreasing and $\mu \mapsto G(t,\mu)$ is concave then both integral terms in the representation for $\Gamma$ are nonnegative so that $\Gamma$ is too. 

    Next we suppose that $G(0,\mu(0)) \leq 1$ and $G(t,\cdot)$ is nonnegative for every $t \geq 0$.
    From the master formula \eqref{eqn:master_formula}, nonnegativity of $\Gamma$ and nondecreasingness of $G(\cdot,\mu)$ we have
    \[V^Q(t) = 1 + G(t,\mu(t)) - G(0,\mu(0))  + \Gamma(t) \geq G(t,\mu(t))  \geq 0, \]
        establishing the nonnegativity claim for $V^Q$. To obtain nonnegativity of $Q$ we recall \eqref{eqn:additive_holdings} which yields
        \begin{align}
            Q_i(t) & = \frac{wN_i}{\overline \bfp(0)}\left(\partial_i G(t,\mu(t)) + V^Q(t) - \nabla G(t,\mu(t))^\top \mu(t)\right) \nonumber \\
            & \geq  \frac{wN_i}{\overline \bfp(0)}\left(\partial_i G(t,\mu(t)) +  G(t,\mu(t))  - \nabla G(t,\mu(t))^\top \mu(t)\right), \label{eqn:Qi_bound}
        \end{align}
        where we again used nonnegativity of $\Gamma$ and that $G(0,\mu(0)) \leq 1$. The nonnegativity of $G(t,\cdot)$ and concavity of $G(t,\cdot)$ guarantee that the right hand side of \eqref{eqn:Qi_bound} is nonnegative as shown in the proof of \cite[Proposition~4.5]{karatzas2017trading} (see equation (7.7) in their paper). This establishes nonnegativity of $Q_i$ and completes the proof.
\end{proof}

As a consequence of the master formula \eqref{eqn:master_formula} we get the entirely explicit, in terms of $\mu$ and $G$, expression for the holdings process
\begin{equation} \label{eqn:frictionless_additive_holdings}
\begin{aligned}
    Q_i(t) & = \frac{wN_i}{\overline \bfp (0)} \Bigg(\partial_{i}G(t,\mu(t)) + 1 + G(t,\mu(t)) - G(0,\mu(0)) - \nabla G(t,\mu(t))^\top \mu(t) \\
& \hspace{1.75cm}  -\int_0^t \partial_tG(s,\mu(s))ds -\frac{1}{2}\sum_{j,k=1}^d\int_0^t \partial_{jk}G(s,\mu(s))d[\mu_j,\mu_k](s)\Bigg).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
From here we see that the constant function $G(\mu) = 1$ generates the market portfolio. Other commonly seen time-independent concave generating functions include (see \cite{fernholz2018volatility})
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item the \emph{diversity-$p$} function $G(\mu) = (\sum_{i=1}^d \mu_i^p)^{1/p}$ for parameter $p \in (0,1)$,
    \item the \emph{geometric weighted mean} $G(\mu) = \prod_{i=1}^d \mu_i^{p_i}$ for constants $p_i > 0$ with $p_1+\dots +p_d = 1$,
    \item the \emph{additively symmetric} function $G(\mu) = \sum_{i=1}^d g(\mu_i)$ for a concave scalar function $g$. 
\end{itemize}
A particular choice of additively symmetric generating function is the \emph{quadratic generation function} obtained by taking $g(x) = \frac{1}{d}-\frac{1}{2}x^2$ so that $G(\mu) = 1 - \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^d\mu_i^2$. This generating function will play an important role in Section~\ref{sec:relarb}. Another choice is $g(x) = -x\log x$, leading to the \emph{entropy generating function} $G(\mu) = \sum_{i=1}^d -\mu_i\log\mu_i$, which for additively generated portfolios was studied in \cite{karatzas2017trading}.

