We also recall the quantity $b_i^J(t,\omega)$ defined by \eqref{eqn:bJ}.
To formulate the coefficients that appear in the dynamics of $P$, we will need invertibility of $A^P$ given by \eqref{eqn:AP}. It turns out, in the setting with (nontrivial) price impact, this is equivalent to a condition on $G$ akin to directional concavity. This is the content of the following proposition, whose proof is in Appendix~\ref{sec:proof_AP}.
% Take $x = \sum_{i=1}^d c_iu_i$ for orthonormal eigenvector $u_i$ and note that $c_d = 0$ if $u_d = {\bf 1}_d$
\begin{prop}[Invertibility of $A^P$]
\label{prop:AP}Suppose $G(t,\cdot) \in C^2(\Delta^{d-1}_+)$ for every $t \geq 0$ and satisfies
\begin{equation}
\label{eqn:quadratic_form}
    x^\top \nabla^2 G(t,\mu) x \leq 0, \qquad \text{whenever} \qquad x^\top {\bf 1}_d = 0, \qquad \text{ for all } \quad t \geq 0, \quad \mu \in \Delta^{d-1}_+.
\end{equation} Then given arbitrary $(t,p,J) \in [0,\infty) \times (0,\infty)^d \times \R^d$, we have that all of the eigenvalues of $A^P(t,p,J)$ are real and greater than or equal to one. In particular, $A^P(t,p,J)$ is invertible.

Conversely, suppose $G(t^*,\cdot)$ does not satisfy \eqref{eqn:quadratic_form} for some $t^* \geq 0$. If there exists a $J^* \in \R^d$ such that $\partial_x h_i(t^*,J^*_i) > 0$ for every $i=1,\dots,d$ then there exists a $p^* \in (0,\infty)^d$ such that $A^P(t^*,p^*,J^*)$ is not invertible.
\end{prop}
Clearly a sufficient condition for \eqref{eqn:quadratic_form} to hold is for $G(t,\cdot)$ to be concave for every $t \geq 0$.
With the previous result in hand we can define the SDE coefficients assuming $G$ satisfies \eqref{eqn:quadratic_form}.
 Below we use the notation $u \circ v$ for componentwise product of vectors, $(u\circ v)_i = u_iv_i$. We also use the notation $u_{\cdot jk}$ for a $d$-dimensional vector with components $(u_{ijk};i=1,\dots,d)$. For $(t,p,J,\omega) \in [0,\infty) \times (0,\infty)^d \times \R^d \times (C(0,\infty);\R^d)$ we define
\begin{subequations} \label{eqn:SDE_coefficients}
\begin{eqnarray}
    &\beta^P(t,p,J)  & = (A^P)^{-1}(t,p,J) \\
    &\alpha^P(t,p,J,\omega)  & = \beta^P(t,p,J)\Big(\partial_t h(t,J) + \partial_x h(t,J) \circ b^J(t,\omega)  \\
    &&\qquad \qquad \quad 
    + \frac{w}{\overline \bfp(p^0)}\big(\partial_x h(t,J) \circ K(t,t) \circ N \circ (\overrightarrow{\nabla \partial_t G})^{\mu(p)}(t,\mu(p))\big)\Big) \\
    &\gamma^P_{\cdot jk}(t,p,J)  &= \beta^P(t,p,J)\widetilde\gamma^P_{\cdot jk}(t,p,J) \\
    &\widetilde \gamma^P_{ijk}(t,p,J)   &=  \partial_x h_i(t,J_i) K_i(t,t)\sum_{\ell,m=1}^d \Upsilon_{ilm}(t,p,J)\beta^P_{j\ell}(t,p,J)\beta^P_{km}(t,p,J) \\
     &&\qquad + \frac{1}{2}\partial_{xx}h_i (t,J_i)K_i^2(t,t)(A^Q\beta^P)_{ij}(t,p,J)(A^Q\beta^P)_{i k}(t,p,J) \\
    &\beta^Q(t,p,J)  &=  A^Q(t,p)\beta^P(t,p,J) \\
    &\alpha^Q(t,p,J,\omega)  &= A^Q(t,p)\alpha^P(t,p,J,\omega) + \frac{w}{\overline \bfp(p^0)}N \circ (\overrightarrow{\nabla \partial_tG})^{\mu(p)}(t,\mu(p)) \\
    &\gamma^Q(t,p,J)_{ijk} & = (A^Q(t,p)\gamma^P_{\cdot jk}(t,p,J))_i  + \sum_{\ell,m=1}^d \Upsilon_{ilm}(t,p,J)\beta^P_{j\ell}(t,p,J)\beta^P_{km}(t,p,J)\\
   & \beta^J(t,p,J)  &= \mathrm{diag}(K(t,t)) \beta^Q(t,p,J) \\
    &\alpha^J(t,p,J,\omega)  &= \mathrm{diag}(K(t,t)) \alpha^Q(t,p,J,\omega) + b^J(t,\omega) \\
    &\gamma^J_{\cdot jk}(t,p,J)  &= \mathrm{diag}(K(t,t)) \gamma_{\cdot jk}^Q(t,p,J).
\end{eqnarray}
\end{subequations}
The corresponding coupled SDE for $(P,Q,J)$ is then given by 
\begin{subequations} \label{eqn:SDE}
    \begin{align} 
 dP_i(t) &  = \alpha^P_i(t,P(t),J(t),Q_{[0,t]})dt + \sum_{j=1}^d\beta^P_{ij}(t,P(t),J(t))dS_j(t) + \sum_{j,k=1}^d\gamma^P_{ijk}(t,P(t),J(t))d[S_j,S_k](t) \raisetag{0.3cm}\label{eqn:SDEP} \\
 dQ_i(t) &  = \alpha^Q_i(t,P(t),J(t),Q_{[0,t]})dt + \sum_{j=1}^d\beta^Q_{ij}(t,P(t),J(t))dS_j(t) +\sum_{j,k=1}^d\gamma^Q_{ijk}(t,P(t),J(t))d[S_j,S_k](t) \raisetag{0.3cm}\label{eqn:SDEQ}  \\
 dJ_i(t) &  = \alpha^J_i(t,P(t),J(t),Q_{[0,t]})dt + \sum_{j=1}^d\beta^J_{ij}(t,P(t),J(t))dS_j(t) + \sum_{j,k=1}^d\gamma^J_{ijk}(t,P(t),J(t))d[S_j,S_k](t). \raisetag{0.3cm}
\label{eqn:SDEJ}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
We consider this SDE with initial condition $(P(0),Q(0),J(0)) = (p^0,q^0,J^0) \in (0,\infty)^d \times \R^d \times \R^d$, where $q^0_i = \frac{wN_i}{\overline \bfp(0)}(\partial_i G(0,\mu(p^0)) + 1 - \nabla G(0,\mu(p^0))^\top \mu(p^0))$ to be consistent with \eqref{eqn:q0}.

We can now state our main theorem. The derivation of the SDE is contained in Appendix~\ref{app:SDE_derivation}, while the proof of this theorem is contained in Appendix~\ref{sec:main_proof}.
\begin{thm}[Additive portfolio generation with price impact] \label{thm:main} Let $G \in C^{1,3}((0,\infty)\times \Delta^{d-1}_+)$ be given and additionally assume that the third derivatives $\partial_{ijk}G$ are locally Lipschitz continuous and that the mixed derivatives $\partial_{it} G$ exist and are locally Lipschitz continuous. We further assume that $G(t,\cdot)$ satisfies \eqref{eqn:quadratic_form} for every $t \geq 0$. 
% $G(t,\cdot)$ is a concave function for every $t \geq 0$. 
% and $u,v$ are vector valued functions with components given by\[u_i(t,J) = \frac{N_ih_i(t,J_i)}{K_i(t,t)}, \qquad v_i(t,p,J) = u_i(t,J)\sum_{k=1}^d \partial_{ik}G(t,\mu(p))\mu_k(p). \] 
Then 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \label{item:SDE} the SDE \eqref{eqn:SDE} has a pathwise unique strong solution on the stochastic time interval $[0,\xi)$, where  \begin{equation} \label{eqn:xi}
        \xi = \inf\{t \geq 0: \mu_i(t) = 0 \text{ for some } i \in \{1,\dots,d\} \text{ or } \overline \bfp(t) = 0\}
    \end{equation}
     is the explosion time,
    \item \label{item:right_process}the processes $P$, $J$ and $Q$ of part \ref{item:SDE} are the unique semimartingale processes satisfying $P(t) = S(t) + I(t)$ for $t \in [0,\xi)$, where $I$ is given by \eqref{eqn:impact} and $Q$ is given by \eqref{eqn:additive_Q_explicit},
    \item \label{item:Q_additive} $Q$ is additively functionally generated by $G$ on $[0,\xi)$ and its wealth process $V^Q$ satisfies \eqref{eqn:additive_master_impact},
    \item \label{item:long-only} if $G(\cdot, \mu)$ is nonincreasing for every $\mu \in \Delta^{d-1}_+$ and $G(t,\cdot)$ is concave for every $t \geq 0$ then the process $\Gamma$ of \eqref{eqn:master_formula} is nondecreasing. If, additionally, $G(0,\mu(p^0)) \leq 1$ and $G$ is nonnegative then on its lifetime $[0,\xi)$, $V^Q$ is nonnegative and $Q_i$ is nonnegative for every $i=1,\dots,d$,
    \item \label{item:positive_price} 
    % if for every $T > 0$, $\sup_{t \in [0,T]}\sup_{\mu \in \Delta^{d-1}_+}\partial_t G(t,\mu) < \infty$ and $G$ satisfies 
    Suppose $G$ is time homogeneous and concave. Set $F_i(\mu) = \partial_i G(\mu) + G(\mu) - \nabla G(\mu)^\top \mu$ and $\overline F_i = \sup_{\mu \in \Delta^{d-1}_+}F_i(\mu)$. If for every $i \in \{1,\dots,d\}$ we have
    \begin{equation} \label{eqn:positive_condition}
    \lim_{n \to \infty} \mu_i^n = 0 \implies 
         \lim_{n \to \infty} F_i(\mu^n) = \overline F_i
    \end{equation} for any sequence $(\mu^n)_{n \in \N} \subset \Delta^{d-1}_+$ then $\xi = \inf\{t \geq 0: \overline \bfp(t) = 0 \text{ or } \overline \bfp(t) = \infty\}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{thm} The examples introduced in Section~\ref{sec:frictionless} all satisfy the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}. Moreover, the diversity-$p$, entropy and geometric weighted mean functions additionally satisfy \eqref{eqn:positive_condition}.
Several remarks are in order.
\begin{remark}
    The pathwise unique strong solution to \eqref{eqn:SDE} is entirely driven by the fundamental price $S$. That is, once a generating function $G$ is chosen the processes $P,Q$ and $J$ can be viewed as functions of the path of $S$. This representation is particularly useful for backtests and trading simulations. Indeed, given a (discretized) trajectory of $S$, the Euler discretization of \eqref{eqn:SDE} provides a straightforward way to simulate the evolution of the price, impact, holdings processes and, as a result, also the investor's relative wealth process $V^Q$ given by \eqref{eqn:additive_master_impact}. We present simulation experiments in Section~\ref{sec:numerics}.
\end{remark}
% \begin{remark}
%     From the representation \eqref{eqn:additive_Q_explicit} it is clear that the holdings $Q$ are entirely determined by the trajectories of $P$ and $J$; i.e.\ $Q(t) = \Psi(t,P_.,J_.)$ for some function $\Psi$. As such the SDE \eqref{eqn:SDE} can, in principle, be written in terms of only $P$ and $J$. However, the form of the SDE used in \eqref{eqn:SDE} with the extra state variables $Q$ allow for a more standard application of SDE existence theorems.
% \end{remark}
\begin{remark}
    In the case of time-independent linear impact $h_i(t,x) = \lambda_ix$ for every $i$ the SDE coefficients \eqref{eqn:SDE_coefficients} become independent of $J$. As such,  the SDE reduces to a $2d$-dimensional equation for $P$ and $Q$ \eqref{eqn:SDEP}-\eqref{eqn:SDEQ} with $J$ given by the right-hand side of \eqref{eqn:SDEJ}.
\end{remark}
\begin{remark}
    Depending on the choice of function $G$, the explosion time $\xi$ for the SDE \eqref{eqn:SDE} may not be maximal. For example, if $G(t,\cdot)$ is a function which is actually $C^{3,1}$ on all of $\R^d$, not just on $\Delta^{d-1}_+$, then the SDE solution can continue to exist even if $\mu_i$ (and hence $P_i$) becomes negative. This process, however, loses the interpretation of being a stock price so we stick with the explosion time $\xi$ given by \eqref{eqn:xi}. 
\end{remark}
    By examining the SDE dynamics in \eqref{eqn:SDE}, as well as the computations leading to its derivation contained in Appendix~\ref{app:SDE_derivation}, it is clear that if the set $\{(t,p,J): A^P(t,p,J) \text{ is not invertible}\}$ is nonempty (equivalent to $G$ failing to satisfy \eqref{eqn:quadratic_form} by Proposition~\ref{prop:AP}) then the SDE coefficients would not be defined everywhere. Even if they are defined almost everywhere, they would no longer be locally Lipschitz continuous leading to difficulties studying the well-posedness of the SDE \eqref{eqn:SDE}. Moreover, if this set of noninvertible points is sufficiently large and visited by the process with positive probability then it is clear that the price process $P$ cannot be a semimartingale. This is in contrast to the frictionless setup, where generic $C^2$ generating functions are easily handled.

On a related note, comparing to the frictionless setting, we see that additional regularity, regarding third derivatives of $G$, is imposed. This is because in the present setting we need the holdings process $Q$ to be a semimartingale. From \eqref{eqn:additive_Q_explicit} we see that $Q_i(t)$ is defined through $\partial_i G(t,\mu(t))$ so we require $\partial_i G$ to be a $C^{1,2}$ function to ensure semimartingality of $Q_i$.

In the sequel, to simplify the exposition and avoid making statements up to an explosion time, we will on occasion make the following assumption.
\begin{assum}[Nonexplosive market] \label{ass:nonexplosion} In the setup of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} assume that $\P(\xi < \infty) = 0$.
\end{assum}
We briefly mention a few sufficient additional conditions that ensure Assumption~\ref{ass:nonexplosion} holds in the case that $G$ satisfies \eqref{eqn:positive_condition}. If $h_i(t,x) = 0$ for some $i$ then $\overline \bfp(t) \geq S_i(t) > 0$. Additionally, if each $h_i$ is bounded by some constant $C_i$ then $\overline \bfp(t) = \sum_{i=1}^d N_i S_i(t) + \sum_{i=1}^d N_ih_i(t,J_i(t)) \leq \sum_{i=1}^d N_i S_i(t) + \sum_{i=1}^d N_iC_i < \infty$. We will not assume these strong conditions directly in the sequel, but will enforce the weaker Assumption~\ref{ass:nonexplosion} in some instances, which we will specify. In Section~\ref{sec:relarb_explicit} we will establish that Assumption~\ref{ass:nonexplosion} holds under additional assumptions on the frictionless market and by imposing certain $L_\infty$ bounds on $G$ and its derivatives. Studying the SDE \eqref{eqn:SDE} and understanding if market explosion is possible in the general case considered in this section remains an interesting problem beyond the scope of this paper.

\section{Relative arbitrage} \label{sec:relarb}
In this section we discuss relative arbitrage in price impact models. That is, whether or not outperformance of the market portfolio \emph{with probability one} over a sufficiently long time horizon is possible. Care is needed when comparing wealth processes of two portfolios to ensure any outperformance is genuine and not attributable to inflated mark-to-market prices caused by the investor's own trades.\footnote{The wealth process $W^Q(t)$ marks-to-market the investor's position using the observed prices $P(t)$, rather than fundamental prices $S(t)$. It is sometimes referred to as \emph{accounting}, rather than \emph{fundamental}, wealth; see \cite[Chapter~2.2]{webster.23}.} This motivates the following definition.
\begin{defn}[Relative arbitrage] Given a time horizon $T > 0$, a trading strategy $Q$ is called a \textit{relative arbitrage} with respect to the market portfolio $Q^\mathcal{M}$ on $[0,T]$ if the following hold
\begin{enumerate}
\item \label{item:liquidate}$Q(0) = Q^\mathcal{M}(0)$ and  $Q(t) = V^Q(T)Q^\mathcal{M}(t)$ for all $t \geq T$,
\item $\P(V^Q(T) \geq 1) = 1$ and  $\P(V^Q(T) >1) > 0$.
\end{enumerate}
If $\P(V^Q(T) > 1) = 1$ then we call $Q$ a \emph{strong relative arbitrage}.
\end{defn}
In essence, a relative arbitrage is a trading strategy which, before initiation and after liquidation, holds the benchmark market portfolio and upon liquidation is guaranteed to outperform the market portfolio. In the case of frictionless markets the first condition \ref{item:liquidate} need not be explicitly stated as instantaneous changes to holdings do not affect the investor's wealth.

\subsection{Implicit conditions for relative arbitrage}

In \cite[Chapter~3]{fernholz2002stochastic} it was shown that under the conditions of \emph{market nondegeneracy} and \emph{market diversity}, relative arbitrage exists in a frictionless market.\footnote{For other sufficient conditions and sharp time horizon bounds for relative arbitrage see \cite{fernholz2018volatility,larsson2021relative}.} We state these conditions here and will show that they imply relative arbitrage in this setting of market impact as well.
\begin{defn}[Market nondegeneracy and market diversity] \label{defn:market_nondegen_diverse}
Given a stopping time $T > 0$ the market is called
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item \emph{nondegenerate} on $[0,T]$ with nondegeneracy constant $\epsilon > 0$ if the log price has absolutely continuous quadratic variation process, $d[\log P](t) = a(t)dt$, and $a$ satisfies the uniformly ellipticity condition,
        \[x^\top a(t) x \geq \epsilon \|x\|^2, \qquad \P\text{-a.s}\]
        for every $t \leq T$ and every $x \in \R^d$,
        \item \emph{diverse} on $[0,T]$ with diversity constant $\delta > 0$ if \[\max_{i=1,\dots,d}\mu_i(t) \leq 1-\delta, \qquad \P\text{-a.s}\]
        for every $t \in [0,T]$.\end{enumerate}
        If $T = \infty$ we simply say the market is nondegenerate and diverse respectively.
\end{defn}
    In nondegenerate and diverse markets, relative arbitrage opportunities can be constructed using functionally generated portfolios. Here we will construct relative arbitrage using the portfolio additively generated by the quadratic generating function. Unlike the frictionless setting, where changes in holdings are cost-free, care is needed to systematically initiate and liquidate in a way that controls the evolution of the relative wealth process. 

    To facilitate this, given a target time horizon $T > 0$ and time homogeneous generating function $H(\mu)$ we will use the time-dependent generating function $G(t,\mu) = \psi(t)H(\mu)$, where $\psi$ is a $C^1$ function that satisfies $\psi(0) =\psi(T) = 0$, $\psi(t) = 1$ for $t \in [T_0,T_1]$, $\psi'(t) > 0$ for  $t \in [0,T_0]$ and $\psi'(0) < 0$ for $t \in [T_1,T]$ where $0 < T_0 < T_1 < T$ are deterministic times. The period $[0,T_0]$ is the \emph{initiation period}, where the investor gradually goes from holding the market portfolio to the target portfolio generated by $H$, while the period $[T_1,T]$ is the \emph{liquidation period} where the investor gradually trades back to the market portfolio. The period $[T_0,T_1]$ will then be referred to as the \emph{main trading period}. For concreteness, given times $T_0,T_1$ and $T$ we use the following choice of $\psi$,
\begin{equation} \label{eqn:psi}
    \psi(t) = 
    \begin{cases}
    \chi\left(\frac{t}{T_0}\right), & 0 \leq t \leq T_1,\\
    1-\chi\left(\frac{t-T_1}{T-T_1}\right), & t \geq T_1,
    \end{cases}\qquad \text{where} \qquad \chi(u) = \begin{cases}
        3u^2-2u^3, & u \leq 1, \\
        1, & u \geq 1.
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
This leads us to the existence of relative arbitrage in the setting with price impact.
\begin{thm}[Relative arbitrage; implicit version] \label{thm:relarb_implicit} Let Assumptions~\ref{ass:impact_inputs} and \ref{ass:nonexplosion} hold. Set
% For any $0 < T_0 < T_1 < T$ we set \begin{equation} \label{eqn:time_dep_entropy}
%     G(t,\mu) = \psi(t)H(\mu), \qquad \text{where}\qquad H(\mu) = -\sum_{i=1}^d\mu_i\log\mu_i
% \end{equation}
\[
    G(t,\mu) = \psi(t)H(\mu), \qquad \text{where} \qquad H(\mu) = 1 - \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^d \mu_i^2
\]
and $\psi$ is given by \eqref{eqn:psi}. We assume the resulting market is nondegenerate and diverse with respective constants $\epsilon$ and $\delta$ independent of the time horizon.

Then there exists a relative arbitrage on $[0,T]$ for any $T > T^* := \frac{2d}{\epsilon \delta^2}$. Concretely, letting $T = \frac{2d}{\epsilon \delta^2} + 2\theta$ for any $\theta > 0$, we have that the strategy additively generated by $G$, with $\psi$ pinned down by setting $T_0 = \theta$ and  $T_1 = \frac{2d}{\epsilon \delta^2} + \theta$, is a relative arbitrage on $[0,T]$.
% Fixing $T = T^* + 3\eta$ for some $\eta > 0$ the trading strategy additively functionally generated by
% \[G(t,\mu) = \psi(t)H(\mu), \qquad \text{where}\qquad H(\mu) = -\sum_{i=1}^d\mu_i\log\mu_i\]
% and $\psi$ is given by \eqref{eqn:psi} with $T_0 = \eta$ and $T_1 = T^*+\eta$,
% is a strong relative arbitrage on $[0,T]$.
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
    We start by noting that $G$ satisfies the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.
    % Moreover, since $\partial_i G(t,\mu) + G(t,\mu) - \nabla G(t,\mu)^\top \mu = - \psi(t)\log \mu_i$ it also satisfies \eqref{eqn:positive_condition}.
    Theorem~\ref{thm:main} together with Assumption~\ref{ass:nonexplosion} guarantees that this function $G$ gives rise to valid and globally defined price, holdings and impact states process $(P,Q,J)$ given by the SDE \eqref{eqn:SDE}. Moreover, $Q$ is additively functionally generated by $G$ and the wealth process $V^Q$ satisfies \eqref{eqn:master_formula} with $\Gamma$ given by \eqref{eqn:Gamma}.
    Next note that $G(\cdot,\mu)$ is nondecreasing on $[0,T_1]$ and nonincreasing on $[T_1,T]$ for each $\mu \in \Delta^{d-1}_+$. As such we see from \eqref{eqn:Gamma} that $\Gamma$ is strictly increasing on $[T_0,T]$ and the only component that contributes to a decrease on $[0,T_0]$ is the term $t \mapsto -\int_{0}^t\partial_t G(s,\mu(s))ds$. Using these observations together with the fact that $G(0,\mu(0)) = G(T,\mu(T)) = 0$ we obtain the bound
    \begin{align*}
        V^Q(T)  = 1+ \Gamma(T) & > 1-  \int_{0}^{T_0}\partial_t G(t,\mu(t))dt - \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i,j=1}^d\int_{T_0}^{T_1}\partial_{ij}G(t,\mu(t))d[\mu_i,\mu_j](t)\\
        & = 1 -\int_{0}^{T_0}\psi'(t)H(\mu(t))dt -\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i,j=1}^d\int_{T_0}^{T_1}\partial_{ij}H(\mu(t))d[\mu_i,\mu_j](t)  \\
        % & \geq 1 - \log (d) + \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^d\int_{T_0}^{T_1}\frac{d[\mu_i](t)}{\mu_i(t)}.
        & \geq \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^d\int_{T_0}^{T_1}d[\mu_i](t).
    \end{align*}
In the final inequality we used that $H$ is bounded by $1$ and that $\psi(0) = 0 \psi(T_0) = 1$ to bound the time integral term. We also used that $\partial_{ij}H(\mu) = -\delta_{ij}$ to simplify the quadratic variation term. 

It now remains to show that $ \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^d\int_{T_0}^{T_1}d[\mu_i](t) \geq 1$ with probability one. Applying It\^o's formula to $\mu_i$ it is easily verified that 
\begin{equation} \label{eqn:mu_QV}
    d[\mu_i](t) = \frac{1}{\overline \bfp^2(t)}(d[\bfp_i](t) - 2\mu_i d[\bfp_i,\overline \bfp](t) + \mu_i^2d[\overline \bfp](t)).
\end{equation} 
It then follows that
% \begin{align*}
%     \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^d\int_{T_0}^{T_1}\frac{d[\mu_i](t)}{\mu_i(t)} & = \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^d\int_{T_0}^{T_1} \frac{1}{\overline \bfp^2(t)\mu_i(t)}d[\bfp_i](t) - \frac{1}{2}\int_{T_0}^{T_1} \frac{1}{\overline \bfp^2(t)}d[\overline\bfp](t) \\
%     & = \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i,j=1}^d\int_{T_0}^{T_1}\mu_i(t)(\delta_{ij}-\mu_j(t))\frac{d[\bfp_i,\bfp_j](t)}{\bfp_i(t)\bfp_j(t)} \\
%     & = \frac{1}{2}\int_{T_0}^{T_1}\mathrm{Tr}(\Psi(t)d[\log P](t)),
% \end{align*}
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^d\int_{T_0}^{T_1}d[\mu_i](t) &  = \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i,j=1}^d \int_{T_0}^{T_1}\frac{1}{\overline \bfp^2(t)}(\delta_{ij} - 2\mu_i(t) + \|\mu(t)\|^2)d[\bfp_i,\bfp_j](t) \\
& =  \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i,j=1}^d\int_{T_0}^{T_1} \mu_i(t)\mu_j(t)(\delta_{ij} - 2\mu_i(t) + \|\mu(t)\|^2)\frac{d[\bfp_i,\bfp_j](t)}{\bfp_i(t)\bfp_j(t)} \\
& =  \frac{1}{2}\int_{T_0}^{T_1}\mathrm{Tr}(\Psi(t)d[\log \bfp ](t)),
\end{align*}
% where $\Psi_{ij}(t) = \mu_i(t)(\delta_{ij} -\mu_j(t))$ 
where $\Psi_{ij}(t) = \mu_i\mu_j(\partial_{ij}-2\mu_i+\|\mu\|^2)$
and $\delta_{ij}$ is the Kronecker delta. By market nondegeneracy we have that $d[\log \bfp](t) = d[\log P](t) \geq \epsilon dt$. Hence, we obtain
% \[ \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^d\int_{T_0}^{T_1}\frac{d[\mu_i](t)}{\mu_i(t)} \geq \frac{\epsilon}{2}\int_{T_0}^{T_1}\mathrm{Tr}(\Psi(t))dt = \frac{\epsilon}{2}\int_{T_0}^{T_1}\sum_{i=1}^d \mu_i(t)(1-\mu_i(t))dt \geq \frac{\epsilon \delta}{2}(T_1-T_0) = \log d,\]
\begin{align*}
    \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^d\int_{T_0}^{T_1}d[\mu_i](t) & \geq \frac{\epsilon}{2}\int_{T_0}^{T_1}\mathrm{Tr}(\Psi(t))dt = \frac{\epsilon}{2}\int_{T_0}^{T_1}\sum_{i=1}^d\mu_i^2(t)(1-2\mu_i(t)+\|\mu(t)\|^2)dt \\
    & \geq \frac{\epsilon}{2}\sum_{i=1}^d \int_{T_0}^{T_1}\mu_i^2(t)(1-\mu_i(t))^2dt \geq \frac{\epsilon\delta^2}{2d}(T_1-T_0) \geq 1,
\end{align*}
where in the second to last inequality we used the diversity conditions, which asserts that $1-\mu_i(t) \geq \delta$ and bounded $\|\mu(t)\|^2$ from below by $\mu_i^2(t)$. This completes the proof.
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
    The proof only required diversity and nondegeneracy to hold up to the time $T_1$.
\end{remark}

The conclusions of Theorem~\ref{thm:relarb_implicit} continue to hold with other generating functions $H$. For example it can similarly be shown that with the entropy function $H(\mu) = - \sum_{i=1}^d \mu_i\log \mu_i$ relative arbitrage can be achieved by the smaller time horizon $T = \frac{2\log d}{\delta \epsilon}$. However, we stick to the the quadratic generating function in Theorem~\ref{thm:relarb_implicit} as it has bounded derivatives and this will let us obtain a refined version of this result in the next subsection. 

\subsection{Explicit conditions for relative arbitrage} \label{sec:relarb_explicit}
In the previous section we obtained relative arbitrage in the market with price impact, but under \emph{implicit} assumptions on the market. Indeed, we directly assumed that the observed market was nondegenerate, diverse and nonexplosive. However, in our framework, the market with price impact is derived from the baselines frictionless market prices $S$, by the price impact specifications $h,K$ and $J^0$ and by our choice of generating function $G$. In this section we develop explicit conditions on these inputs that guarantee the existence of relative arbitrage. To this end, we make the following definitions.  
\begin{defn}[Bounded volatility]
The market is said to have \emph{bounded volatility} if we have the almost sure bound $\sum_{i,j=1}^d [\log P_i,\log P_j](t) \leq \sigma^2 t$
for every $t \geq 0$ and some $\sigma^2 >0$.
\end{defn}

\begin{defn}[Floor constraints]
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The market capitalizations satisfy a \emph{floor constraint} with  bound $\kappa > 0$ on $[0,T]$ if $\overline \bfp(t) \geq \kappa$ almost surely for every $t \in [0,T]$,
    \item The market weights are said to satisfy a \emph{floor constraint} with bound $\ell > 0$ on $[0,T]$ if $\min_{i\in\{1,\dots,d\}}\mu_i(t) \geq \ell$ almost surely for every $t \in [0,T]$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{defn}
\begin{remark}
    When $d = 2$, market diversity implies a market weight floor constraint since $\min_{i\in\{1,2\}}\mu_i(t) = 1 - \max_{i \in \{1,2\}}\mu_i(t) \geq \delta$, where $\delta$ is the diversity constant.
\end{remark}
In the sequel, we will say that the \emph{frictionless market} is diverse, nondegenerate, has bounded volatility or satisfies a floor constraint if the corresponding definition holds with $S$ in place of $P$. 
To simplify the exposition of what is to come we will assume that the impact functions are of separable form \begin{equation} \label{eqn:h_separable}
    h_i(t,x) = \phi_i(t)\widehat h_i(x), \qquad i=1,\dots,d, \quad t \geq 0, x \in \R,
\end{equation} for some function $\widehat h_i$ and where $\phi_i$ is nonnegative and satisfies $\overline \phi_i = \sup_{t \geq 0} \phi_i(t) < \infty$ for each $i$. We also assume that the initial impact trajectories are zero.

We will now need a technical lemma, whose proof is postponed to Appendix~\ref{app:relarb_proofs}.
\begin{lem} \label{lem:relarb}
    Let Assumption~\ref{ass:impact_inputs} hold and further assume that the impact shape functions are of the form \eqref{eqn:h_separable} and that $J_i^0 = 0$ for every $i$.
Suppose that the frictionless market is diverse, nondegenerate, has bounded volatility and satisfies capitalization and market weights floors with respective constants $(\delta_S,\epsilon_S,\sigma_S^2,\kappa_S,\ell_S).$ Set
\[\delta_P = \frac{\delta_S}{4}, \qquad \epsilon_P = \epsilon_S \frac{\ell_S^2(1-\frac{1}{2}\delta_S)^d}{4\|N\|_\infty\|N^{-1}\|_\infty(1-\delta_S)^d}, \qquad T^* = \frac{2d}{\epsilon_P\delta_P^2}.\] Given $\theta > 0$ set $T = T^* + \theta$ and define
\begin{equation}  \label{eqn:time_dep_quadratic} 
G(t,\mu) = \nu \psi(t)H(\mu), \qquad \text{where} \qquad H(\mu) = 1 - \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^d \mu_i^2,
\end{equation}
$\psi$ is given by \eqref{eqn:psi} for any $0 < T_0 < T_1 < T$ and $\nu \in (0,\overline \nu)$,
where $\overline \nu$
 is defined in \eqref{eqn:bar_nu}.
Then there exists a choice of $\theta$ such that the observed market satisfies Assumption~\ref{ass:nonexplosion}. Moreover, the observed market is globally nondegenerate with constant $\epsilon_P$ and diverse with constant $\delta_P$ on $[0,T]$.
\end{lem}
\begin{remark}
    The choice of generating function $H$ in \eqref{eqn:time_dep_quadratic} can be generalized. By inspecting the proof it is clear that the conclusions of the lemma will hold if $H$ is a smooth generating function with bounded derivatives and if $\nu$ is chosen to be sufficiently small. The upper bound on $\nu$ may depend on the $L_\infty$ norms of $H$ and its derivatives in addition to the current constants. The computations and estimates, however, significantly simplify when $H$ is quadratic as in \eqref{eqn:time_dep_quadratic}. 
\end{remark}
% \begin{remark} \fbox{\textcolor{red}{Make this part of discussion following Theorem?}}
%     The impact specifications $h_i$ and $K_i$ do not influence $\delta_P,\epsilon_P$ and hence the outperformance time horizon $T^*$. They do however, restrict the allowable trading speed through $\overline \nu$. As such, they have a crucial influence on the achievable outperformance size by the time $T^*$, which is discussed in more detail below.
% \end{remark}
\begin{thm}[Relative arbitrage; explicit  version]\label{thm:relarb_explicit} 
    Let the assumptions of Lemma~\ref{lem:relarb} hold. Set $T_0 = \eta$, $T_1 = T^*+ \eta$ and $T = T^* + 2\eta$ for any $\eta \in (0,\theta/2]$, where $\theta$ is as in Lemma~\ref{lem:relarb}. Then the trading strategy additively generated by $G$ given by \eqref{eqn:time_dep_quadratic} is a relative arbitrage on $[0,T]$. In particular, relative arbitrage exists for any $T > T^*$.
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
We proceed as in the proof of Theorem~\ref{lem:relarb} to obtain that
 \begin{align*}
        V^Q(T)  = 1+ \Gamma(T) & > 1 -\nu \int_{T_1}^T\psi'(t)H(\mu(t))dt -\frac{\nu}{2}\sum_{i,j=1}^d\int_{T_0}^{T_1}\partial_{ij}H(\mu(t))d[\mu_i,\mu_j](t)  \\
        % & \geq 1 - \log (d) + \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^d\int_{T_0}^{T_1}\frac{d[\mu_i](t)}{\mu_i(t)}.
        & \geq 1 - \nu  +\frac{\nu}{2}\sum_{i=1}^d\int_{T_0}^{T_1}d[\mu_i](t).
    \end{align*}
    As before, we need to show that $\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^d\int_{T_0}^{T_1}d[\mu_i](t) \geq 1$ with probability one. This part now follows in exactly the same was as in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:relarb_implicit} as, from Lemma~\ref{lem:relarb}, we have diversity and nondegeneracy of the observed market up to the time $T_1$.
\end{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:relarb_implicit} shows that, under additional assumptions not needed in the frictionless case, relative arbitrage is still possible to obtain in the setting with price impact. However, it should be noted that the proof is considerably more challenging as one needs the technical Lemma~\ref{lem:relarb}, which requires establishing nondegeneracy and diversity of the observed market, as well as nonexplosion of the SDE \eqref{eqn:SDE}. Many estimates are employed in the proof and, as such, the constants $\epsilon_P,\delta_P$ and $T^*$ obtained in Lemma~\ref{lem:relarb} may be loose.

Nevertheless, these quantitative bounds, in terms of the fundamental input variables, provide financial intuition regarding the nature of the outperformance. Indeed, by inspecting the constants $\delta_P,\epsilon_P$ and $T^*$ we see that they are entirely independent of the impact shape functions $h$ and decay kernels $K$. The impact coefficients do, however, appear in the bound on the \emph{trading speed} parameter $\nu$, through the constant $\overline \nu$ defined in \eqref{eqn:bar_nu}. As such, the less liquid the market is, captured by $h$ and $K$, the slower the investor needs to trade to achieve relative arbitrage. The outperformance time $T^*$ is worse in the case with price impact, since $\delta_P < \delta_S$ and $\epsilon_P < \epsilon_S$ but, if the investor appropriately regulates their trading speed $\nu$, the state of illiquidity need not further lengthen the outperformance time.

The decrease in the trading speed, however, does directly impact the \emph{size} of the achievable outperformance. To see this most clearly consider the frictionless market and suppose that a strategy $Q$ generated by some function $G$ achieves relative arbitrage on some time horizon $T$. Then the function  $\nu G$, which generates the strategy $Q_\nu$, leads from the master formula \eqref{eqn:master_formula} and \eqref{eqn:Gamma_frict} to the relative outperformance amount
\[V^{Q_\nu}(T) - 1 = \nu (G(T,\mu(T)) - G(0,\mu(0)) + \Gamma(T)) = \nu(V^Q(T)-1). \]
  As such, a slower trading speed $\nu$ linearly reduces the original outperformance amount $V^Q(T) - 1$. In the market with price impact, the relationship is more complicated since the choice of $\nu$ itself effects the market weight process $\mu$, but we can expect a qualitatively similar effect to hold.

It follows that an investor seeking relative arbitrage may need to balance outperformance time with outperformance amount. This is not something that appears in the frictionless setting as one could always suitably scale the generating function to achieve the desired outperformance level. This tradeoff is consistent with previous results on arbitrage in markets with frictions, where arbitrage may be possible to generate, but scalable arbitrage is not (see \cite{guasoni2015hedging}). 

