In this section we present numerical experiments illustrating the theoretical results of the previous sections. To this end, we will specify a model for the fundamental stock price satisfying the assumptions of Lemma~\ref{lem:relarb}, which we know from Theorem~\ref{thm:relarb_explicit} exemplifies favourable market conditions for additive functional generation. For simplicity we take $d = 2$ and define
\begin{align*}
 d\mu_1^S(t) & = \alpha(\overline \mu - \mu_1^S(t))dt + \eta \sqrt{(\mu_1^S(t)-\delta_S)(1-\delta_S-\mu_1^S(t))}dW(t), \\
    d\log \overline \bfs(t) & = \frac{\zeta-1}{2(\log\overline \bfs(t)-\log \kappa_S)}dt + \sqrt{\epsilon_S}dB(t)
\end{align*}
for parameters $\alpha,\eta,\zeta,\epsilon_S,\kappa_S> 0$, $\delta_S \in (0,1/2)$, $\overline \mu \in (\delta_S,1-\delta_S)$ and where $B$  and $W$ are uncorrelated Brownian motions. The frictionless market weight process $\mu_1^S$ is a Jacobi process on $(\delta_S,1-\delta_S)$ and, as such, stays in that open interval almost surely under the standard Feller conditions $2\alpha (\overline \mu-\delta_S) \geq \eta^2$ and $2\alpha (1-\delta_S -\overline\mu) \geq \eta^2$. The log total capitalization process is modelled by a Bessel process which is shifted by $\log \kappa_S$ and scaled by $\sqrt{\epsilon_S}$. By standard properties of the Bessel process, we have $\overline \bfs > \kappa_S$ almost surely whenever $\zeta \geq \epsilon_S+1$ (see e.g.\ \cite[Chapter~XI.1]{revuz1999continuous}). 

Together with initial conditions $\overline \bfs(0) > \kappa_S$ and $\mu_1^S(0) \in (\delta_S,1-\delta_S)$ this specifies the frictionless model by setting  $\mu_2^S(t) = 1- \mu_1^S(t)$ and $S_i(t) = \frac{1}{N_i}\overline \bfs(t) \mu_i(t)$. It is then easy to see that the frictionless market satisfies all of the assumptions of Lemma~\ref{lem:relarb}. Indeed, it is diverse and satisfies the market weight floor property both with constant $\delta_S$. The market cap floor is  clearly $\kappa_S$. Next, because we have that $\log S_i(t) = -\log N_i + \log \overline \bfs(t) + \log \mu_i(t)$ and $\mu_i$ is independent of $\overline \bfs$, we see that
\[\frac{d[\log S_i](t)}{dt} = \epsilon_S +\eta^2\frac{(\mu_i^S(t)-\delta_S)(1-\delta_S - \mu_i^S(t))}{(\mu_i^S(t))^2}. \]
From here it follows that the frictionless market is nondegenerate with constant $\epsilon_S$ and has bounded volatility with constant 
$\sigma_S^2 = \epsilon_S + \eta^2\frac{(1-2\delta_S)^2}{4\delta_S(1-\delta_S)}$, where we used the fact that $x \mapsto \frac{(x-\delta_S)(1-\delta_S-x)}{x^2}$ on $(\delta_S,1-\delta_S)$ is maximized at $x = 2\delta_S(1-\delta_S)$. For our numerical experiments we work in daily units and set the frictionless market parameters to be
\begin{align*}
& N_1  = N_2 = 10^9, \quad \overline \mu = \mu_1^S(0) = 0.5, \quad  \alpha = 0.01, \quad \eta = 0.02, \quad \delta_S = 0.1, \\
& \overline \bfs(0) = 30\times 10^{9}, \quad \epsilon_S = 0.02^2, \quad \zeta =2\epsilon_S+1, \quad \kappa_S = 10^7.      
\end{align*}
The parameters are chosen so that the two stocks have identical marginal distributions and yield volatility and stock capitalizations that are commonly seen in real markets. These parameter choices lead to an initial market capitalization for each stock of \$15B, which is approximately the size of the 500th largest US equity. The investor's initial wealth is taken to be $w$ = \$100M.


Next we specify the impact model. For simplicity we consider identical impact specifications for both assets, and set them to be time homogeneous linear price impact $h_1(t,x) = h_2(t,x) = \lambda x$ for $\lambda > 0$ and exponential decay kernel $K_1(t,s) =K_2(t,s) =  e^{-\beta(t-s)}$ for $\beta > 0$.
  For the impact decay we take $\beta =2$ corresponding to a decay half-life of 1/3 a trading day as reported in \cite{hey.al.23}. We choose $\lambda$ so that a time weighted average price (TWAP) strategy trading 1\% of average daily volume (ADV) leads to an average 11.5 basis point (bp) price impact at the end of the trading day, which is in line with the average estimates reported in \cite[Table~X]{frazzini.al.18} for this trading volume. The TWAP strategy corresponds to $Q_i(t) = 0.01\mathrm{ADV} t$ and consequently $J_i(t) = \frac{0.01 \mathrm{ADV}}{\beta}(1-e^{-\beta t})$. This leads to the requirement
\[S_i(0)*0.00115 = \int_0^1 I_i(t)dt = \lambda \int_0^1 \frac{0.01 \mathrm{ADV}}{\beta}(1-e^{-\beta t})dt = \frac{\lambda 0.01\mathrm{ADV}}{\beta^2}(\beta-1+e^{-\beta}).\] 
We select an ADV of \$100M, which is in line with mid-cap US equities. Using these choices we deduce that $\lambda = 6.08\times 10^{-8}$. 

With these parameter choices we have by Theorem~\ref{thm:relarb_explicit} that the portfolio generated by time-dependent quadratic generating function \eqref{eqn:time_dep_quadratic} outperforms the market portfolio over a sufficiently large time horizon when the trading speed parameter $\nu$ is small enough. The bound on $\nu$ and guaranteed probability one outperformance time $T^*$ derived in the course of the theorem may be very loose, but nevertheless provides mathematical justification for trading such a strategy in these market conditions. Instead, we select the trading speed $\nu$ and simulation time horizon $T$ to be in line with reasonable trade sizes and trading horizons of market participants. As such, we set $\nu = 5$, which was tuned to enable a realistic daily trade size. For the time horizon, we take 21 days (one trading month) for the initiation period, 5 years for the main trading period, 21 days for the liquidation period and we simulate for another 21 days after liquidation when the investor just holds the market. This corresponds to
\[T_0 = 21, \quad T_1 = T_0 + 5\times 252, \quad T = T_1 + 21\]
appearing in the definition of the function $\psi$ of \eqref{eqn:psi}
and we simulate until the horizon $\widetilde T = T + 21$. We run $N_{\mathrm{sim}}= 1000$ simulations using an Euler--Mariyama scheme for the SDE \eqref{eqn:SDE} with $M = 2\widetilde T$ equally spaced discretization points leading to a time step of $\Delta t = 1/2$, which corresponds to trading twice a day. We denote the corresponding trading times as $t = (t_i)_{i=1,\dots,M}$. We compare the performance of the trading strategy $Q$ generated by $G$ given in \eqref{eqn:time_dep_quadratic} in the price impact setting to its performance without price impact. This amounts to computing the relative wealth $V^Q$ when the price and quantity traded are obtained from the SDE \eqref{eqn:SDE} and, separately, computing it when assuming no frictions (i.e.\ $\lambda = 0$), but using the same trajectory of the frictionless price process $S$.  

The results of the numerical experiments are reported in Figure~\ref{fig:mean}. The subscript `F' denotes a quantity in the frictionless market, while the use of the overline indicates an overage over the $M$ simulations. For instance, $V_F^Q$ is the relative wealth of $V$ when trading $Q$ in the frictionless market and $\overline Q_1(t_i) = \frac{1}{M}\sum_{j=1}^M Q_1^{(j)}(t_i)$, where $Q^{(j)}$ is the outcome of the $j$th simulation. As expected, based on the theoretical results of Section~\ref{sec:relarb_explicit}, the strategy outperforms the market even in the price impact setting as illustrated in the top left panel of Figure~\ref{fig:mean}. However, the presence of price impact leads to outperformance by a smaller amount than in the frictionless market. Indeed, in nominal terms, an average of approximately \$1.5M less is made over the five year period due to market frictions. Additionally, the shaded blue region in the top left panel presents the range of values achieved by $V^Q(t)$ over the 1000 simulations. Impressively, with these favourable price dynamics, the portfolio outperforms the market in every realization by the end of the trading period. However, we clearly see that the relative wealth process can experience large drawdowns along the way and that its distribution is skewed left. This highlights the observed difficulty in Section~\ref{sec:relarb_explicit} to obtain \emph{probability one} outperformance guarantees and reinforces that a significantly longer time horizon is needed for relative arbitrage, though the simulations suggest a substantially shorter period is enough for outperformance to occur with high probability. Obtaining tighter theoretical bounds on relative arbitrage than those obtained in Section~\ref{sec:relarb} is an interesting problem for future work.

The top right panel of Figure~\ref{fig:mean} illustrates the master formula decomposition \eqref{eqn:master_formula} for the strategy trading in the market with price impact. During the initiation period the dominant term in the $\Gamma$ decomposition \eqref{eqn:Gamma} is the time derivative term $-\int_0^\cdot \partial_t G(t,\mu(t))dt$, which leads to the stark drop in $\Gamma$. This is, of course, compensated by the term involving $G$, which increases to the positive value $G(T_0,\mu(T_0))$ from zero during this time period. During the five years of time homogeneous trading, the $\Gamma$ term increases, as guaranteed by the theory, while the $G(t,\mu(t))$ term, on average over the 1000 samples, remains nearly constant. The combination of an increasing term and a static term explains the outperformance of the market portfolio. The liquidation period leads to a reversal of what occurs during the initiation period. The function $G$ decreases to zero and, as such, the same time derivative term is the main contributor to $\Gamma$ leading to a rapid offsetting positive increase. Finally, in the last 21 days of the simulation, $G(t,\mu(t)) = 0$ for $t \geq T$ and $d\Gamma(t) = 0$ so that the relative wealth no longer fluctuates since the investor holds the market portfolio.



\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[scale=0.5]{mean.pdf}
    \caption{Results of the numerical experiments across $N_{\mathrm{sim}}=1000$ simulations. Mean relative wealth with and without frictions with shaded region representing band of achieved values for $V^Q(t)$ (top left); Master formula \eqref{eqn:master_formula} decomposition for the relative wealth process with price impact (top right); Average number of shares held in asset one along with a generic path (bottom left); Histogram of daily dollar volume traded truncated to exclude the 1st and 99th percentile (bottom right). In the first three panels, the dotted lines indicate the times $T_0,T_1$ and $T$, while in the final panel the dotted line is the median daily dollar volume.  }
    \label{fig:mean}
\end{figure}

The behaviour in this final 21 day period is also visible in the bottom left panel, where the number of shares held in both assets is constant during that period. Otherwise, as the investor gains wealth, the number of shares held in the assets tends to increase over time on average. However, individual trajectories are significantly more noisy and, as the generic trajectory illustrates, the processes $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ are highly negatively correlated due to the self-financing nature of the portfolio. Indeed, buying additional shares in asset one requires selling shares in asset two to finance this and vice versa. 
% NOTE: w and nu are linked in terms of trade size.

Finally, the bottom right panel showcases a histogram of gross daily trading volume seen throughout the entire simulation period. We focus on the five year time homogeneous trading period and avoid biasing the distribution with the other phases. As such, we omit the first 21 days of each simulation, corresponding to the initiation phase, and the final 42 days, corresponding to the liquidation and holding phases. The daily volume quantities $DV$ making up the histogram are computed as follows. Letting $i_*$ be the smallest index such that $t_{i_*} \geq T_1$ and $i^*$ be the largest index such that $t_{i^*} \leq T$ we compute for every $i \in \{i_*,\dots,\lfloor\frac{i^*}{2}\rfloor\}$ and every simulation $j \in \{1,\dots,N_{\mathrm{sim}}\}$, 
\[DV_i^{(j)} := \sum_{k=1}^d\left(|\Delta Q_k^{(j)}(t_{2i})|\frac{P^{(j)}_k(t_{2i-1})+P_k^{(j)}(t_{2i})}{2} + |\Delta Q_k^{(j)}(t_{2i+1})|\frac{P^{(j)}_k(t_{2i})+P^{(j)}_k(t_{2i+1})}{2}\right), \]
where $\Delta Q_k^{(j)}(t_\ell) = Q_k^{(j)}(t_\ell) - Q_k^{(j)}(t_{\ell-1})$.
The quantity $DV_i^{(j)}$ represents the gross traded dollar volume across the $d=2$ assets on a given day. In the computation of $DV_i^{(j)}$, we assume that the effective execution price is midway between the initial and final price in the corresponding trading period. The histogram in the bottom right panel of Figure~\ref{fig:mean} plots the empirical distribution of $DV = (DV_i^{(j)})_{ij}$ across all $i$ and $j$, truncated to exclude the 1st and 99th percentile for better visualization. We see that the median daily gross trade is approximately \$4M corresponding, since there are two assets, to a median daily trade of 2\% of ADV. The 5th percentile and 95th percentile of $DV$ correspond to\$0.8M and \$13.4M, which similarly lead to trades of $0.4\%$ and $6.7\%$ of ADV respectively. These are within the range of quantities that can be reasonably executed in a single traded day (see e.g.\ \cite{frazzini.al.18} and \cite[Chapter~12]{bouchaud.al.18}). 
% Indeed, the study \cite[Table~X]{frazzini.al.18} includes real market trades of up to 10\% of ADV (daily traded volume (DTV), in their notation).

As such, the numerical experiments largely validate the theoretical results of the previous sections. The strategy additively generated by $G$ defined in \eqref{eqn:time_dep_quadratic} outperforms the market portfolio when the frictionless market satisfies the assumptions of Lemma~\ref{lem:relarb} even over a five year time horizon, though it outperforms by a smaller amount than it would in a frictionless market. The master formula decomposition \eqref{eqn:master_formula} behaves as theoretically predicted through the initiation, main trading and liquidation phases leading to outperformance through the stability of the term $G(t,\mu(t))$ and increasing nature (during the main trading period) of the $\Gamma(t)$ term. Finally, when the parameter $\nu$ is appropriately tuned the ensuing trade sizes are within reasonable execution limits of market participants.





\section{Conclusion} \label{sec:conclusion}
We introduced a framework for stochastic portfolio theory that is able to incorporate an investor's price impact. In this setting we derived the relative wealth equation \eqref{eqn:relative_wealth} with respect to the market portfolio and derived a master formula for additive functional generation of trading strategies. Unlike the frictionless case, care is needed to ensure that the trading strategy leads to a well-defined market with semimartingale prices; this is the content of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, which establishes well-posedness of the SDE \eqref{eqn:SDE}. As an application of the master formula, we develop an analogue of a relative arbitrage result from frictionless SPT to the price impact setting. Versions including implicit and explicit assumptions on the model inputs are developed in Theorems~\ref{thm:relarb_implicit} and \ref{thm:relarb_explicit} respectively. Finally, we perform numerical simulations of the model to validate and compliment the theoretical results.

Our framework allows for many core tenets of SPT to be analyzed in a market with price impact, but extending certain results from frictionless SPT to the current setting remains open. For instance, \emph{rank-based} generating functions are widely studied in SPT and allow one to incorporate the effects of \emph{leakage} into the analysis, which effects portfolio performance when stocks enter and exit the investment universe. Rank-based generating functions lead to holdings processes $Q$, which are not semimartingales and, as such, are beyond the scope of this paper. More generally, extending the analysis to broader classes of generating functions, such as multiplicative functional generation, is an important topic. Further open problems concern optimal portfolio selection in the setting of SPT with price impact, studying the impact of trading on the capital distribution curve, developing equilibrium models for price formation and establishing robustness guarantees for long-term performance of functionally generated portfolios.

