\subsection{Protein Inverse Folding}
\subsubsection{Setup}
\paragraph{Dataset.} In this work, the \textbf{CATH} dataset \citep{cath}, widely adopted in protein inverse folding, is employed. We follow the data splitting of preceding works \citep{StructGNN, pifold}, in which proteins are divided according to the CATH topology principles, giving rise to a structure of 18024 proteins for training, 608 for validation, and 1120 for testing. 

\paragraph{Evaluation metrics.} We mainly employ structure recovery metrics such as \textbf{TM-score} and \textbf{RMSD}, and sequence recovery metrics comprising perplexity and native structure recovery (\textbf{NSR}) for protein inverse folding evaluation in this work. The structure recovery is the same as the metrics for RNA and the sequence recovery follows previous works \citep{pifold,bench}.

\paragraph{Compared approaches.} We mainly compare autoregressive methods (PiFold-AR and ProteinMPNN \citep{proteinmpnn}) with non-autoregressive methods (StructGNN \citep{StructGNN}, GraphTrans \citep{StructGNN}, GCA \citep{GCA}, GVP \citep{GVP}, AlphaDesign \citep{alphadesign}, PiFold \citep{pifold}, KWDesign \cite{kwdesign}). PiFold-AR is implemented with the encoder of PiFold and a stochastic order decoding autoregressive decoder.

