\subsection{$\mathbb{Z}_{+}$-rings and the Frobenius-Perron dimensions}\label{sec-Z+FPdim}
In this subsection, we recall some basic results on $\mathbb{Z}_{+}$-rings and the Frobenius-Perron dimensions.
% that will be used later and fix some notations. 
The readers are referred to \cite{MR3242743} for details. Let $\mathbbm{k}$ be an algebraically closed field.

Given a finite $\mathbbm{k}$-linear abelian category $\mathcal{C}$, $\text{Irr}(\mathcal{C})$ denotes the set of isomorphism classes of irreducible/simple objects in $\mathcal{C}$, and $\text{Gr}(\mathcal{C})$ is the Grothendieck group of $\mathcal{C}$. Given a $\mathbbm{k}$-algebra $A$, we abbreviate $\text{Gr}(A\text{-mod})$ as $\text{Gr}(A)$. If $\mathcal{C}$ is further a finite tensor category, then $\text{Gr}(\mathcal{C})$ is a transitive unital $\mathbb{Z}_{+}$-ring with $\mathbb{Z}_{+}$-basis $\text{Irr}(\mathcal{C})$ \cite[Prop. 4.5.4]{MR3242743}. In particular, for any finite-dimensional Hopf algebra $H$, the Grothendieck ring $\text{Gr}(H)$ is a unital $\mathbb{Z}_{+}$-ring with $\text{Irr}(H)$ as its $\mathbb{Z}_{+}$-basis.
%the set of irreducible $H$-modules isomorphic class

Let $(R,\{b_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m})$ be a transitive unital $\mathbb{Z}_{+}$-ring of finite rank, where $\{b_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}$ is the $\mathbb{Z}_{+}$-basis of $R$. The Frobenius-Perron dimension of  $a\in R$ is denoted by $\text{FPdim}(a)$. For a finite-dimensional Hopf algebra $H$ and an irreducible $H$-module $V$, the Frobenius-Perron dimension of $[V]$ is $\dim_{\mathbbm{k}}V$. The following facts are needed later.
%For a finite-dimensional Hopf algebra $H$, the Frobenius-Perron dimension function on $\text{Gr}(H)$ is precisely the $\mathbbm{k}$-dimension function. 

%Let $R$ be a ring that is free as a $\mathbb{Z}$-module and has a $\mathbb{Z}$-basis $\{b_{i}\}_{i\in I}$. The pair $(R,\{b_{i}\}_{i\in I})$ is called a \textit{$\mathbb{Z}_{+}$-ring}, if for any $i,j\in I$, the structure constants $c_{ij}^{k}$ in the product $b_{i}b_{j}=\sum_{k\in I}c_{ij}^{k}b_{k}$ are all non-negative integers. In this case, $\{b_{i}\}_{i\in I}$ is referred to as the \textit{$\mathbb{Z}_{+}$-basis} of $R$. When no confusion arises, the $\mathbb{Z}_{+}$-ring $(R,\{b_{i}\}_{i\in I})$ is simply denoted by $R$. If the identity $1$ of a $\mathbb{Z}_{+}$-ring $R$ lies in its $\mathbb{Z}_{+}$-basis, then $R$ is called a \textit{unital $\mathbb{Z}_{+}$-ring}. In this paper, the $\mathbb{Z}_{+}$-rings we consider are all Grothendieck rings of the categories of finite dimensional representations of finite dimensional Hopf algebras.

 %Let $H$ be a finite dimensional algebra over $\mathbbm{k}$. Recall that the \textit{Grothendieck group} $\text{Gr}(H)$ of $H\text{-mod}$ is the free abelian group with basis given by the set of isomorphism classes of all simple $H$-modules. Let $\{[X_{1}],...,[X_{m}]\}$ be the set of isomorphic classes of all simple $H$-modules. For any finite dimensional $H$-module $V$, write $[V]=\sum_{i=1}^{m}[V: X_{i}][X_{i}]$, where $[V:X_{i}]$ is the number of the composition factors of $V$ that are isomorphic to $X_{i}$. When $H$ is a (finite dimensional) Hopf algebra, the Grothendieck group $\text{Gr}(H)$ has a ring structure, where the multiplication is given by $[X_{i}][X_{j}]=[X_{i}\otimes X_{j}]$. The resulting ring is called the \textit{Grothendieck ring} of $H\text{-mod}$ (also referred to as the Grothendieck ring of $H$), which is clearly a $\mathbb{Z}_{+}$-ring with $\mathbb{Z}_{+}$-basis given by $\{[X_{1}],...,[X_{m}]\}$. Since $\mathbbm{k}$ is an  algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, the Grothendieck ring of a finite dimensional semisimple Hopf algebra over $\mathbbm{k}$ is further a \textit{fusion ring}. A fusion ring is a unital $\mathbb{Z}_{+}$-ring $R$ of finite free rank (with $\mathbb{Z}_{+}$-basis $\{b_{i}\}_{i\in I}$, where $I$ is a finite index set) that satisfies the following conditions:
