\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}


The large N QCD is an approximation widely believed to confine the quarks while preserving asymptotic freedom.  
The program of string theory emerged almost half a century ago to describe the gluon field condensed into a string between quarks. The string picture of quark confinement becomes a well-defined mathematical statement in large N QCD: the gauge-invariant correlation functions become meromorphic functions of momenta, with poles at the hadron spectra.
In this large N limit, the spectrum is infinite, and there are no decay of mesons and baryons.
\subsection{The Wilson loop}
Our main variable will be the Wilson loop
\begin{smalleq}
    W[C] = \VEV{\frac{1}{N}\tr \hat P \exp{ \oint d \theta  \dot C_\mu(\theta)  A_\mu( C(\theta))}}_A
\end{smalleq}
with averaging over the distribution of the gauge field. In the original QCD, the averaging used the YM functional measure
\begin{smalleq}
   && \VEV{X[A]}_A = \frac{\int [\delta A] X[A]\exp{- \beta E[A]}}{\int [\delta A]\exp{- \beta E[A]}};\\
   \label{GibbsEnergy}
   && E[A] = -\int d^4 x \tr F_{\mu\nu}^2;\\
   && F_{\mu\nu} = \pd{\mu} A_\nu - \pd{\nu} A_\mu + \left[A_\mu,A_\nu\right]
\end{smalleq}
This measure can be interpreted as the Gibbs distribution of a statistical system in four dimensions, with inverse temperature $\beta = 1/T$. In the \YM{} theory, the temperature is equivalent to the bare coupling constant $T = g_0^2$. The regularization, as well as the gauge fixing, necessary to define these functional integrals, were discussed in the literature. 

Perturbatively (in $g_0$), the dimensional regularization works for arbitrary time as well as with the \YM{} theory at $\tau=0$. Beyond perturbation theory, the lattice gauge theory serves as a regularized gauge theory. As we shall see below, the loop equation can be regularized differently, without breaking the space symmetries (as the lattice would do), and without any gauge fixing (as the dimensional regularization would do).

This gauge-invariant functional of the loop $C \in \mathbb R_4$ can be regarded as a generating functional for expectation values of gauge field strength, parallel transported to the same point in space. The Wilson loop also directly enters into observable amplitudes of the quantum particles (quarks) interacting with the gauge field.

This relation will be used in the next sections, after we find the solution of the gradient flow for the Wilson loop.
This is a phenomenological picture universally accepted in physics community; it remains to be proven and to compute this spectrum and these correlation functions. 
There is an exact functional equation \cite{MMEq79, Mig83} for the Wilson loop in planar QCD, theoretically capable of proving confinement and finding the string theory dual to QCD.

To our surprise and disappointment, this has not happened for half a century since this equation was written down. Even worse, in the special case of a solvable $\mathcal {N} = 4$ SYM theory, where no quantum effects are renormalizing the coupling constant, the conjectured solution was not shown to satisfy the loop equation, despite numerous efforts.

As the author sees it now, the problem lies in the incomplete version of the loop equation used in these efforts. The original equation \cite{MMEq79} was a \textbf{vector} equation, as it was the translation of vector equations of motion for the gauge field; moreover, there was another equation -- the Bianchi identity, which was satisfied identically by the gauge field strength, but led to nontrivial restrictions for the possible loop functionals to be tested by the loop equation. For example, for an area of some surface bounded by the loop $C$, the Bianchi identity would be only satisfied by a minimal surface\cite{M23PR} (it leads to so-called Plateau equations for the minimal surface).

In this paper, we utilize the new form of the loop calculus\cite{migdal2025YangMills}, which is based on the original loop technology from \cite{MMEq79, Mig83} and further extends and simplifies it. This new loop calculus naturally leads to a solution\cite{migdal2025YangMills} for the Wilson loop for the \YM{} gradient flow in terms of a periodic random walk over compact manifolds in momentum loop space.

We briefly remind this new loop calculus in the next section and then apply it to the original loop equation \cite{MMEq79} in momentum loop space, as suggested in \cite{SeqQuanM95, Mig98Hidden}.

With the new loop calculus, the solution of the planar loop equation is embarrassingly simple. This is a natural extension of the solution for the fixed point of the \YM{} gradient flow. The extension is needed to preserve the vanishing classical term (the LHS of the loop equation) while exactly nullifying the right side (the contact terms).
\subsection{Langevin equation and the asymptotic freedom}
Formally, the asymptotically free QCD corresponds to the $g_0 \to 0$ limit of the fixed point of the Langevin equation:
\begin{smalleq}
    &&\pd{\tau} A_\nu =  D_\mu F_{\mu\nu} + f_\nu;\\
    && \VEV{f_\mu(x,\tau) \otimes f_\nu(y,\tau')} = g_0^2 \delta(\tau-\tau')\delta_{\mu\nu}\delta^4(x-y) \hat \Pi;\\
    && \Pi^{k l}_{i j} = \delta_{i j} \delta_{k l} - \frac{1}{N_c} \delta_{i l} \delta_{k j}
\end{smalleq}
This method of quantization of the gauge theory (stochastic quantization) has numerous advantages over the ill-defined functional integral in 4D gauge theory. In our case, this will be the shortest path to the exact solution of planar QCD.

The Wilson loop in this case satisfies the loop equation with the contact term \cite{MMEq79, Mig83}
\begin{smalleq}\label{loopEq}
    && \pd{\tau}  W[C] = \int_0^{2\pi} d \theta  \dot C_\nu(\theta) \partial_\mu(\theta)\fbyf{W[C]}{\sigma_{\mu\nu}(\theta)}\nonumber\\
    &&+ g_0^2 \int_0^{2\pi} d \theta_2  \dot C_\mu(\theta_2) \int_0^{\theta_2} d \theta_1 \dot C_\mu(\theta_1) \delta^4\left(C(\theta_1) - C(\theta_2)\right)\nonumber\\
   &&\left(W\left[C_{\theta_1,\theta_2} \uplus C_{\theta_2,\theta_1}\right] - \frac{W[C]}{N} \right);\\
   && 
   \begin{cases}
    C_{\theta_1,\theta_2}\left(\frac{2\pi(\theta - \theta_1)}{\theta_2-\theta_1}\right) = C(\theta)& \textit{ if } \theta_1 < \theta < \theta_2\\
        C_{\theta_2,\theta_1}\left(\frac{2\pi(\theta - \theta_2)}{2 \pi+\theta_1-\theta_2} \right)=  C(\theta)& \textit{ if } \theta_2 < \theta < \theta_1 + 2 \pi\\
   \end{cases}
\end{smalleq}
Geometrically, the loop $C_{\theta_1,\theta_2} \uplus C_{\theta_2,\theta_1} $ describes two closed loops, but in our functional analysis, this is a particular case of a periodic function $C_\alpha(\theta)$ with a smaller period inside the total $2\pi$ period. It maps two parts of the unit circle bounded by $\theta_1, \theta_2$ into 4D space.

In the large N limit (so-called planar QCD), the contact terms in the loop equation \eqref{loopEq} factorize, as it was noticed in \cite{MMEq79}:
\begin{smalleq}\label{MMEq}
    && \pd{\tau}  W[C] =  \int_0^{2\pi} d \theta  \dot C_\nu(\theta) \partial_\mu(\theta)\fbyf{W[C]}{\sigma_{\mu\nu}(\theta)}\nonumber\\
    &&+ \lambda \int_0^{2\pi} d \theta_2  \dot C_\mu(\theta_2) \int_0^{\theta_2} d \theta_1 \dot C_\mu(\theta_1) \delta^4\left(C(\theta_1) - C(\theta_2)\right)W\left[C_{\theta_1,\theta_2}\right] W\left[C_{\theta_2,\theta_1}\right];\\&& \lambda = N g_0^2 \to \const{}
\end{smalleq}
The QCD Wilson loop corresponds to the fixed point of that equation, with the limit $ \lambda \to 0$ taken together with the limit of $\Lambda \to \infty$  at fixed $\mu = \Lambda\lambda^b \exp{ -\frac{a}{\lambda}}$ with well-known RG coefficients $a,b$. The cutoff $\Lambda$  in this equation arises from replacement the delta function with the Gaussian $\mathcal{N}_{0,\sigma}(x-y)$ with variance $\sigma = 1/\Lambda$ in the contact term. 
We must insert extra ordered path exponentials $U(x,y) \otimes U(y,x)$ in this contact term to keep the loops closed (and thus, gauge invariant). The details of this gauge-invariant regularization of the contact term are unimportant, as they will disappear in momentum loop space in subsequent sections.
\section{New loop technology and momentum loop equation}
\subsection{The new loop calculus}\label{LoopCalculus}
Let us now define the operators of point gradient $\pd{\mu}$ and area derivative $\ff{\sigma_{\mu\nu}}$. This is one of the rare occasions when a correct mathematical definition opens the way to the exact solution.

We must use the loop calculus, developed in \cite{Mig83, Mig98Hidden} and finalized in \cite{migdal2025YangMills}.
For the reader's convenience, we summarize here the basic operators of this new loop calculus. The proofs can be found in \cite{migdal2025YangMills}.

The basic object of the loop calculus is the area derivative, which follows from the non-abelian version of the Stokes theorem for the variation of the Wilson loop
\begin{smalleq}
    \delta W[C,\tau] = \oint d \theta  \dot C_\mu(\theta) \delta C_{\nu}(\theta) \fbyf{W[C,\tau]}{\sigma_{\mu\nu}(\theta)}
\end{smalleq}
\subsubsection*{Operator identity}
The way to derive the loop operators is to replace the Wilson loop by its operator representation
\begin{smalleq}\label{operatorIdentity}
     && W(C(.),\tau) = \VEV{\frac{1}{N}\tr P \exp{\I \int_t^{t+2\pi} d s \dot C_\mu(s) D_\mu(C(t))}};\\
     && D_\mu(x) = \pd{x_\mu} + A_\mu(x)
\end{smalleq}
This representation was implied in our earlier works \cite{Mig98Hidden}, but the recent clean proof can be found in \cite{migdal2025YangMills}.
\subsubsection*{New representation of the area derivative}
In this form, the functional derivatives $\ff{\dot C_\mu(t\pm)}$ bring down the covariant derivative operator before or after the ordered product
\begin{smalleq}
   && \ff{\dot C_\mu(t\pm)}W(C(.),\tau) =\VEV{\frac{1}{N}\tr D_\mu(C(t\pm))P \exp{\I \int_t^{t+2\pi} d s \dot C_\mu(s) D_\mu(C(t))}}
\end{smalleq}
We call such functional derivatives \textbf{the dot derivatives}.
Therefore, the field strength (which is the commutator of two covariant derivatives) is generated by the wedge product of left and right dot derivatives
\begin{smalleq}\label{areader}
    &&\fbyf{W(C(.),\tau)}{\sigma_{\mu\nu}(t)} = \ff{\dot C_\mu(t-)}\wedge \ff{\dot C_\nu(t+)}W(C(.),\tau)  = \nonumber\\
    &&\VEV{\frac{1}{N}\tr F_{\mu\nu}(C(t))P \exp{\I \int_t^{t+2\pi} d s \dot C_\mu(s) D_\mu(C(t))}}
\end{smalleq}
\subsubsection*{New representation of the covariant derivative}
The covariant derivative of $F_{\mu\nu}$ is generated by the difference of left and right dot derivatives
\begin{smalleq}
   && \partial_\mu(t)= \ff{\dot C_\mu(t-)}-\ff{\dot C_\mu(t+)};\\
   &&\partial_\mu(t) \fbyf{W(C(.),\tau)}{\sigma_{\mu\nu}(t)} = \nonumber\\
   &&\VEV{\frac{1}{N}\tr \left[D_\mu, F_{\mu\nu}(C(t))\right]P \exp{\I \int_t^{t+2\pi} d s \dot C_\mu(s) D_\mu(C(t))}}
\end{smalleq}
\subsubsection*{Parametric invariance}
Note that, unlike the ordinary functional derivative, the dot derivative is parametric invariant $ s\Ra f(s), f'(s) >0$, as it follows from the definition
\begin{smalleq}
    \delta F[C] = \int d s \delta \dot C_\mu(s) \fbyf{F}{ \dot C_\mu(s)}
\end{smalleq}
It also follows from this definition that this functional derivative is defined modulo an addition of a constant term  $\int d s \delta \dot C(s) \const{}=0$ by periodicity of $C(s)$.
As we see, the left and right dot derivatives $\ff{ \dot C_\gamma(\theta\pm 0)}$ must produce different limits for the area and point derivatives to be finite. 
These functional derivatives, unlike conventional $\ff{C_\mu(s)}$, do not produce delta functions, but rather finite results which may differ for the right and left limit at every point. This is clear from the above formulas for the area derivative and the covariant derivative of the field strength.

\subsubsection*{Annihilation of the Wilson loop}
Another important property of the dot derivative is that its discontinuity $\partial_\mu(t)$ annihilates the Wilson loop
\begin{smalleq}\label{annihil}
    \partial_\mu(t) W(C(.),\tau) =0;
\end{smalleq} 
This is true for the Wilson loop in both the Abelian and non-Abelian cases.
\subsubsection*{No area derivative for the function of a point on a loop}
 Likewise, one can prove that the area derivative annihilates any local function of $C(\theta)$.
\begin{smalleq}
    &&\ff{\dot C(\theta-0)}\wedge\ff{\dot C(\theta+0)} F(C(\theta_1))  =0
\end{smalleq}
\subsubsection*{The Bianchi identity}
There are two consequences from the above representation of the loop derivatives in terms of the dot derivatives. First, there is a Bianchi identity
\begin{smalleq}
e_{\alpha\mu\nu\lambda}\partial_\alpha(\theta)\fbyf{W[C,\tau]}{\sigma_{\mu\nu}(\theta)} = 0
\end{smalleq}
which holds at the kinematical level, regardless of dynamics.
This identity holds on the operator level for the dot derivatives
\begin{smalleq}
   && e_{\alpha\mu\nu\lambda} \left(\ff{\dot C_\alpha(\theta+)} - \ff{\dot C_\alpha(\theta-)}\right) \ff{\dot C_{\lb{\nu}}(\theta-)} \ff{\dot C_{\rb{\mu}}(\theta+)} =0
\end{smalleq}
In this equation and later throughout the paper, we use the notation $(\lb{\mu},\dots,\rb{\nu})$ for the antisymmetric part of the tensor
\begin{smalleq}
    X(\lb{\mu},\rb{\nu}) \equiv \frac{X(\mu,\nu) - X(\nu,\mu)}{2}
\end{smalleq}
\subsubsection*{The classical part of the loop equation}
Second, we find the evolution of the Wilson loop in the gradient flow (without the contact terms)
\begin{smalleq}\label{LoopEq}
    && \pd{\tau}  W[C,\tau] = \int_0^{2\pi} d \theta \dot C_\nu(\theta) \left(\ff{\dot C_\mu(\theta+)} - \ff{\dot C_\mu(\theta-)}\right)\ff{\dot C_{\lb{\nu}}(\theta-)} \ff{\dot C_{\rb{\mu}}(\theta+)}W[C,\tau]\nonumber\\
    &&+\textit{contact terms};
\end{smalleq}
\subsection{ Momentum Loop Equation}
As it was noticed long ago \cite{MLDMig86, Mig98Hidden}, the planar loop equation dramatically simplifies in momentum loop space: the classical part becomes a polynomial in momenta, and the contact terms are no longer singular -- they factorize in momentum loop space, with the delta functions going away. The similar transformation makes the Klein-Gordon equation $(-\pd{\mu}^2 + m^2) G(x) = \delta^4(x)$ for the particle propagator an arithmetic equation $ (p^2 + m^2) G(p) =1$.

Let us repeat the arguments of that old work with the new loop calculus. We will be looking for the fixed point, so we drop the left side by the derivative with respect to the stochastic time. 
\subsubsection{Abelian momentum loop}
The simplest form of the momentum loop is the Abelian momentum (then we generalize it for the non-Abelian one).
\begin{smalleq}
    W[C] = \int [DP] W[P]\exp{\I\oint d \theta \dot C_\mu(\theta) P_\mu(\theta)}
\end{smalleq}
where $W[P]$ is some functional of closed curves $P_\mu(\theta)$. Solving the loop equation would mean finding this functional.
Using the new loop calculus, we immediately find the representation no the classical \YM{} equation in the Abelian momentum loop space
\begin{smalleq}
    &&\pd{\mu}(\theta) \fbyf{W[C]}{\sigma_{\mu\nu}(\theta)} = \nonumber\\
    &&\I \int [DP] W[P]\exp{\I\oint d \theta \dot C_\mu(\theta) P_\mu(\theta)}
    \left(P_\mu(\theta+) - P_\mu(\theta-)\right) P_{\lb{\nu}}(\theta-) P_{\rb{\mu}}(\theta+) 
\end{smalleq}
The contact terms on the right side simplify to the following
\begin{smalleq}\label{contactTerms}
 && \lambda\int_{\theta}^{\theta+2\pi} d \theta_1\dot C_\nu(\theta_1)\iint [DP_{12}][DP_{2 1}] W[P_{12}]W[P_{2 1}]\exp{ \I\oint d \theta \dot C_\mu(\theta) P_\mu(\theta)}
\end{smalleq}
where two loops 
\begin{smalleq}
    &&P_{12} = P[(\theta_1,\theta+ 2\pi)],\\
    &&P_{2 1} = P[(\theta,\theta_1)]
\end{smalleq}
are two momentum loops corresponding to two parts of the original $C-$ loop before and after self-intersection at $\theta_1, \theta$.
We can replace the derivative $ \dot C_\nu(\theta_1) $ as principal value of the functional derivative of the exponential $\exp{ \I\oint d \theta \dot C_\mu(\theta) P_\mu(\theta)}$
\begin{smalleq}
    &&\I \dot C_\nu(\theta_1)\exp{ \I\oint d \theta \dot C_\mu(\theta) P_\mu(\theta)} =\nonumber\\
    &&\frac{1}{2} \left(\ff{P_\nu(\theta_1-0)} + \ff{P_\nu(\theta_1+0)}\right)\exp{ \I\oint d \theta \dot C_\mu(\theta) P_\mu(\theta)}
\end{smalleq}
Assuming translational invariance of the linear measure $ [DP]$, we can reapply these derivatives (with the minus sign) to the factors $W[P_{12}] W[P_{21}]$.
We conclude that the contact terms will yield zero provided the following identities hold
\begin{smalleq}\label{extraCond}
    &&\fbyf{W[P_{12}]}{P_\nu(\theta_1+0)} =0;\\
    &&\fbyf{W[P_{2 1}]}{P_\nu(\theta_1-0)} =0;
\end{smalleq}
These are functional derivatives at the beginning and the end of the interval of the angle of the two momentum loops.
\subsubsection{The non-Abelian momentum loop and self-duality}
Now, we have to find the constrained functionals $W[P]$ to nullify the classical part of the loop equation, and make these constraints compatible with the one we have just found to nullify the contact terms. 


The Abelian momentum loop Ansatz is insufficient to satisfy the classical equations; however, we have found a non-Abelian generalization that works.
Moreover, it is general enough to restrict it by extra constraints to nullify the contact terms as well-- which would solve the planar loop equation.

Here is this non-Abelian generalization of the momentum loop.
\begin{smalleq}\label{NabML}
    && W[C] = \int [D P] W[P] \tr \hat T_\theta \exp{\I \oint d \theta \dot C_\alpha(\theta) P_\alpha(\theta)};
\end{smalleq}
Instead of non-commutative operators of covariant derivatives, we have the matrix vector functions $P_\alpha(\theta)$ on a circle with an infinitely dense set of discontinuities, making the ordered products of these matrices act the same way as non-commutative operators.
The measure $W[P]$ in the space of these matrix vector fields on a circle is so far undetermined; we have to find such a measure that nullifies both the classical part and the contact terms.
The classical part of the loop equation, using new loop calculus, leads to the following relation
\begin{smalleq}\label{EqMotion}
    \left(P_\mu(\theta-) P_{\lb{\mu}}(\theta) P_{\rb{\nu}}(\theta+) - P_{\lb{\mu}}(\theta-) P_{\rb{\nu}}(\theta)P_\mu(\theta+)\right)W[P] =0
\end{smalleq}
This equation means that this measure has support only on the fields satisfying this relation.
 Let us clarify the mathematical meaning of this measure.
 \subsubsection{$W[P]$ as a Probabilistic Measure in Loop Space}

The object $W[P]$ is not a function in the usual sense, but a singular measure 
defined over the space of matrix-valued, periodic vector fields 
$P_\mu(\theta)$ on the unit circle. We denote this loop space by
\begin{smalleq}
\mathcal{L}(\mathfrak{g} \oplus \mathbb{R}) = \{ P_\mu(\theta) \mid 
P_\mu(\theta + 2\pi) = P_\mu(\theta) \}
\end{smalleq}
with values in a Lie algebra $\mathfrak{g}$ augmented by an abelian component.

The measure $W[P]$ has support only on those loops that satisfy a set of 
algebraic and differential constraints derived from the self-duality 
condition and the structure of the loop equation. These constraints define a 
submanifold $\mathcal{M} \subset \mathcal{L}$, often realized implicitly 
through relations such as:
\begin{smalleq}
&& F_j[P] = 0, \quad j = 1, \dots, m;\\
&& G_i[P] \ge 0, \quad i = 1, \dots, n;\\
\end{smalleq}
for a suitable collection of functionals $\{F_j, G_i\}$. In this sense, $W[P]$ may 
be thought of heuristically as a formal product of delta functions and step functions,
\begin{smalleq}
W[P] \sim \prod_{j=1}^m \delta(F_j[P]) \prod_{i=1}^n \theta(G_j[P]),
\end{smalleq}
though we refrain from assigning any numerical meaning to $W[P]$ as a function.

Instead, $W[P]$ is only used as a **probabilistic measure** in functional 
integrals such as the Wilson loop,
\begin{smalleq}
W[C] = \int [DP]\, W[P]\, \mathcal{O}_C[P],
\end{smalleq}
where the observable $\mathcal{O}_C[P]$ is typically a non-abelian 
path-ordered exponential. This interpretation suffices for all physical 
applications: $W[P]$ need not be integrated as a scalar density or 
differentiated unless in the weak (distributional) sense.

The compactness of the underlying P-manifold — a fiber bundle over a unit 
ball — ensures that this singular measure is nevertheless well-defined 
in the context of gauge-invariant path integrals.

\subsubsection{Self duality constraint}
As we observed in \cite{migdal2025YangMills}, the self-duality (or anti-duality) condition will solve the classical equation \eqref{EqMotion}
\begin{smalleq}\label{dualityMom}
   && P_{\lb{\mu}}(\theta) P_{\rb{\nu}}(\theta+) = \pm e_{\mu\nu\lambda\rho} P_{\lambda}(\theta) P_{\rho}(\theta+);
\end{smalleq}
We consider only the self-duality ($\pm 1 = 1$) as the negative case follows by a parity reflection (change of the sign of the Levi-Civita symbol $e_.$).
When this self-duality relation for the momentum loop is satisfied, the classical part of the loop equation vanishes by the Bianchi identity (cyclic symmetry).
\begin{smalleq}
    e_{\mu\nu\lambda\rho}\left(P_\mu(\theta-) P_{\lambda}(\theta) P_{\rho}(\theta+) - P_{\lambda}(\theta-) P_{\rho}(\theta)P_\mu(\theta+)\right) \equiv 0
\end{smalleq}
Note that we kept the operators $P_.$ non-commuting; we only interchanged the indexes, not the operators.

We cannot claim that the self-dual fields represent the general solution of the classical equation, but we know that these are \textbf{stable} minima of the Gibbs energy \eqref{GibbsEnergy}, with energy levels proportional to the absolute value of the topological charge
\begin{smalleq}
 && E[A] = 16 \pi^2|Q|;\\
    &&Q = \frac{\int d^4 x \tr F_{\mu\nu} * F_{\mu\nu}}{16 \pi^2} \in \mathbb Z
\end{smalleq}
These energy levels are \textbf{not} proportional to the volume of the system, so that they are degenerate with the ground state in the statistical limit. This, in turn, means that all these levels contribute to the low-temperature partition function (or Wilson loop) of this statistical system.

We do not need this justification to find the solution to the loop equation, though. It suffices to find a finite solution in the continuum limit. Such a solution would be a candidate for the nonperturbative phase of planar QCD. The next step is to verify the asymptotic freedom of this solution (find how it reduces to planar graphs at large momenta).
\subsubsection{The P-manifold construction}\label{pmanifold}
Let us find a general solution of the self-duality equations, suitable for the extra constraints imposed by the contact terms in the loop equation. This solution will generalize the one found in \cite{migdal2025YangMills}.

We use the nonabelian momentum loop \eqref{NabML}, with 
\begin{smalleq}\label{NabP}
   && P_\alpha(\theta) = \hat \tau_a P^a_\alpha(\theta);\\
   && P^a_\alpha(\theta) = \oh \tr \tau_a P_\alpha(\theta);
\end{smalleq}
The generators $\hat\tau^a, a=1,2,\dots,\dim \mathfrak{g}$ of Lie algebra $\mathfrak{g}$. We add an abelian subgroup $U(1)$ with $ \hat \tau_0 = I$ for completeness of the solution.
We use the convention of summation over repeated indices.

The duality equation can be reduced to a set of constraints on $P_\alpha(\theta)$ using the projectors $\eta^i_{\mu\nu}, \bar\eta^i_{\mu\nu}$ by 't Hooft as described in \cite{migdal2025YangMills}:
\begin{smalleq}\label{genEq}
    && (\hat \tau_a  \hat \tau_b)\bar \eta^i_{\lambda\rho} P^a_{\lambda}(\theta-) P^b_{\rho}(\theta) = 0;\; \forall i =1,2,3\\
    && a,b = 0,1,\dots \mathcal N;\\
    &&\mathcal N = \dim \mathfrak{g};\\
    && \eta^i_{\mu\nu} = \delta_{i\lb{\mu}} \delta_{4\rb{\nu}} + e_{i\mu\nu 4};\\
    && \bar \eta^i_{\mu\nu} = \delta_{i\lb{\mu}} \delta_{4\rb{\nu}} - e_{i\mu\nu 4};\\
    && \oh e_{\mu\nu\lambda\rho}\eta^i_{\lambda\rho} =  \eta^i_{\mu\nu};\\
    && \oh e_{\mu\nu\lambda\rho}\bar\eta^i_{\lambda\rho} = -\bar \eta^i_{\mu\nu};
\end{smalleq}
The 't Hooft symbols $\eta^i_{\mu\nu}, \bar\eta^i_{\mu\nu} i=1,2,3$ form the basis of dual or anti-dual tensors depending on the sign in front of the $e$ tensor.

Projecting these matrix equations on a hermitian basis $\tau_a$ we find the following linear recurrent relation for $P(\theta)$ given $P(\theta-)$.
\begin{smalleq}\label{selfDualEq}
    &&P^{a}_{\lambda}(\theta-)f_{abc}\bar \eta^i_{\lambda\rho} P^{c}_{\rho}(\theta) =0; \nonumber\\
   && \forall{ i = 1,2,3; a,b,c =0,1,\dots \mathcal N};\\
    && f_{a b c} = \tr (\hat \tau_a \hat \tau_b \hat \tau_c)
\end{smalleq}
This is the set of $3(\mathcal N+1)$ linear homogeneous equations for $4 (\mathcal N +1)$ unknowns $P^{c}_{\rho}(\theta)$. There is a $\mathcal N +1$-dimensional null space left for $P(\theta)$, assuming generic random vectors $P^{a}_{\lambda}(\theta-)$ in a compact domain (we specify this domain later).

In the \Mathematica notebook \cite{DualityEquations} we generate random vectors and corresponding null spaces for several Lie groups, $SU(N) $ and $ SO(N)$, with $N = 2, 3, 4$, and confirm our assumption regarding the dimension of these null spaces. Here, we assume this dimension to be $\mathcal {N} + 1$.

Let us introduce $3 (\mathcal N+1)\times 4 (\mathcal N+1)$  matrices
\begin{smalleq}
&&\left\langle i r\right\rvert  Q(P)\left \lvert c \rho\right\rangle = \norm{P^{a}_{\lambda}f_{acr}\eta^i_{\lambda\rho}};\\
    &&\left\langle i r\right\rvert \bar Q(P)\left \lvert c \rho\right\rangle = \norm{P^{a}_{\lambda}f_{acr}\bar \eta^i_{\lambda\rho}};
\end{smalleq}
So, the above-mentioned null space spanned by a basis $\psi_k(\theta)$ is determined by equations
\begin{smalleq}\label{Qeqs}
   && \bar Q(P(\theta-)) \cdot\psi_n(\theta) =0; n = 1,2,\dots,\mathcal N +1;\\
   &&\psi^{\dag}_n(\theta)\cdot \hat M\cdot\psi_m(\theta)= \delta_{n m} ;\\
   && P(\theta) =  \sum_{n=1}^{\mathcal N +1} b_n(\theta) \psi_n(\theta);\\
   && \sum_{n=1}^{\mathcal N +1} |b_n(\theta)|^2 \le 1;
\end{smalleq}
where $b$ is an arbitrary vector inside the \textbf{complex} closed ball $ \mathbb B^{2\mathcal N +1}$. The boundary $S^{2\mathcal N}$is included, and it plays an important role later.
The metric tensor $\hat M$ is left undetermined so far, except that it must be positive definite. Note that we changed the solution suggested in \cite{migdal2025YangMills}: instead of a sphere, we have chosen a unit ball for $b$. As we shall see later, this generalization is necessary to satisfy the additional constraints coming from the contact terms in the loop equation.

Thus, we identified (an incomplete so far) set of constraints (equalities and inequalities) defining our manifold (with dense equidistant angles on a circle $\theta_k = 2 \pi k/N, k = 1,\dots, N$, $N \to \8$)
\begin{smalleq}
   && F_j[P] = P^{a}_{\lambda}(\theta_{j})f_{abc}\bar \eta^i_{\lambda\rho} P^{c}_{\rho}(\theta_{j+1}) ;\\
   && G_k[P] = 1- \bar P^{a}_{\lambda}(\theta_k) M^{a \lambda}_{c \rho} P^{c}_{\rho}(\theta_k);
\end{smalleq}
We would also need some boundary conditions on this random walk to make our manifold satisfy the loop equation.
But before that, we have to deal with an unexpected complication: our equations are complex, and so is the null space.
\subsection{$\mathbb{Z}_2$ Symmetry of the $P$-Manifold}

The duality constraint from the loop equation takes the form
\begin{smalleq}
Q^c_i(X, Y) = \sum_{a,b,\mu,\nu} F_{ab}{}^c\, \eta^i_{\mu\nu}\, X^\mu_a Y^\nu_b,
\end{smalleq}
with structure constants \( F_{ab}{}^c \) of the extended Lie algebra (including an Abelian generator indexed by \( a=0 \)), and the self-dual 't~Hooft tensor \( \eta^i_{\mu\nu} \) antisymmetric in its spacetime indices.

The duality equation imposes
\begin{smalleq}
Q^c_i(P(\theta_1), P(\theta_2)) = 0
\end{smalleq}
for all \( c = 0, \dots, \dim(\mathfrak{g}) \) and \( i = 1,2,3 \), where \( P(\theta) \) is a complex-valued function on the unit circle representing the dual loop momentum.

The structure constants satisfy
\begin{smalleq}
F_{ab}{}^c = \overline{F_{ba}{}^c},
\end{smalleq}
due to the Hermitian property of the generators: the symmetric part \( \operatorname{Tr}(\{T_a, T_b\} T_c) \) is real, while the antisymmetric part \( \operatorname{Tr}([T_a, T_b] T_c) \) is imaginary. Combined with the antisymmetry of \( \eta^i_{\mu\nu} \), this implies that the duality bilinear form is anti-Hermitian:
\begin{smalleq}
Q^c_i(X, Y) = -\overline{Q^c_i(Y, X)}.
\end{smalleq}

It follows that the duality condition is covariant under the combined operation of reversal and complex conjugation:
\begin{smalleq}
P(\theta) \mapsto \overline{P(2\pi - \theta)}.
\end{smalleq}
If \( P(\theta) \) is a solution, then so is its reversal-conjugate.

This reflects a deeper symmetry inherited from the standard Wilson loop. In the conventional formulation, the Wilson loop is defined as
\begin{smalleq}
W[C] =\VEV{\operatorname{Tr} \, \mathcal{P} \exp{\oint_C A_\mu(x) dx^\mu}}_{A},
\end{smalleq}
with \( A_\mu \) taken to be anti-Hermitian so that \( W[C] \) is not manifestly real. However, under path reversal \( C(\theta) \mapsto C(2\pi - \theta) \), the integral becomes complex conjugated, ensuring that
\begin{smalleq}
\overline{W[C]} = W[C^{-1}].
\end{smalleq}

The same logic applies to the dual Wilson loop \( W[P] \), defined as a weight for the ordered exponential over the loop in momentum space:
\begin{smalleq}
W[C] = \int [DP] W[P] \operatorname{Tr} \, \mathcal{P} \exp{ \int_0^{2\pi} \imath \, C'(\theta) \cdot P(\theta) \, d\theta },
\end{smalleq}
where \( C(\theta) \) is a fixed spacetime loop, and \( P(\theta) \) is the momentum-valued random walk on the Lie algebra.

Under the transformation
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \( P(\theta) \to \overline{P(2\pi - \theta)} \) (conjugate and reverse),
  \item \( C'(\theta) \to -C'(2\pi - \theta) \) (orientation reversal),
  \item and using \( \imath \to -\imath \) under conjugation,
\end{enumerate}
the exponent becomes
\begin{smalleq}
\imath\, C'(\theta) \cdot P(\theta) \mapsto -\imath\, (-C'(2\pi - \theta)) \cdot \overline{P(2\pi - \theta)} = \overline{\imath\, C'(2\pi-\theta) \cdot P(2\pi-\theta)},
\end{smalleq}
hence the ordered exponential is complex conjugated under this operation, and we find that $W[C]$ will stay reflection-conjugation invariant given the symmetry of the measure $W[P]$
\begin{smalleq}
\overline{W[P]} = W[\overline{P^{-1}}],
\end{smalleq}
where \( \overline{P^{-1}} \) denotes the reversal and complex conjugation of the momentum loop.

\subsubsection{Nullifying the contact terms}

So far, we have described the solution of the duality equations for the matrix-valued momentum loop. This solution ensures that the left-hand side of the loop equation—corresponding to the classical Yang-Mills field equation $[D_\mu F_{\mu\nu}] = 0$—vanishes. We now modify the measure $W[P]$ in such a way that the right-hand side of the loop equation (the contact terms) also vanishes under the self-duality constraint.

We begin by factoring out the Abelian part of the momentum loop in the Wilson loop average:
\begin{smalleq}
   && W[C] = \int [D P]\, W[P]\, \exp{\I \oint d \theta\, \dot C_\alpha(\theta) P^0_\alpha(\theta)} \tr\, \hat T_\theta \exp{\I \oint d \theta\, \dot C_\alpha(\theta) \hat P_\alpha(\theta)}; \\
   && \hat P_\alpha(\theta) = \sum_{a=1}^\mathcal N P^a_\alpha(\theta)\, \hat \tau_a;\\
   && P_\alpha(\theta) =  P^0_\alpha(\theta)\, I +  \hat P_\alpha(\theta)
\end{smalleq}

The contact terms in Eq.~\eqref{contactTerms} contain an Abelian factor $\dot C_\nu(\theta_1)$, which can be brought down by varying the Wilson loop by the Abelian part of the momentum loop:
\begin{smalleq}
    \dot C_\nu(\theta_1) \Rightarrow \frac{-\I}{2} \fbyf{}{P^0_\nu(\theta_1-0)} + \frac{-\I}{2} \fbyf{}{P^0_\nu(\theta_1+0)}
\end{smalleq}

Following the argument from the Abelian case, we require that this variation vanishes. This leads to the constraint on the measure:
\begin{smalleq}
    \fbyf{W[P_{ab}]}{P^0_\mu(\theta_a+0)} = \fbyf{W[P_{ab}]}{P^0_\mu(\theta_b-0)} = 0
\end{smalleq}
where $\theta_a$ and $\theta_b$ denote the initial and final points of the random walk.

\subsubsection{Discrete approximation of the $P$-manifold constraints}\label{extraFG}

To enforce the contact term constraints, we discretize the circle into angles $\theta_k = \frac{2\pi k}{N}$, with $N \to \infty$. Each $P_\mu^a(\theta_k)$ satisfies a normalization constraint defined by a quadratic form $\hat M$:
\begin{smalleq}\label{surfaces}
    && M^{a \mu}_{b \nu} = \delta_{\mu\nu} \left( A\, \delta_{ab} + (B - A)\, \delta_{a0} \delta_{0b} \right);\\
    && A\, |\hat P(\theta_k)|^2 + B\, |P^0(\theta_k)|^2 \le 1; \quad k = 1, \dots, N
\end{smalleq}

The intermediate points $P(\theta_2), \dots, P(\theta_{N-2})$ satisfy the recurrent duality equations (with periodicity $P(\theta_0) = P(\theta_N)$):
\begin{smalleq}
    P^a_\lambda(\theta_k)\, f_{acr}\, \bar \eta^i_{\lambda\rho} P^c_\rho(\theta_{k+1}) = 0, \quad \forall k = 2, \dots, N-2
\end{smalleq}

To satisfy the contact term constraints, we add the following equalities at the endpoints:
\begin{subequations}\label{contactConstraints}
\begin{smalleq}
    && F_{m+1} = 1 - A\, |\hat P(\theta_1)|^2; \\
    && F_{m+2} = 1 - A\, |\hat P(\theta_{N-1})|^2
\end{smalleq}
\end{subequations}



The only terms in $W[P]$ involving $P^0$ and $\hat P$ are of the form:
\begin{smalleq}
    \theta\left(1 - A\, |\hat P(\theta_1)|^2 - B\, |P^0(\theta_1)|^2\right)\,
    \delta\left(1 - A\, |\hat P(\theta_1)|^2\right)
\end{smalleq}

Varying the step function yields a delta function contribution:
\begin{smalleq}
    2 B\, \bar P^0(\theta_1)
    \delta\left(1 - A\, |\hat P(\theta_1)|^2 - B\, |P^0(\theta_1)|^2\right)\,
    \delta\left(1 - A\, |\hat P(\theta_1)|^2\right)
\end{smalleq}

This vanishes due to the second delta function, whose argument sets the first delta function to enforce:
\begin{smalleq}
    |P^0(\theta_1)| = 0
\end{smalleq}
Thus, $P^0(\theta_1)$ vanishes by virtue of the  $F$ constraint. To permit a broader set of solutions, we could impose a softened constraint $F_{m+1} = 1 - \epsilon - A\, |\hat P(\theta_1)|^2$, restricting $P^0$ to  a small sphere:
\begin{smalleq}
    |P^0(\theta_1)| = \epsilon 
\end{smalleq}
Hence, the condition \eqref{contactTerms} is satisfied in the distributional sense. The extremum of $W[P]$ at $P^0(\theta_1)$ is now degenerate, resulting in a nontrivial distribution over the sphere and a nontrivial dependence of $W[C]$ on the Abelian part.

The same analysis applies at the endpoint $\theta_{N-1}$ via the constraint $F_{m+2}$.

Note that the non-Abelian components $\hat P(\theta_1)$ and $\hat P(\theta_{N-1})$ remain random points on the sphere of radius $1/\sqrt{A}$, effectively serving as the initial and final points of the random walk. The periodicity condition $P(\theta_0) = P(\theta_N) = 0$ remains enforced.

\section{Is there an area law?}
Our solution is manifestly nonperturbative, but is there a mass gap? The ADS/CFT duality appeared to be a string theory, yet it described the conformal field theory without a gap in the mass spectrum.
The most obvious test would be to verify the area law for a large circular loop. 
\begin{smalleq}
    C(\theta) = L \left\{ \cos\theta, \sin\theta, 0, 0\right\}; L \to \8
\end{smalleq}
The abelian factor in our solution will be oscillating, suggesting the complex saddle point
\begin{smalleq}
    &&P^0_\mu(\theta) \propto \I T_{\mu\nu}C_\nu(\theta);\\
    && \I \int d \theta C_\mu'(\theta) P^0_\mu(\theta) \propto -T_{1 2} \pi L^2
\end{smalleq}

This scenario needs to be elaborated, of course. The tensor $T_{\mu \nu}$ needs to be proportional to the normal tensor of this minimal surface
\begin{smalleq}
   && T_{\mu\nu} \propto \frac{\Sigma_{\mu\nu}}{\norm{\Sigma} } ;\\
   && \Sigma_{\mu\nu} = \int d \theta C'_\mu(\theta) C_\nu(\theta) \propto \pi L^2\left(\delta_{\mu 1}\delta_{\nu 2} - \delta_{\nu 1}\delta_{\mu 2}\right);
\end{smalleq}

How can we obtain such a saddle point trajectory in a self-dual random walk on the ball?

We introduce the Fourier integrals for the constraints and write 
\begin{smalleq}
    &&W[P] \propto \int [D P] \iint_{-\8}^\8 [d \xi ] \exp{\I \sum_{k=1}^{m+2} \xi (\theta_k) \cdot F_{k}}\nonumber\\
    && \iint_{-\8}^\8 [d\eta] \iint_{0}^\8 [d \lambda]\exp{\I \sum_{k=1}^{n} \eta(\theta_k) (G_k -\lambda(\theta_k))}
\end{smalleq}
where the conditions $F_k, G_k$ were defined above, in \ref{pmanifold}, \ref{extraFG}.

\section{Equations of motion and minimal surfaces}
The direct path to the proof of the area law would be to study saddle points of this multiple integral in the complex plane.
There is, however, an indirect way-- study the area law Anzatz in the coordinate space, given the known structure of our solution. Namely, our solution for the Wilson loop satisfies the classical part of the loop equations, with the contact terms vanishing by themselves as a result of extra constraints on the momentum loop trajectories $P(\theta)$.

In our solution, this classical part of the loop equation reduces to the Bianchi identity, which is manifest in momentum loop space. However, it imposes nontrivial restrictions on the solution in coordinate loop space. This issue was studied in \cite{M23PR, migdal2024quantum} in the context of the turbulence theory. Let us reformulate and verify this condition n for the Area law in QCD in four dimensions.

As we established in the new loop calculus \ref{LoopCalculus}, the local derivative $\pd{C_\mu(\theta)}$ of any Stokes-type functional vanishes; therefore, the loop operator applied to any Stokes-type functional $G(F[C])$ obeys the Leibniz rule
\begin{smalleq}
    \pd{C_\alpha(\theta)} \ff{\sigma_{\mu\nu}(\theta)}G(F[C]) = G'(F[C]) \pd{C_\alpha(\theta)} \ff{\sigma_{\mu\nu}(\theta)}F[C]
\end{smalleq}
\subsection{Regularized minimal area and its area derivative}
The area $|S|$ of any smooth surface $S$ bounded by the given loop $C$ can be represented as a limit of a double surface integral
\begin{smalleq}
    &&|S|_\epsilon = A(\epsilon)\int_{x,y \in S} d \sigma_{\mu\nu}(x) d \sigma_{\mu\nu}(y) \delta_{\epsilon}((x-y)^2);\\
    && d \sigma_{\mu\nu}(x) = d X_\mu(\xi,\eta) \wedge d X_\nu(\xi,\eta) = t_{\mu\nu}(\xi) d^2 \xi \sqrt{g(\xi)};\\
    && S: x_\mu = X_\mu(\xi_1, \xi_2), X(\xi_1, \xi_2) \in \mathbb R^4;\\\
    && \lim_{\epsilon\to 0} \delta_\epsilon((x-y)^2)  = \delta^4(x-y)
\end{smalleq}
This $\delta_\epsilon((x-y)^2)$ is a kernel approximating the delta function, say, the Gaussian distribution with variance $\epsilon^2$. In the limit $\epsilon\to 0$, the infinitesimal vicinity $|x-y|\sim \epsilon$ will contribute to the double surface integral, so that with a proper normalization constant $A(\epsilon)$, the double tensor integral reduces to a single area integral
\begin{smalleq}
    &&|S|_\epsilon \to A(\epsilon) \int d^2\xi \sqrt{g(\xi)} \left(t_{\mu\nu}(\xi)\right)^2 \int d^2 \eta \sqrt{g(\xi)} \delta_{\epsilon}\left(g_{i j}(\xi) \eta_i \eta_j\right)\to \nonumber\\
    && A(\epsilon) B(\epsilon) \int d^2\xi \sqrt{g(\xi)};\\
    && B(\epsilon) = \int d^2 \eta \sqrt{g(\xi)} \delta_{\epsilon}\left(g_{i j}(\xi) \eta_i \eta_j\right)
\end{smalleq}
This constant $B(\epsilon)$ does not depend on the coordinates $\xi$ at the surface by the parametric invariance.
We observe that by choosing $A(\epsilon) = 1/B(\epsilon)$ we reproduce the standard definition of the area in the local limit $\epsilon \to 0$.

On the other hand, this double area integral at finite $\epsilon$ represents the Stokes-type functional, with area derivative
\begin{smalleq}
    &&\ff{\sigma_{\mu\nu}(\theta)}|S|_\epsilon = 2 A(\epsilon)\int_{y \in S} d \sigma_{\mu\nu}(y) \delta_{\epsilon}\left((C(\theta)-y)^2\right);
\end{smalleq}
In the limit $\epsilon\to 0$ this are derivative becomes the normal tensor $t_{\mu\nu}$ at the boundary $C$ of the surface
\begin{smalleq}
    &&\ff{\sigma_{\mu\nu}(\theta)}|S|_\epsilon \to 2 A(\epsilon) t_{\mu\nu}(C(\theta)) \int d^2 \eta \sqrt{g(\xi+\eta)} \delta_{\epsilon}\left(g_{i j}(\xi) \eta_i \eta_j\right) = 2  t_{\mu\nu}(C(\theta))
\end{smalleq}
Note the factor of $2$ in front of the tangent tensor. This factor arose due to the symmetry of the regularized area $|S|_\epsilon$: we had to differentiate each of the two integrals by $ x$ and $ y$. Should we use the local formula
\begin{smalleq}
    |S|_0 = \int d^2\xi \sqrt{g(\xi)}
\end{smalleq}
we would have to find the variation of the determinant of the metric tensor at the boundary. Such a calculation is possible, using explicit formulas for the tangent and normal derivatives of the equation for the minimum surface we describe below. The naive formula, used sometimes in string theory
\begin{smalleq}
    \delta |S| \stackrel{?}{=} \int_{\delta S} d \sigma_{\mu\nu} t_{\mu\nu}
\end{smalleq}
misses this factor of $2$.
\subsection{The Bianchi identity = Plateau equation}
Coming back to the Bianchi identity in the loop calculus, it would require the following property of this normal tensor
\begin{smalleq}
    e_{\alpha\mu\nu\gamma}\pd{C_\alpha(\theta)}t_{\mu\nu}(C(\theta)) =0;
\end{smalleq}
Let us prove that this equation is equivalent to the requirement of vanishing mean external curvature-- the Plateau equation for the minimal surface.
Let us derive this Plateau equation from the first principle.
The minimal surface should be extremal for a first variation of the following kind: add a small bump on one side and find the resulting area. Then subtract the area of a surface without a bump.
There are two directions $\lambda \rho$ orthogonal to the plane $\mu\nu$ in four dimensions. Let us fix the direction $\lambda$ and make a bump $\delta S_{\lambda\rho}$ in direction $\rho$.
The variation of the minimal area $A_{min}[S]$ reduces to a surface integral over an infinitesimal closed surface $\delta S_{\lambda\rho}$ made of this bump and its shadow on the original surface:
\begin{smalleq}
    \delta A_{min}[S] = 2\int_{x \in \delta S_{\lambda\rho}} d \sigma_{\mu\nu}(x) t_{\mu\nu}(x)
\end{smalleq}
By the Stokes-Gauss theorem, this is equal to a volume integral of the corresponding divergence over the infinitesimal 3-volume $\delta V_{\lambda\rho}$ bounded by this surface $\delta S_{\lambda\rho}$. In the general frame, in invariant form
\begin{smalleq}
    \delta A_{min}[S] \propto \int_{y \in \delta V_{\lambda\rho}}  e_{\lambda\rho\mu\nu}\pd{y_\rho} t_{\mu\nu}(y) =0
\end{smalleq}
This equation is satisfied by the minimal surface, and it is equivalent to the Bianchi identity of the loop calculus. For the surface, this is a divergency of the normal tensor. The vanishing divergence of this tensor is equivalent to zero mean curvature of the surface, the so-called Plateau equation.

We conclude that a minimal surface satisfies the necessary part of the loop equations: the Bianchi identity.
\subsection{Classical loop equation for the minimal area}
The equation of motion would require an extra condition
\begin{smalleq}
   \int d \theta \dot C_\nu(\theta) \left(\ff{\dot C_\mu(\theta+)} - \ff{\dot C_\mu(\theta-)}\right) t_{\mu\nu}(C(\theta)) =0 
\end{smalleq}

Here is an important detail. In the coordinate loop space, the generic loop is not self-intersecting, therefore, the loop equation has to be satisfied for such loops \textbf{without contact terms}. Our solution, as we have seen above, satisfies \textbf{both} equations: it nullifies the classical one, and on top of that, it matches the contact terms (by nullifying them).

By this logic, the minimal area Anzatz
\begin{smalleq}
    W[C] \sim Z[C]\exp{ - a A_{min}[C]}
\end{smalleq}
should satisfy the classical loop equation asymptotically at large area. This is the WKB approximation to the loop equation, regarded as the Schrödinger equation in loop space \cite{Mig83, M23PR}. 
\begin{smalleq}\label{Amineq}
    \pd{\mu} \ff{\sigma_{\mu\nu}(\theta)}A_{min}[C] =0
\end{smalleq}
The pre-exponential factor $Z[C]$ must be handled in the next WKB approximations, and here the contact terms must become relevant.
Let us concentrate on the leading term \eqref{Amineq}.

\subsection{Area Derivative of the Minimal Surface and the Tangent Bivector}

We consider a minimal surface $X^\mu(r,\theta)$ in Euclidean space, parametrized by a radial coordinate $r \in [0,1]$ and an angular coordinate $\theta \in [0,2\pi]$. The boundary of the surface lies at $r=0$ and is given by a closed contour $C^\mu(\theta) = X^\mu(0,\theta)$.

Let us define the tangent vector to the boundary loop. Up to normalization, it is the derivative of the loop.
\begin{smalleq}
\dot{C}^\mu(\theta) = \frac{d C^\mu(\theta)}{d\theta}.
\end{smalleq}

The normal derivative of the surface at the boundary, $\partial_r X^\mu(0,\theta)$, defines the direction in which the surface grows inward from the contour. For a minimal surface (zero mean curvature), this normal derivative is related to the Hilbert transform of the tangent vector:
\begin{smalleq}\label{HilbertTransform}
\partial_r X^\mu(0,\theta) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \, \text{P.V.} \int_0^{2\pi} \dot{C}^\mu(\theta') \cot\left( \frac{\theta - \theta'}{2} \right) d\theta',
\label{eq:hilbert-normal}
\end{smalleq}
where the integral is understood in the principal value sense. This result follows from classical complex analysis and conformal parametrizations of minimal surfaces \cite[Chapter~7]{Duren2004}.

We now define the antisymmetric tensor at the boundary:
\begin{smalleq}\label{regTensor}
&&t^{\mu\nu}(\theta) = \frac{\dot{C}^\mu(\theta) \, \partial_r X^\nu(0,\theta) - \dot{C}^\nu(\theta) \, \partial_r X^\mu(0,\theta)}{Z},\\
&& Z = \sqrt{\left(\dot{C}^\mu(\theta) \, \partial_r X^\nu(0,\theta) - \dot{C}^\nu(\theta) \, \partial_r X^\mu(0,\theta)\right)^2}
\end{smalleq}
which is the tangent bivector to the surface at the boundary point $C(\theta)$. This bivector is proportional to the oriented area element spanned by the vectors tangent to the surface at the boundary.

This principal value corresponds to replacement
\begin{smalleq}\label{regCot}
    \cot_\epsilon\left( \frac{\theta - \theta'}{2} \right) = \frac{\tan\left( \frac{\theta - \theta'}{2} \right)}{\epsilon^2 + \tan^2\left( \frac{\theta - \theta'}{2} \right)}
\end{smalleq}
This parameter $\epsilon$ has the same meaning as the parameter $\epsilon$ in the regularized area $|S|_\epsilon$. The point-splitted area  corresponds to the regularized tangent tensor
\begin{smalleq}
    t_\epsilon^{\mu\nu}(\theta) \propto \int_S d \sigma_{\mu\nu}(y) \delta_{\epsilon}((C(\theta)-y)^2)
\end{smalleq}
This is an integral over the surface form in the vicinity of the boundary point $x =C(\theta)$. At finite $\epsilon$ it is differentiable by variations of $C(\theta)$. The gradient $\pd{C(\theta)}$, which we represented as a discontinuity of the dot functional derivative, would yield zero when applied to a tensor that is integral like this one. This is a particular case of the Stokes-type functional, so the rule \eqref{annihil} will apply.

We can verify that by explicit computation of the $t_{\mu\nu}$ tensor \eqref{regTensor} with the Hilbert kernel regularized by \eqref{regCot}. Taking a discontinuity of the dot functional derivative, we note that the local dependence of this tensor on $\dot C(\theta)$ does not contribute. to this discontinuity. This is because the delta function is an even function
\begin{smalleq}
    &&\fbyf{\dot C(\theta)}{\dot C(\theta+ \lambda)} \propto \delta(\lambda);\\
    && \delta(\lambda) - \delta(-\lambda) =0
\end{smalleq}
The derivatives of this tensor on $\partial_r X^\nu(0,\theta)$  bring the nonzero contribution proportional to
\begin{smalleq}
    &&\fbyf{\partial_r X^\nu(0,\theta)}{\dot C(\theta+ \lambda)}\propto \cot_\epsilon\left( \frac{\lambda}{2} \right) ;
\end{smalleq}
 The difference of these dot derivatives at $\pm \lambda$ yields:
\begin{smalleq}
     \cot_\epsilon\left( \frac{\lambda}{2} \right) -\cot_\epsilon\left( \frac{-\lambda}{2} \right)   \to \frac{\lambda}{\oq \lambda^2 +\epsilon^2 }
\end{smalleq}
If we set $\lambda =0 $ before setting $\epsilon\to 0$ we get a zero discontinuity, in accordance with the above argument of the regularized normal tensor. 

The opposite limit, setting $\epsilon=0$ first, yields $\pm\8$ in the $\lambda\to 0$ limit.
Which limit is a correct one?

As we previously discussed, the area is not a Stokes-type functional; it needs to be regularized before being substituted into the loop equation. The true asymptotic behavior of the Wilson loop at large area cannot be precisely the area law; it must be some Stokes-type functional reaching the limit of the area when regularization is removed.

Therefore, we must keep regularization finite when substituting it into the loop equation and remove regularization only after computing the loop operators.
This limit corresponds to $\lambda \to 0$ before $\epsilon\to 0$. In this limit, the loop equation is satisfied in a trivial way, but the Bianchi identity is only satisfied by the Plateau equation for the minimal surface.

Also note that the Bianchi identity can be verified from this representation of the area derivative of the minimal surface \eqref{regTensor},\eqref{HilbertTransform}. Applying the dot-derivative $\ff{\dot C_\alpha(\theta_1)}$ to the tensor $t_{\mu\nu}(\theta)$, we get in the limit $\theta_1\to \theta$ one of the four tensor structures, all vanishing.
\begin{smalleq}
    &&e_{\lambda\alpha\mu\nu} \delta_{\alpha\mu } =0;\\
    && e_{\lambda\alpha\mu\nu} \delta_{\alpha\nu }=0;\\
    &&e_{\lambda\alpha\mu\nu} \partial_r X^\nu(0,\theta) \partial_r X^\alpha(0,\theta)  =0;\\
    && e_{\lambda\alpha\mu\nu}  \dot C_\alpha(\theta) \dot C_\mu(\theta)=0;
\end{smalleq}
\subsection{The contact terms}

The contact terms already involve the next correction, i.e. one has to apply the loop operator to pre-factor $Z[C]$. Matching it with the contact terms, we have to match the exponential factors, in addition to matching the result of the loop operator applied to $Z[C]$ with the coefficient $\int d\theta_1\dot C_\nu(\theta_1)\delta^4(C(\theta) - C(\theta_1) $ in front of the product of the exponential factors. Matching the exponential factors we get
\begin{smalleq}
    \exp{-F[C]} = \exp{-F[C_{12}]}\exp{-F[C_{21}]}
\end{smalleq}
This condition is satisfied for $F[C] = a A_{min}[C]$. When a loop self-intersects, the global minimum of the area is achieved by
\begin{smalleq}
    A_{min}[C_{12} \uplus C_{2 1}] = A_{min}[C_{12}] + A_{min}[C_{21}] 
\end{smalleq}
There could be a cylindrical minimal surface connecting these two loops. However, while being locally minimal (having zero mean curvature everywhere), it, in most cases, has an area larger than the sum of the minimal areas of the two loops.

There could be exceptions from this additivity for some twisted loops. The area law only applies to large, smooth loops, not to singular cases.

However, the quark confinement does not require the area law to be universal, it would suffice to have this law for a square or a circle.

\subsection{Self-dual minimal surfaces in extended bundle space: a conjecture}


It is often assumed that the area law in gauge theory arises from a string-theoretic picture in which the expectation value of the Wilson loop is given by the exponential of a classical minimal surface in $\mathbb{R}^4$. In particular, in the AdS/CFT correspondence, the minimal surface lives in a higher-dimensional curved space (such as AdS$_5$), while its boundary is constrained to physical spacetime $\mathbb{R}^4$ where the Wilson loop is defined.

In the present work, we construct a solution to the planar QCD loop equation using a dual formulation in terms of matrix-valued momentum loops $P(\theta)$ satisfying a set of self-duality constraints. The solution exhibits features often associated with string-theoretic duals—such as reflection-conjugation symmetry and a compact target space for the dual variables—yet it does not reduce to a classical minimal surface in $\mathbb{R}^4$. The reason lies in the structure of the self-duality constraint.

In our matrix solution, the area derivative of the Wilson loop is either self-dual or anti-dual:
\begin{smalleq}
0 = \frac{\delta W[C]}{\delta \sigma_{\mu\nu}(\theta)}  
\begin{cases}
  \bar\eta^i_{\mu\nu} & \text{(dual)} \\
  \eta^i_{\mu\nu} & \text{(anti-dual)}
\end{cases}
\end{smalleq}
where $\eta^i_{\mu\nu}$ and $\bar\eta^i_{\mu\nu}$ are the standard 't Hooft symbols. 

The classical minimal surface in $\mathbb{R}^4$—defined by the Plateau problem with boundary $C(\theta)$—has an area element given locally by the wedge product of two tangent vectors at the boundary. The area derivative, as we have seen, is proportional to this area element:
\begin{smalleq}
\frac{\delta \exp{-A_{\min}[C]}}{\delta \sigma_{\mu\nu}(\theta)} 
\propto t_{\mu\nu}(\theta) 
\propto \dot{C}_\mu(\theta) \wedge \partial_r X_\nu(r=0,\theta).
\end{smalleq}
This bivector is real and antisymmetric, but cannot be purely self-dual or anti-self-dual. The wedge product of two real tangent vectors in $\mathbb{R}^4$ spans the six-dimensional space of antisymmetric tensors, not the three-dimensional self-dual or anti-self-dual subspaces. Hence, the minimal surface in physical space cannot satisfy our duality constraint, even in the classical limit.

However, it is known \cite{TorralboUrbano2016} from differential geometry that the space of oriented 2-planes in \(\mathbb{C}^4\)---the Grassmannian \(G_2^+(\mathbb{C}^4)\)---decomposes as \(S^2_+ \times S^2_-\), where \(S^2_+\) corresponds to the image of the self-dual part of the Gauss map. This suggests a refined interpretation: rather than viewing the minimal surface as lying entirely in \(\mathbb{C}^4\), one can consider a lift of the surface into an extended bundle over the disk with fibers in \(\mathbb{C}^4 \otimes \mathfrak{g} \times (S^2_+)^{\mathcal{N}+1}\). Each component of the matrix-valued field \(P(\theta_1,\theta_2)\) projects its area element into the self-dual subspace of \(\Lambda^2(\mathbb{C}^4)\), labeled by internal indices.
\newtheorem{conjecture}{Conjecture}
\begin{conjecture}{\textbf{Matrix Surface Duality}}
In the classical (WKB) limit, the matrix-valued momentum loop ensemble corresponds to a generalized minimal surface embedded in a bundle over the disk with fibers in \(\mathbb{C}^4 \otimes \mathfrak{g} \times (S^2_+)^{\mathcal{N}+1}\). At each point, the tangent bivectors lie in the self-dual subspace of \(\Lambda^2(\mathbb{C}^4)\), and the duality constraints project them accordingly. This construction captures the internal matrix structure of the ensemble and provides a geometric foundation for the dual surface interpretation of the planar QCD loop equation.
\end{conjecture}

This conjecture (inspired by a private communication from Semon Rezchikov) refines the earlier proposal based on abstract noncommutative geometry, replacing it with a concrete geometric structure tailored to the \(3(\mathcal{N}+1)\) component duality constraint. The physical loop \(C(\theta) \subset \mathbb{R}^4\) remains the boundary of the surface, but the dual surface itself lives in a bundle space carrying both spacetime and internal algebraic structure. The matrix nature of the momenta induces a family of self-dual Gauss maps, one for each internal generator.

We describe the geometry of this conjecture in more detail in next publication.

\subsection{Conclusion}

In conclusion, we have not yet proven the area law—but we have taken a decisive step forward. We showed that the area law Ansatz satisfies the full planar loop equation, including its classical and contact terms, when properly regularized. To our knowledge, this is the first such verification in a continuum formulation. The key advance enabling this result is the new loop calculus we developed, based on functional derivatives with respect to the loop velocity $\dot C(\theta)$. This formalism ensures that both the classical term and the loop-space Bianchi identity are treated consistently.

However, we have not yet demonstrated that the matrix-valued random walk ensemble proposed here asymptotically leads to the area law. This ensemble may instead yield another consistent asymptotic regime, such as a perimeter law. To resolve this, a saddle-point analysis of the large-loop limit—e.g., a circular loop—is required.

More significantly, we have discovered that our self-dual ensemble cannot correspond to a classical minimal surface in $\mathbb{R}^4$, as such surfaces cannot support self-dual area elements. This led us to propose a new geometric conjecture: the matrix-valued momentum loops describe a generalized minimal surface embedded in a higher-dimensional matrix space, where tangent bivectors can be self-dual. In this setting, the loop equation is satisfied as a consequence of matrix geometry and the loop-space Bianchi identity. Exploring this matrix surface picture may provide a new route toward a QCD string dual.

\section{Summary}

In this work, we constructed an exact solution of the planar QCD loop equation in four-dimensional Euclidean space using a novel matrix-valued momentum loop formalism. Central to this construction is a new version of loop calculus, formulated in terms of functional derivatives with respect to the loop velocity $\dot C(\theta)$. This framework yields well-defined, finite expressions for area and point derivatives in loop space and highlights the role of the loop-space Bianchi identity in ensuring consistency with the underlying gauge theory.

We proposed a family of solutions defined by matrix-valued random walks on compact group manifolds, constrained by a self-duality condition imposed on the momentum bivectors. These constraints ensure the vanishing of the classical (Yang–Mills) part of the loop equation. To also nullify the contact terms—which arise in the planar ($N \to \infty$) limit—we imposed additional boundary and continuity conditions at the endpoints of the momentum loops. The resulting ensemble satisfies the full planar QCD loop equation in the continuum, without gauge fixing or lattice regularization.

A key structural feature of this solution is its invariance under a reflection–conjugation symmetry: loop orientation reversal combined with complex conjugation maps one solution of the duality constraints to another. This symmetry holds for an arbitrary compact Lie algebra used in the momentum ensemble and supports the robustness of the solution under reversal of the loop orientation.

We emphasize, however, that the solution is not unique. The internal group used in the momentum space construction is not constrained by the original SU($N$) gauge group of the QCD action. Rather, it enters as an auxiliary structure in the dual formulation. Whether any particular group is dynamically preferred remains an open question.

To explore the asymptotic behavior, we analyzed the area law Ansatz using our loop calculus. We verified that the regularized minimal surface representation satisfies the classical loop equation and the Bianchi identity. The contact term structure is consistent with the multiplicative property of Wilson loops over self-intersecting contours, provided regularization is removed only after loop operators act. This makes the area law a consistent WKB solution to the loop equation.

Nonetheless, we found that the classical minimal surface in $\mathbb{R}^4$ cannot be self-dual and therefore cannot represent a dual solution in our matrix framework. This led us to conjecture the existence of a higher-dimensional matrix surface—embedded in a compact space of Hermitian matrices—whose area element can be self-dual and which satisfies both the duality constraint and loop-space Bianchi identity. Constructing such a surface explicitly would provide a string-theoretic interpretation of our solution and potentially a nonperturbative dual of QCD.

Whether the matrix ensemble follows the area law in the large-loop limit, or exhibits a different phase, remains the subject of future work.

