\section{ Self-dual area elements and matrix-valued surfaces in \( \mathbb{R}^4 \times S^2_+ \)}

In this appendix, we explore a possible geometric realization of the self-duality constraint imposed on the momentum variables \( P_\mu(\theta) \) in our matrix ensemble. We begin by revisiting the structure of the constraint and its relation to classical differential geometry.

The duality condition arises from the bilinear form
\begin{equation}
Q_{i c}(P_1, P_2) = \operatorname{Tr}(P_\mu(1) P_\nu(2)\, \bar\eta^i_{\mu\nu}\, T_c),
\end{equation}
which is covariant under both spacetime and internal group transformations. The spacetime indices \( \mu, \nu \) are contracted with the anti-self-dual 't Hooft symbols \( \bar\eta^i_{\mu\nu} \), while the trace over internal Lie algebra generators \( T_c \) ensures gauge invariance.

The functional \( Q_{i c}(P_1, P_2) \) therefore defines a family of scalar-valued constraints labeled by the index pair \( (i, c) \), where \( i = 1,2,3 \) and \( c = 0,1,\dots,\mathcal{N} \). These constraints play the role of local self-duality conditions. In discretized form, they appear as
\begin{equation}
Q_{i c}(P(\theta), P(\theta + \delta)) = 0,
\end{equation}
imposing self-duality between neighboring values of \( P_\mu(\theta) \) along the loop.

To define an area element over a two-dimensional surface with the topology of a disk in parameter space \( (\theta_1, \theta_2) \), we promote the constraint to the tangent vectors of the surface:
\begin{equation}
\Omega_{i,c}(P) = e^{\alpha\beta}Q_{i c}(\partial_\alpha P, \partial_\beta P) = 0, \quad \quad i=1,2,3,\quad c = 0,1,\dots,\mathcal{N},
\end{equation}
Note that this $\Omega_{i,c}(P)$ is a real quadratic form: the imaginary antisymmetric  part of the triple trace $ \operatorname{Tr}[T_a T_b] T_c $  dropped because of symmetry $(a,\mu) \leftrightarrow (b,\nu)$. 
Therefore, this constraint now has real solutions. Also note, that this $\Omega$ form is covariant, so that these constraints are parametric invariant as well as covariant with respect to space and internal symmetry groups.
One can write $\Omega $ in a maniferstly real symmetric form
\begin{equation}
    \Omega_{i,c}(P) = e^{\alpha\beta}\partial_\alpha P^a_\mu  \partial_\beta P^b_\nu d_{a b c} \eta^i_{\mu\nu}
\end{equation}
Altogether, this defines \( 3(\mathcal{N} + 1) \) real equations for \( 4(\mathcal{N} + 1) \) independent real components of $P(\theta_1,\theta_2)$ at each point of the surface, ensuring that the antisymmetric part of the tangent bivectors lies in the self-dual subspace of \( \Lambda^2(\mathbb{R}^4) \) for each component in the internal space.

We propose that this generalized surface, defined by a field \( P(\theta_1, \theta_2) \), is not embedded in physical spacetime \( \mathbb{R}^4 \), but in a higher-dimensional vector bundle over a disk in the parameter plane, with fibers in \( \mathbb{R}^{4(\mathcal{N}+1)} \). Each point on the surface carries a real vector \( P_\mu^c \), i.e., a real 4-vector in spacetime labeled by a Lie algebra generator. The self-duality constraints ensure that for each component \( c \), the induced area element \( d P_\mu^c \wedge d P_\nu^c \) lies in the self-dual subspace of \( \Lambda^2(\mathbb{R}^4) \).

The Gauss map then associates to each point a family of self-dual 2-planes in \( \mathbb{R}^4 \), labeled by the internal index \( c \). Classical differential geometry shows that the oriented Grassmannian \( G_2^+(\mathbb{R}^4) \) decomposes into \( S^2_+ \times S^2_- \), and restricting to \( S^2_+ \) corresponds to purely self-dual tangent planes \cite{TorralboUrbano2016}. Thus, our constraint lifts the geometry into a product space that includes both spacetime and internal structure.

Unlike classical minimal surfaces in \( \mathbb{R}^4 \times S^2_+ \), where the Gauss map assigns a single 
self-dual 2-plane to each point, our formulation imposes \( (\mathcal{N}+1) \) independent self-duality 
constraints—one for each spacetime direction and Lie algebra generator. Consequently, the dual surface is 
not simply a map into \( \mathbb{R}^4 \times S^2_+ \), but rather into a higher-dimensional fiber bundle 
over the disk, with fibers in \( \mathbb{R}^4 \otimes \mathfrak{g} \times (S^2_+)^{\mathcal{N}+1} \). Each 
component of the internal algebra carries its own self-dual structure, reflecting the matrix nature of the 
momentum variables. This richer target space goes beyond the scope of standard differential geometry and 
represents a matrix-valued generalization of the classical Gauss map, compatible with the self-duality 
structure of the Wilson loop in the dual ensemble.

This provides a geometric framework for our duality condition: the matrix ensemble defines a surface in a vector bundle over the disk with structure group including both spacetime and internal degrees of freedom. The self-duality condition is realized as a projection of all tangent bivectors onto the self-dual subspace in spacetime, uniformly across all internal components.

The area functional in conformal metric is then defined byas follows
\begin{equation}
    A[C] = \int d^2 \theta \left(\oh \tr \pd{\alpha} P_\mu \pd{\alpha} P_\mu + \lambda^{i c} \Omega_{i,c}(P)  \right)
\end{equation}
Here $\lambda^{i c}(\theta)$ is the Lagrange multiplier.

The variation leads to a linear equation
\begin{equation}
   \left( \pd{\theta}^2 \delta_{\mu\nu} \delta_{a b}+ \eta^i_{\mu\nu} d_{a b c}\lambda^{i c}\right) P^c_\nu(\theta) =0
\end{equation}
with an extra condition 
\begin{equation}
   P^a_\mu\eta^i_{\mu\nu} d_{a b c}P^b_\nu =0
\end{equation}
The boundary conditions will be Dirichlet type
\begin{eqnarray}
   && P^0_\mu(0,\theta) = C_\mu(\theta);\\
   && P^a_\mu(0,\theta) = 0 \forall a >0
\end{eqnarray}
This boundary condition ensures that the surface is bounded by our loop $C$. The tangent tensor at the boundary
\begin{equation}
    t_{\mu\nu} \propto \left(\dot C_{\mu}(\theta) \pd{r} P_\nu^a(0,\theta)- \dot C_{\nu}(\theta) \pd{r} P_\mu^a(0,\theta)\right)
\end{equation}
It remains to be proven that this tensor is self-dual by virtue of the $\Omega $ constraints.

This construction links the self-dual matrix ensemble with a generalization of classical minimal surfaces to matrix-valued geometry with duality constraints.

Constructing explicit solutions to these constraints—i.e., finding fields \( P(\theta_1, \theta_2) \) satisfying both the duality and minimality conditions—remains an open problem. Such surfaces, if they exist, may provide a geometric foundation for understanding the confining phase of planar QCD through the dual matrix ensemble.

