\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusions}

In this paper, we develop a new framework for the estimation of Sobol' indices using importance sampling.  
We demonstrate that $\mathbb{E}\left[ m_u^2(\mathbf{X}_u) \right]$ can be estimated from any sampling distribution different from the reference one. In particular, we show that by choosing optimal densities, it is possible to achieve a lower bound on the asymptotic variance than the efficiency bound established in \cite{DaVeiga2013}. In some special cases, zero-variance estimation can theoretically be achieved; however, this is not feasible in practice since the optimal densities depend on unknown quantities. We also show that combining our method with the rank-based estimator \cite{Gamboa2022} is particularly useful for studying the distributional sensitivity of Sobol' indices, especially in the given-data setting where samples can be reused efficiently through the reverse importance sampling mechanism \cite{Lemaitre2015}.
Our approach can be naturally extended to other contexts, such as the given-model setting where a computational budget is available to evaluate the black-box model. In such cases, we expect to approximate the optimal densities presented in our framework and achieve significant variance reduction. Future work may focus on constructing joint optimal densities for the simultaneous estimation of all \(\eta_u\), rather than treating them independently.


