\begin{abstract}
In statistical physics, a well-accepted knowledge is that  liquid-gas (LG) phase transition with diverging critical fluctuations lies in the universality class of Ising. Yet, whether non-equilibrium effect can alter this conclusion remains an open question.  Here, we study the LG criticality of a hyperuniform (HU) fluid composed of active spinners, where phase separation is induced by dissipative collisions. Strikingly, at the LG critical point, the HU fluid exhibits normal Gaussian density fluctuations instead of the diverging fluctuations.  Through hydrodynamic field theory and renormalization-group analysis, we demonstrate that hyperuniformity reduces the upper critical dimension from $d_c = 4$ (classical fluids) to $d_c = 2$ due to an emerging long-range correlation induced by center-of-mass conservation.  Large-scale stochastic field simulations confirm this dimensional reduction, and  reveal  diverging response function along with anomalous zero-range correlation function at the critical point, indicating the absence of an effective fluctuation-dissipation relationship in the system. The simulation also identifies anomalous finite-size scaling in density fluctuation, energy fluctuation, and Binder cumulant. Furthermore, HU fluid also exihibits non-classical spinodal decomposition dynamics with diverging  decomposition time but non-diverging decomposition length scale. Our results provide an exception to the  classical paradigms of LG critical phenomena  and highlight how  non-equilibrium effect  can reshape  universality classes of classical phase transitions.
\end{abstract}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The liquid-gas (LG) phase transition is a fundamental thermodynamic phenomenon observed across diverse kinds of matter.  At its critical point, the distinction between liquid and gas phases vanishes, giving rise to diverging density fluctuations, leading to striking phenomena such as critical opalescence~\cite{chu1972critical}. Pioneering work by Lee and Yang established the equivalence between the lattice gas model and the Ising model, anchoring LG criticality within the Ising universality class for equilibrium systems with short-range interactions~\cite{Lee1952,Yang1964}. This framework has been extensively validated through numerical simulations \cite{Wilding1992,Bruce1992,Wilding1997,Watanabe2012,Yarmolinsky2017,Yarmolinsky2018} and experiments \cite{Hayes1977,Pestak1984,Kim1984}.

For non-equilibrium system, however, criticality need not follow the same universality as in equilibrium counterparts~\cite{Marro1996,speck2022critical,tauber1999non, santos2002non,bertrand2022diversity,jentsch2023critical,miller2024phase}. Nonetheless, it has been increasingly recognized that an effective temperature and the corresponding Ising universality class can emerge from non-equilibrium LG or binary phase separation, provided that no additional symmetry breaking or long-range interactions are presented~\cite{Young2020,nakano2021long,ikeda2024dynamical}. Examples of such behavior include the non-equilibrium kinetic Ising model~\cite{sides1998kinetic}, the two-temperature model~\cite{szolnoki1999stationary}, 
motility-induced phase separation (MIPS) in active Brownian particle systems~\cite{Partridge2019,Dittrich2023}, active Ornstein--Uhlenbeck particle systems~\cite{Maggi2021,Maggi2022}, noise-induced phase separation~\cite{paoluzzi2024noise,paoluzzi2022scaling}, run-and-tumble particle systems~\cite{saha2024site,ray2024motility}, quorum-sensing active particle systems~\cite{Gnan2022}, and active colloidal mixtures~\cite{Han2017}.  These findings, along with ongoing debates~\cite{dittrich2021critical,siebert2018critical,bhowmick2025geometric}, raise the question of whether the Ising universality class is unique for LG criticality of fluids, irrespective of their equilibrium or non-equilibrium nature.


A parallel frontier lies in the study of disordered hyperuniformity (HU), a structural hallmark of disordered systems with suppressed long-wavelength density fluctuations \cite{Torquato2003,Torquato2018,PhysRevLett.118.020601,Tjhung2015,
Wilken2020,Chen2021,hexner2015hyperuniformity,
zheng2024universal,
oppenheimer2022hyperuniformity,lei2023does,chen2024emergent,liu2024universal, liu2023local,wang2025active}. Hyperuniform fluid states have recently been realized in active matter, including circle swimmers \cite{Lei2019,Zhang2022,Huang2021} and active spinners \cite{Lei2019h} with short range interaction.  These discoveries motivate a critical inquiry: Can the universality of LG phase transitions be altered by hyperuniformity?


