\subsection{Wilsonian Momentum Shell Renormalization Group Method}
We start to calculate the flow equations of coupling using Wilsonian momentum shell renormalization group approach~\cite{wilson1983renormalization,Papanikolaou2023}.
The first step is coarse-graining, i.e., integrating out the wave vectors in an infinitesimal shell $q \in [\Lambda/b,\Lambda]$ where $b=1+\delta l$.  Let $\nu$ be a coupling in the action ($\nu=$ $r$ or $u$), then its linear corrected coupling $\Tilde{\nu}$ due to the infinitesimal coarse-graining is $\Tilde{\nu}=\nu+C_{\nu}\nu\delta l$
with $C_{\nu}$ quantifying the graphical one-loop corrections for $\nu$ (see Eq.~(\ref{nonlinearCorrect})). 
Here we only have to consider graphs with a loop, since graphs with multiple loops are of higher order and can be neglected. The next step is to restore the cut-off $\Lambda/b \to \Lambda$ through rescaling
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \nu'=b^{\Delta_{\nu}}\Tilde{\nu}
    =
    (1+\delta l)^{\Delta_{\nu}}(1+C_{\nu}\delta l)\nu 
    \approx
    [1+(\Delta_{\nu}+C_{\nu})\delta l]\nu
\end{eqnarray*}
Then we can get Wilsonian flow equations
\begin{eqnarray}\label{DefineFlowEquations}
    \partial_l \nu = (\Delta_{\nu}+C_{\nu})\nu
\end{eqnarray}
This equation describes the change of couplings like $r$ or $u$ under the renormalization as $l$ increases.

In order to perform integration in an infinitesimal shell $q\in [\Lambda/b,\Lambda]$, it's convenient to split the fields in Infra-Red (IR) and Ultra-Violet (UV) modes, i.e.,$\psi=\psi_>+\psi_<,~\Tilde{\psi}=\Tilde{\psi}_>+\Tilde{\psi}_<$, where
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \psi_{<}(\mathbf{q},\omega)=
    \left\{
        \begin{aligned}
            &\psi(\mathbf{q},\omega),&0<q<\Lambda/b\\
            &0,&\Lambda/b<q<\Lambda
        \end{aligned}
    \right.,
    ~
    \psi_{>}(\mathbf{q},\omega)=
    \left\{
        \begin{aligned}
            &0,&0<q<\Lambda/b\\
            &\psi(\mathbf{q},\omega),&\Lambda/b<q<\Lambda
        \end{aligned}
    \right.
\end{eqnarray*} 
and
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \Tilde{\psi}_{<}(\mathbf{q},\omega)=
    \left\{
        \begin{aligned}
            &\Tilde{\psi}(\mathbf{q},\omega),&0<q<\Lambda/b\\
            &0,&\Lambda/b<q<\Lambda
        \end{aligned}
    \right.,
    ~
    \Tilde{\psi}_{>}(\mathbf{q},\omega)=
    \left\{
        \begin{aligned}
            &0,&0<q<\Lambda/b\\
            &\Tilde{\psi}(\mathbf{q},\omega),&\Lambda/b<q<\Lambda
        \end{aligned}
    \right.
\end{eqnarray*} 
The action in wavenumber space then can be written in the following form
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \mathcal{A}[\Tilde{\psi},\psi]
    =
    \mathcal{A}[\Tilde{\psi}_>+\Tilde{\psi}_<,~\psi_>+\psi_<]
    =
    \mathcal{A}_0[\Tilde{\psi}_<,\psi_<]
    +
    \mathcal{A}_0[\Tilde{\psi}_>,\psi_>]
    +
    \mathcal{A}_{I}[\Tilde{\psi}_>+\Tilde{\psi}_<,\psi_>+\psi_<]
\end{eqnarray*}
where $\mathcal{A}_0$ is the Gaussian part of the action in wavenumber space and $\mathcal{A}_{I}$ is the nonlinear part of the action in wavenumber space, where
\begin{eqnarray*}
    &&\mathcal{A}_{0}[\Tilde{\psi},{\psi}]
    =
    \int_q\int_{\omega}
     \left(
         \Tilde{\psi}(-\mathbf{q},-\omega)
         \left[{
             -i\omega+q^2(r+q^2)    
         }\right]
         {\psi}(\mathbf{q},\omega)
         -
         Dq^{2+\theta}\Tilde{\psi}(-\mathbf{q},-\omega)\Tilde{\psi}(\mathbf{q},\omega)
        \right)
    \\
    &&\mathcal{A}_{I}[\Tilde{\psi},{\psi}]
    = 
        \int_0^{\Lambda}\prod_{i=1}^4 \frac{d^dk_i}{(2\pi)^d}\int_0^{\infty}\frac{d\omega_i}{2\pi}~ \frac{uk_1^2}{6}
        \times 
        \Tilde{\psi}(\mathbf{k}_1,\omega_1)
        {\psi}(\mathbf{k}_2,\omega_2)
        {\psi}(\mathbf{k}_3,\omega_3)
        {\psi}(\mathbf{k}_4,\omega_4)
        \times (2\pi)^{d+1}\delta^d\left(\sum_i\mathbf{k}_i\right)\delta\left(\sum_i \omega_i\right)
\end{eqnarray*}
The above equations can be obtained based on Eqs.~(\ref{Fourier}) and Eqs.~(\ref{action_0}-\ref{action_ini}). After coarsening (integrating out UV modes), we can get an effective action $\mathcal{A}'$ for IR modes
\begin{eqnarray}
    e^{-\mathcal{A}'[\Tilde{\psi}_<,\psi_<]}
    =
    e^{-\mathcal{A}_0[\Tilde{\psi}_<,\psi_<]}
    \int \mathcal{D}\Tilde{\psi}_> \mathcal{D}{\psi}_>
    e^{-\mathcal{A}_0[\Tilde{\psi}_>,\psi_>]}
    e^{-\mathcal{A}_{I}[\Tilde{\psi}_>+\Tilde{\psi}_<,\psi_>+\psi_<]}
\end{eqnarray}
Taking the logarithm of both sides of the above equation yields
\begin{eqnarray}\label{nonlinearCorrect}
    \mathcal{A}'[\Tilde{\psi}_<,\psi_<]
    =
    \mathcal{A}_0[\Tilde{\psi}_<,\psi_<]
    -
    \ln \left\langle 
        e^{-\mathcal{A}_{I}[\Tilde{\psi}_>+\Tilde{\psi}_<,\psi_>+\psi_<]}
    \right\rangle_{>0}
\end{eqnarray}
where $\langle \cdots \rangle_{>0}$ represents Gaussian averaging for short wave modes. Expanding the logarithm on the right-hand side of the above equation yields
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \ln \left\langle 
        e^{-\mathcal{A}_{I}[\Tilde{\psi}_>+\Tilde{\psi}_<,\psi_>+\psi_<]}
    \right\rangle_{>0}
    =
    -\left\langle \mathcal{A}_{I} \right \rangle_{>0}
     +\frac{1}{2}\left[\left\langle \mathcal{A}_{I}^2 \right \rangle_{>0}-\left\langle \mathcal{A}_{I} \right \rangle_{>0}^2 \right] + \cdots
\end{eqnarray*}
where $\left\langle \mathcal{A}_{I} \right \rangle_{>0} $ is the first-order cumulant, which can be calculated as
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \left\langle\mathcal{A}_{I}[\Tilde{\psi}_>+\Tilde{\psi}_<,\psi_>+\psi_<]\right\rangle_{>0}
    = 
        \int_0^{\Lambda}\prod_{i=0}^4 \frac{d^dk_i}{(2\pi)^d}\int_0^{\infty}\frac{d\omega_i}{2\pi}~ \frac{uk_1^2}{6}
        \times 
        \left\langle
        \text{terms with $\psi$ and $\Tilde{\psi}$ }
        \right\rangle_{>0}
        \times (2\pi)^{d+1}\delta^d\left(\sum_i\mathbf{k}_i\right)\delta\left(\sum_i \omega_i\right)
\end{eqnarray*}
There are 8 types of averages in the above integrals: (1)$\langle \Tilde{\psi}_{>1}\psi_{>2}\psi_{>3}\psi_{>4} \rangle_{>0}$;
(2)$\Tilde{\psi}_{<1}\langle \psi_{>2}\psi_{>3}\psi_{>4} \rangle_{>0}=0$;
(3)$~3{\psi}_{<2}\langle \Tilde{\psi}_{>1}\psi_{>3}\psi_{>4} \rangle_{>0}=0$;
(4)$~3\Tilde{\psi}_{<1}{\psi}_{<2}\langle \psi_{>3}\psi_{>4} \rangle_{>0}$;
(5)$~3{\psi}_{<3}{\psi}_{<2}\langle \Tilde{\psi}_{>1}\psi_{>4} \rangle_{>0}$;
(6)$~3\Tilde{\psi}_{<1}{\psi}_{<2}{\psi}_{<3}\langle \psi_{>4} \rangle_{>0}=0$;
(7)${\psi}_{<4}{\psi}_{<2}{\psi}_{<3}\langle \Tilde{\psi}_{>1} \rangle_{>0}=0$;
(8)$\Tilde{\psi}_{<1}{\psi}_{<2}{\psi}_{<3}{\psi_{<4}}$, where $\psi_{>i} \equiv \psi_{>}(\mathbf{k}_i,\omega_i)$ and $\psi_{<i} \equiv \psi_{<}(\mathbf{k}_i,\omega_i)$. 
The (1) term leaves no IR field, so it will only contribute a constant to the new action $\mathcal{A}'$. Terms (2,~3,~5,~6,~7) are vanishing, so they are non-contributing. (8) term $\Tilde{\psi}_<{\psi}_<^3$ is just the bare nonlinear term of new action $\mathcal{A}'$. Only term (4) contributes corrections to $\Tilde{\psi}_<\psi_<$ in new action $\mathcal{A}'$. The question now is how to calculate
\begin{eqnarray*}
    3\times\int_0^{\Lambda}\prod_{i=0}^4 \frac{d^dk_i}{(2\pi)^d}\int_0^{\infty}\frac{d\omega_i}{2\pi}
    ~ \frac{uk_1^2}{6}
        \times 
        \Tilde{\psi}_{<1}{\psi}_{<2}
        \left\langle
        \psi_{>3}\psi_{>4}
        \right\rangle_{>0}
        \times (2\pi)^{d+1}\delta^d\left(\sum_i\mathbf{k}_i\right)\delta\left(\sum_i \omega_i\right)
\end{eqnarray*}
In practice the integral above can generally be written as
\begin{eqnarray*}
        -\int_0^{\Lambda/b}
        \frac{d^dq}{(2\pi)^{d}}\int_{\omega}~ \Tilde{\psi}_<(-\mathbf{q},-\omega){\psi}_<(\mathbf{q},\omega)
        \times  \Sigma 
\end{eqnarray*} 
Then we can obtain the Gaussian part of the effective action of IR modes by coarse-grained
\begin{eqnarray}
    \mathcal{A}'_0[\Tilde{\psi}_<,\psi_<]
    =
    \int_0^{\Lambda/b} \frac{d^d q}{(2\pi)^d}\int_{\omega}
     \left(
         \Tilde{\psi}_<(-\mathbf{q},-\omega)
         \left(G_0^{-1} - \Sigma   \right) 
         {\psi}_<(\mathbf{q},\omega)
         -
         Dq^{2+\theta}\Tilde{\psi}_<(-\mathbf{q},-\omega)\Tilde{\psi}_<(\mathbf{q},\omega)
        \right)
\end{eqnarray} 
The Gaussian part of the original action in momentum space (Eq.~(\ref{actionGaussian})) is
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \mathcal{A}_0[\Tilde{\psi},\psi]
    =
    \int_0^{\Lambda} \frac{d^d q}{(2\pi)^d}\int_{\omega}
     \left(
         \Tilde{\psi}(-\mathbf{q},-\omega)
         G_0^{-1}
         {\psi}(\mathbf{q},\omega)
         -
         Dq^{2+\theta}\Tilde{\psi}(-\mathbf{q},-\omega)\Tilde{\psi}(\mathbf{q},\omega)
        \right)
\end{eqnarray*}
According to the Dyson equation, the inverse of bare propagator $G_0(\mathbf{q},\omega)^{-1}=-i\omega+q^2(r+q^2)$ is corrected  to $\Gamma^{(1,1)}(\mathbf{q},\omega)=G^{-1}(-\mathbf{q},-\omega)=G^{-1}_0(-\mathbf{q},-\omega)-\Sigma(-\mathbf{q},-\omega)$. Here, we call $\Gamma^{(1,1)}$ the $(1+1)$-point vertex function, which can be generated by taking the functional derivation of the vertex generating functional about $\Tilde{\psi}$ and $\psi$ once, respectively~\cite{CriDyn}.  $\Sigma(\mathbf{q},\omega)$ is also called the self-energy, which in general contains all contributions of one-particle irreducible (1PI) graphs, which  cannot be factorized into lower-order graphs by severing a single internal line~\cite{CriDyn}. This result can be visualized in Feynman graphs of correction of propagator as
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \includegraphics[width=180mm]{Feyn/Feyn2.png}
\end{eqnarray*}
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \includegraphics[width=70mm]{Feyn/Feyn5.png}
\end{eqnarray*}
which corresponds to
\begin{eqnarray}
    -i\omega+q^2\left({
        {r}+q^2  }\right)
    \Rightarrow
    -i\omega+q^2\left({
        \Tilde{r}+q^2
    }\right)
    =
    -i\omega+q^2\left[{
        r+q^2+\frac{D u}{2}\int_{\Lambda/(1+\delta l)}^{\Lambda} \frac{d^dk}{(2\pi)^d}
     \frac{k^{\theta}}{r+k^2} + O(u^2)
    }\right]
\end{eqnarray}
From this, we can obtain the change of coupling $r$
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \Tilde{r}
    =
    r+\frac{D u}{2}\int_{\Lambda/(1+\delta l)}^{\Lambda}
    \frac{d^dk}{(2\pi)^d} \frac{k^{\theta}}{r+k^2}+O(u^2)
\end{eqnarray*}
The above equation involves a shell integral. For isotropic function $h(k)$, we can get
\begin{eqnarray}\label{shell_int}
\begin{aligned}
        \int_{\Omega} \frac{d^dk}{(2\pi)^d} h(k)
    &=
    { \frac{S_{d-1}}{(2\pi)^d}\int_0^{\pi} d\theta\ 
    \sin^{d-2}\theta\int_{\Lambda/(1+\delta l)}^{\Lambda}
    dk~k^{d-1} h(k) }
    \\
    &=
    {  K_d \int_{\Lambda/(1+\delta l)}^{\Lambda}dk~k^{d-1}h(k) }
    =
    K_d \Lambda^d h(\Lambda)\delta l + O(\delta l^2)
\end{aligned}
\end{eqnarray}
where $\Omega$ is the shell region $k\in[\Lambda/b,\Lambda]$, and $S_d=2\pi^{d/2}/{\Gamma(d/2)}$ the surface area of a unit hypersphere in $d$ dimensions, where $\Gamma(d/2)$ is the Gamma function and $K_d=S_d/(2\pi)^d$.
Then we can get the corrected coupling
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \Tilde{r}=
    r+\frac{D u}{2}\frac{K_d\Lambda^{d+\theta}}{r+\Lambda^2}\delta l
\end{eqnarray*}
This means 
\begin{eqnarray}
    C_r=
    \frac{D u}{2r}\frac{K_d\Lambda^{d+\theta}}{r+\Lambda^2}
\end{eqnarray}


Similar methods are applied for nonlinear term with coupling $u$ in the effective action, which corresponds to the $(1+3)$-point vertex functions {$\Gamma^{(1,3)}$}. We can use  $\Gamma^{(1,3)}$ to obtain the coarse-grained correction to coupling $u$. The corresponding Feynman graphs are
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \includegraphics[width=150mm]{Feyn/Feyn3.png}
\end{eqnarray*}
From which, we can obtain
\begin{eqnarray*}
        \left({\frac{3}{2}q}\right)^2 {u} 
        ~~~~ \Rightarrow ~~~
        \left({\frac{3}{2}q}\right)^2 \Tilde{u} 
        =
        \left({\frac{3}{2}q}\right)^2 u \left[ 
            1-3Du\int_{\Lambda/(1+\delta l)}^{\Lambda}
            \frac{d^dk}{(2\pi)^d}
            \frac{k^{\theta}}{r+k^2}
            \frac{ (q-k)^2 }{ i\omega + \Delta(k) + \Delta(q-k) }
            \right]
\end{eqnarray*}
where $\Delta(q)=q^2(r+q^2)$ and the integration can be simplified into
\begin{eqnarray*}
	\int_{\Lambda/(1+\delta l)}^{\Lambda}
            \frac{d^dk}{(2\pi)^d}
            \frac{k^{\theta}}{r+k^2}
            \frac{ (q-k)^2 }{ i\omega + \Delta(k) + \Delta(q-k) }
    =
    \frac{1}{2}\int_{\Lambda/(1+\delta l)}^{\Lambda}
            \frac{d^dk}{(2\pi)^d}
            \frac{k^{\theta}}{\left(r+k^2\right)^2}
    \left(
      1+O(q,\omega)
    \right).
\end{eqnarray*}
Here we omit terms dependent on external momentum and external frequency, because they only contribute to the coarse-grained action with irrelevant terms.  More explicitly, the correction can be decomposed into two terms: one  independent on the external momentum (the first term) and one dependent of it (the second term).
The momentum-independent term contributes solely to the $q^2\Tilde{\psi}\psi^3$ term in the coarse-grained action, which is the term of interest for us. On the other hand, the momentum-dependent terms give rise to corrections to higher-order terms in the coarse-grained action, specifically those proportional to $q^{2+i}\omega^{j}\Tilde{\psi}\psi^3$, where $i$ and $j$ are arbitrary positive integers. These higher-order terms are irrelevant by analysis of scaling dimension and will vanish after an RG flow.
Then we can get the corrected coupling
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \Tilde{u}
    =
    u
    - 
    \frac{3}{2}Du^2
    \int_{\Lambda/(1+\delta l)}^{\Lambda}
            \frac{d^dk}{(2\pi)^d}
            \frac{k^{\theta}}{\left(r+k^2\right)^2}
    =
    u 
    - 
    \frac{3D u}{2}\frac{K_d\Lambda^{d+\theta}}{(r+\Lambda^2)^2}\cdot u\delta l
\end{eqnarray*}
This means
\begin{eqnarray}
    C_u
    =
    -\frac{3D u}{2}\frac{K_d\Lambda^{d+\theta}}{(r+\Lambda^2)^2}
\end{eqnarray}
For the final flow equations, we introduce the reduced couplings and fields to make the Wisonian flow equation concise
\begin{eqnarray}
\begin{aligned}
    &\overline{r}=\frac{r}{\Lambda^2}\\
    &\overline{u}=u {D K_d} \Lambda^{-\varepsilon}\\
    &\bar{\Tilde{\psi}}=\Tilde{\psi}{D^{1/2}}\Lambda^{-(d-\theta+2)/2}\\
    &\bar{\psi}=\psi {D^{-1/2}}\Lambda^{-(d+\theta-2)/2}
\end{aligned}
\end{eqnarray}
where $\varepsilon = d_c  -d = 4 - \theta - d$  and $ d_c =4 - \theta$ is upper critical dimension. According to Eq. (\ref{DefineFlowEquations}), Wilsonian flow equation of $\overline{u}$ reads
\begin{eqnarray*}
     \partial_l \overline{u} = \left({ \frac{\partial_l u}{u} + \frac{\partial_l D}{D} }\right)
     \overline{u}
     =
     ( \varepsilon -  C_u  + {C_{D}} )\overline{u}
     =
     \left(
     {
         \varepsilon - \frac{3\overline{u}}{2}
         \frac{1}{(\overline{r}+1)^2}
     }\right)
     \overline{u}
 \end{eqnarray*} 
 Here $C_{D} = 0$ because the first order correction to $D$ is a two-loop integral and thus of order $\delta l^2$~\cite{CriDyn}.  For $\overline{r}$ we can treat it in the same way, and we can get the flow equations of coupling
 \begin{eqnarray}\label{flowEquations}
     \partial_l \overline{r} = 2\overline{r}+\frac{\overline{u}}{2}\frac{1}{\overline{r}+1},\ \ \ 
     \partial_l\overline{u}
     =
     \left(
     {
         \varepsilon - \frac{3\overline{u}}{2}
         \frac{1}{(\overline{r}+1)^2}
     }\right)
     \overline{u}
 \end{eqnarray}

 \begin{figure}[!bhtp]
	\resizebox{80mm}{!}{\includegraphics[trim=0.0in 0.0in 0.0in 0.0in]{figure/RG_flow.png} }
\caption{Streamline plots of the flow equations of HU fluids ($\theta=2$) in 1.9 dimensions, which is the same as Ising model in 3.9 dimension.}\label{RG_flow}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{RG_flow} plot streamline plots of the flow equations for  $\theta=2$ in 1.9 dimensions (\ref{flowEquations}).
When $d> d_c$, i.e., $\varepsilon<0$, the Gaussian fixed point $G$ ($\overline{u}^*_G=\overline{r}^*_G=0$) is IR stable. Besides the Gaussian fixed point, these equations
have another IR stable fixed point $P_{\theta}$ ($\overline{u}^*_{\theta}=2\varepsilon/3$, $\overline{r}^*_{\theta}=-\varepsilon/6$), and it is Wilson-Fisher (WF) fixed point~\cite{wilson1972critical} when $\theta=0$. 
We define ${\delta \bar{r}} =\bar{r}-\bar{r}^*_{\theta}$ and $\delta \bar{u} =\bar{u}-\bar{u}^*_{\theta}$. The linearized flow equations are
\begin{eqnarray}
    \partial_l\begin{pmatrix}
        {\delta \bar{r}} \\
        \delta\bar{u}
    \end{pmatrix}
    =
    \begin{pmatrix}
        2-\dfrac{\varepsilon}{3} &\dfrac{1}{2}\left({ 1+\dfrac{\varepsilon}{6} }\right) \\
        0 &-\varepsilon
    \end{pmatrix}
    \begin{pmatrix}
        {\delta \bar{r}} \\
        \delta\bar{u}
    \end{pmatrix}
\end{eqnarray}
We see that this fixed point has one positive and one negative eigenvalue for $\varepsilon>0$
\begin{eqnarray}
    \Delta_{\delta \bar{u}}=-\varepsilon,\ \ \ 
    \Delta_{{\delta \bar{r}}} = 2-\frac{\varepsilon}{3}
\end{eqnarray}
The flow makes $\bar{u}$ approach $\bar{u}^*_{\theta}$, but makes $\bar{r}$ depart from $\bar{r}^*_{\theta}$ (for $\bar{r}\ne\bar{r}_{\theta}^*$). This means that $\bar{u}^*_{\theta}$ is a critical point. Next, we calculate all critical exponents defined as follows
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \xi\sim\tau^{-\nu},~c\sim\tau^{-\alpha},~\psi\sim\tau^{\beta},~\chi\sim\tau^{-\gamma},~\psi\sim h^{1/\delta},~\text{with}~\tau=\frac{|T-T_c|}{T_c}
\end{eqnarray*}
where $\xi,~c,~\chi$ are correlation length, specific heat and susceptibility respectively. The correlation length  diverges as $\xi\sim |{r- \bar{r}^*_{\theta}}|^{-\nu}$, which leads to $\Delta_{\xi}=-\nu\Delta_{ {\delta \bar{r}}}=-1$ and  $\nu=1/\Delta_{{\delta \bar{r}}}=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{\varepsilon}{12}+O(\varepsilon^2)$.
The first order correction of anomalous dimension $\eta$ and the dynamic critical exponent $z$ are due to two-loop correction, i.e., $\eta= O(\varepsilon^2),~z= 4+O(\varepsilon^2)$. 
Using the results of the scaling dimensional analysis, we can get scaling relations
\begin{eqnarray}\label{scalingRelation}
        \alpha+2\beta+\gamma=2,\ \gamma=\beta(\delta-1),\
    \gamma=\nu(2-\eta),\
    \alpha=2-(d+\theta)\nu
\end{eqnarray}
This indicates that only two critical exponents  are independent. Through Eq.~(\ref{scalingRelation}) we get
\begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
        \nu &= \frac{1}{2}+\frac{\varepsilon}{12}+O(\varepsilon^2),\ ~~~~ 
    \alpha =  \frac{\varepsilon}{6}+O(\varepsilon^2),~~~\\
    \eta &= O(\varepsilon^2),\ ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
\beta  = \frac{1}{2}-\frac{\varepsilon}{6}+O(\varepsilon^2),~~~\\
    \gamma &=1+\frac{\varepsilon}{6}+O(\varepsilon^2),\ ~~~~~~~
    \delta = 3+\varepsilon+O(\varepsilon^2)~~~,\\
    z&= 4+O(\varepsilon^2).
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}






\section*{Appendix E: Details of Field Simulations}
To simulate the field equation in 2D space
\begin{eqnarray}   \label{CMC-modelB-s2}
    \frac{\partial \psi}{\partial t}
    =  \nabla^2  \left( r\psi + \frac{u}{6} \psi^3  - \kappa\nabla^2 \psi  +h  \right)
   +  \xi(\mathbf{x},t)
\end{eqnarray} 
where $h$ is the strength of external field, we perform simulations in 2D square boxes or elongated boxes with periodic boundary condition started from random initial configurations. 
We use the D2Q9 difference method, which takes into account the influence of the next nearest neighboring grid points on the basis of central difference.
The Laplacian operator is discretized as
\begin{eqnarray}
    \nabla^2 \psi(x,y) 
    =
    \sum_{j=-1}^1 \sum_{i=-1}^1~ (\mathbf{K})_{j+2,i+2}\psi(x+i\sigma,y+j\sigma)
\end{eqnarray} 
where $\mathbf{K}$ is the differential kernel of the Laplacian operator
\begin{eqnarray}
    \mathbf{K}
    =
    \frac{1}{6\sigma^2}
    \begin{pmatrix}
        1&4&1\\
        4&-20&4\\
        1&4&1
    \end{pmatrix}
\end{eqnarray} 
To facilitate the numerical calculation, we set $u=36,~,\kappa=36,~D=18$ in  Eq.~(\ref{CMC-modelB-s2}).

\begin{figure*}[!bhtp]
	\resizebox{70mm}{!}{\includegraphics[trim=0.0in 0.0in 0.0in 0.0in]{figure/Func.png} }
\caption{ The diagram is the schematic of dimensionless external field function $\Tilde{h}(x,y)$ for a fixed $y$ (The $y$  does not affect the function value), where we set $L=20 \sigma,~\Tilde{h}_0=10$. }\label{Func}
\end{figure*}

The correlation function is calculated as,
\begin{eqnarray}
	C(r)=\langle \psi(0)\cdot \psi(r) \rangle-\langle \psi(0) \rangle\langle \psi(r) \rangle
\end{eqnarray} 
The structure factor of particle system is calculated as
\begin{eqnarray}
S(\mathbf{q})=\frac{1}{N}\left\langle\left|\sum_{j=0}^{N} e^{i \mathbf{q} \cdot \boldsymbol{r}_{j}}\right|^{2}\right\rangle 
\end{eqnarray}
with $\mathbf{q}=[q_x,q_y]=[\frac{2\pi}{L}{i},\frac{2\pi}{L}{j}]\ ({i},{j}=1,2,\cdots)$ in 2D square boxes and $L$ is the box size. Here, $\langle . \rangle$ represents the time average. The structure factor of field is calculated as
\begin{eqnarray}
S(\mathbf{q})=\frac{\sigma^2}{L^2}\left\langle\left|\sum_{0\leqslant i,j < L}\Tilde{\psi}(\mathbf{r}_{ij}) e^{i \mathbf{q} \cdot \mathbf{r}_{ij} }\right|^{2}\right\rangle 
\end{eqnarray}
where $\mathbf{r}_{ij}=(i,j)$ and $\Tilde{\psi}(\mathbf{r}_{ij})$ is the dimensionless density field. To obtain the data shown in Fig.~\ref{Lattice1}, we perform simulation in elongated boxes with size $2L\times6L$. We calculate the center of mass of the fluid in 2D periodic condition, which help to determine the liquid/gas phase in the system.  The gas-liquid coexistence reduced total density $\psi_{l}$, $\psi_{g}$, compressibility $\chi$, density fluctuation $\chi_{\rho}$  and Binder cumulants $U_4$  are sampled in the corresponding $L\times L$ sub-boxes of liquid and gas phase~\cite{Maggi2021},
\begin{eqnarray}
\chi_c &=& \left.\frac{\partial  \Delta\psi_{lg}  }{\partial h}\right|_{h\to0},  ~~~~~\Delta \psi_{lg} = \psi_l-\psi_g,~~~~~ \psi_i = \frac{1}{L^2}\left\langle \sum_{0\leqslant i,j<L} \sigma^2 \psi_i(\mathbf{r}_{ij}) \right \rangle, \\
\chi_{\rho} &=& \left\langle \left[ {\psi_i}-\left\langle {\psi_i}\right\rangle \right]^{2}  \right\rangle,~~~
     U_4 = \frac
     {\left\langle \left[ {\psi_i}-\left\langle {\psi_i}\right\rangle \right]^{2}  \right\rangle^2}  {\left\langle \left[{\psi_i} -\left\langle {\psi_i}\right\rangle  \right]^{4}  \right\rangle}   
\end{eqnarray} 
where $\psi_i$ with $i=l,g$ are the reduced density in sub-box of either the liquid or gas phase below the critical temperature. Above the critical temperature, $\psi_i$ samples the homogeneous fluid phase in these sub-boxes. To compute susceptibility function $\chi(x)=\left.\partial \langle\psi(x)\rangle / \partial h\right|_{h\to0} $, the external field function $h(x,y)$ is set as a delta function at the origin with finite magnitude.  To compute  the compressibility of the system $\chi_c=\left.\partial \langle\psi\rangle / \partial h\right|_{h\to0}$, the external field function $h(x,y)$  is set to a function spliced by two sigmod functions  
\begin{eqnarray*}
h(x,y) = 
\left\{
\begin{matrix}
\dfrac{h_0}{1 + \exp(-x/\sigma + 1.5L/\sigma)}, &   0 < x < 3L \\
\dfrac{h_0}{1 + \exp(x/\sigma - 4.5L/\sigma)}, &   3L < x < 6L
\end{matrix}
\right.
\end{eqnarray*}
The schematic of $h(x,y)$ is plotted on Fig.~\ref{Func}.
We use the following method to calculate energy fluctuation which usually associated with specific heat,
\begin{eqnarray}
	c_v=\frac{\langle E^{2} \rangle-\langle E \rangle^2}{\langle E \rangle}                               
\end{eqnarray}
where 
\begin{eqnarray}
    e(\mathbf{r}_{ij}) =  \frac{r}{2}\psi(\mathbf{r}_{ij})^2 + \frac{1}{2}[\nabla \psi (\mathbf{r}_{ij}) ]^2 + \frac{u}{4!}\psi(\mathbf{r}_{ij})^4 , ~~E = \sum_{0\leqslant i,j<L} \sigma^2 e(\mathbf{r}_{ij})
\end{eqnarray} 
Generally energy fluctuations obey the finite size scaling $c_v(\tau,L) = f(\tau) + L^{-\lambda}g(\tau L^{1/\nu})$. Expanding the function $g(\tau L^{1/\nu})$ gives $c_v(\tau,L) = f(\tau) + c_0 L^{-\lambda} + \sum^{\infty}_{i=1} c_i \tau^i L^{i/\nu-\lambda}$.  
Since $c_v(T,L)$ does not diverge with $L$ in Fig.3(h), high order terms with $i/\nu - \lambda >0$ could be neglected. By fitting the data to $c_v(\tau, L) = f(\tau) + c_0 L^{-\lambda} + c_1 \tau L^{1/\nu - \lambda}$, we determine that $\lambda=2.11\pm0.02,~c_0=24.0\pm0.5,~c_1=13.0\pm0.2$.
%Since the $c_v(\tau,L)$ does not diverge with $L$,  the coefficients of high order terms $c_i$ with $i/\nu-\lambda>0$ should be all zero. By fiting the data, we find $c_i=0$ for $i > 1$, or $c_v(\tau,L) = f(\tau) + c_0 L^{-\lambda} + c_1 \tau L^{1/\nu-\lambda}$, and determine that $\lambda=2.11\pm0.02,~c_0=24.0\pm0.5,~c_1=13.0\pm0.2$. 



