\begin{abstract}

Dripping dynamics has been well studied over the past century and forms a classic example of chaotic system in physics. With an increase in the inlet flow rate, periodic droplet formation from a faucet becomes chaotic in terms of the droplet size and the length of the liquid column at the time of pinch-off. With a further increase in the flow rate, dripping regime transitions into jetting regime where the liquid column length is much longer than that observed in the dripping case. In general, dripping faucet is seen as a long time behavior of the system at fixed control parameters. In the steady state condition, different nonlinear behaviors such as periodic and chaotic formation of droplets are observed in the dripping and jetting regimes. It is known that dripping faucet shows chaotic dripping regime before jetting regime ensues. 
%Dripping faucet shows interesting route to chaos before transitions to jetting at critical inlet conditions.
At a critical inlet velocity, $U_{m-d_j}$, we note that dripping to jetting transition occurs after several droplets have formed in the dripping regime. The transition behaviour can be characterized by the time evolution of the liquid jet length $L$ and droplet size $D_p$. Solution to slender jet equation show that the dripping-jetting transition region is a function of the fluid properties. Further, we show that perturbations in the inlet velocity can significantly modify the transient behavior of the dripping to jetting regime transition.

 %At the critical velocity, liquid jet shows two types of transitions, one due to change in the velocity, dripping to jetting transition will occur and other is due to the transient or start-up behaviors at the fixed inlet velocity. The start-up behaviors are ignored in the steady state analysis. However, the transition behavior can be characterized by the time evolution of the liquid jet length $L$, pinchoff length $L_p$ and droplet size $D_p$. At the critical velocity $U_{m_{dj}}$, jet evolves from dripping regime followed by a transition regime to reach a steady state jetting regime. This temporal evolution follow with clear mechanisms namely the tip contraction results in capillary wave formation and escape of pinchoff mechanism. These mechanism influence the jet length $L$ and droplet characteristics $D_p$. This nonlinear evolution influences the droplet size distribution and they depend on the fluid properties and nozzle diameter. By solving slender jet equations, it is shown that the dripping-jetting transition region is a function of fluid properties (Kapitza number) at a fixed Bond number. By imposing the velocity perturbations, the initial dripping can be reduced to control the droplet size distribution.
\end{abstract}

